// Mathematics lessons — loaded on demand by ensureLessons('math') in index.html.
// Shape: LESSON_DB.math.m<moduleIndex>[topicIndex] = lesson. Order matches COURSE_DATA.math.
// Every lesson opens with a real-life hook and ends with a 3-question quick check
// (quiz items use the QBANK shape {q,opts,a,et} so module quizzes can reuse them).
LESSON_DB.math = LESSON_DB.math || {};

// ─── Module 0: Algebra & Equations ──────────────────────────────────────────
LESSON_DB.math.m0 = {
  0: {emoji:"√", subtitle:"Grade 10–11 · Why some numbers never end — and how to tame them",
    illusText:"Every measurement you take — a tile, a phone screen, a soccer pitch — lands somewhere on the number line. Some of those numbers just never stop.",
    hook:{title:"The tiler's problem", body:"Sipho is tiling a square bathroom floor in Tembisa that is exactly 2 m². How long is each wall? His calculator says <strong>1.41421356…</strong> and it never ends. He can't buy 1.41421356… m of skirting board. This lesson is about numbers like √2: what they are, how to write them exactly, and when it is fine to round."},
    intro:"Number families sit inside each other: ℕ ⊂ ℤ ⊂ ℚ ⊂ ℝ. <strong>Natural</strong> numbers ℕ count (1, 2, 3…). <strong>Integers</strong> ℤ add 0 and negatives (Sutherland hits −10 °C). <strong>Rational</strong> numbers ℚ can be written as a/b with b ≠ 0 — every decimal that stops or repeats. <strong>Irrational</strong> numbers can't. Together they make the <strong>real</strong> numbers ℝ. A <strong>surd</strong> is an irrational root, like √2 or ∛5.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🔢 Rational or irrational?", body:"0.75 = 3/4 → rational. 0.333… = 1/3 → rational (it repeats). √16 = 4 → rational. √7, π and √2 → irrational. Quick test: if the number under the root is a perfect square (or cube for ∛), the answer is rational.<br><br>Why can't √2 be a fraction? Dividing by b leaves only b possible remainders, so a remainder must come back and the decimal repeats. Every fraction ends or repeats; √2 and π have been proved never to.<br><br>√(−4) is not on the number line at all — no real number squared is negative. It is <strong>non-real</strong>, a word you'll need for nature of roots."},
      {t:"teal", title:"📐 Estimating and rounding", body:"Find the perfect squares on either side. √30: 25 < 30 < 36, so 5 < √30 < 6, closer to 5, so √30 is a bit under 5.5. The calculator gives 5.477…, which rounds to <strong>5.48</strong> (2 d.p.) because the third decimal is 7.<br><br>Why keep surds exact? Early rounding grows. The exact (√2)¹⁰ = 2⁵ = 32, but 1.4¹⁰ ≈ 28.9 — almost 10% out. That's why exams say “leave your answer in surd form”."},
      {t:"gold", title:"✂️ Calculating with surds (Grade 11)", body:"√a × √b = √(ab) because a root is the exponent ½, and (ab)^½ = a^½ × b^½. So pull out the biggest perfect square: √50 = √25 × √2 = 5√2.<br><br>But √a + √b is <strong>not</strong> √(a + b): √9 + √16 = 7, not 5. Add only <strong>like</strong> surds: 3√2 + 5√2 = 8√2, just like 3x + 5x.<br><br>Brackets work like algebra: (√3 + 2)(√3 − 2) = 3 − 4 = <strong>−1</strong>. It's a difference of squares, so the surds cancel — the trick behind rationalising denominators."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Write 0.454545… as a fraction", body:"Let x = 0.4545…<br>Two digits repeat, so multiply by 100: 100x = 45.4545…<br>Subtract, so the endless tails cancel: 99x = 45<br>x = 45/99; divide top and bottom by 9.", answer:"x = <strong>5/11</strong>. Check: 5 ÷ 11 = 0.4545… ✓"},
      {title:"Simplify √50 + √18 − √8 (Grade 11)", body:"√50 = √(25 × 2) = 5√2<br>√18 = √(9 × 2) = 3√2<br>√8 = √(4 × 2) = 2√2<br>Like surds: 5 + 3 − 2 = 6.", answer:"<strong>6√2</strong>."},
      {title:"Solve √(x + 3) = x − 3 (Grade 11)", body:"Square both sides: x + 3 = x² − 6x + 9<br>Standard form: x² − 7x + 6 = 0<br>Factorise: (x − 6)(x − 1) = 0 → x = 6 or x = 1<br>Squaring can create false roots, so check in the original:<br>x = 6: √9 = 3 and 6 − 3 = 3 ✓<br>x = 1: √4 = 2 but 1 − 3 = −2 ✗", answer:"<strong>x = 6</strong>. Reject x = 1 — a square root is never negative."},
    ],
    formula:"√(ab) = √a × √b &nbsp;·&nbsp; √(a/b) = √a / √b &nbsp;·&nbsp; m√a + n√a = (m+n)√a",
    formulaNote:"Only multiply/divide under one root. Never split a root over + or −.",
    mistakes:["√9 + √16 = √25. A root doesn't split over +. Fix: 3 + 4 = 7.","Calling 0.333… irrational. It repeats, so it is 1/3. Irrational means never ends <strong>and</strong> never repeats.","Rounding √2 to 1.4 in line one. The error grows. Fix: stay exact and round once at the end.","√72 = 2√18. 18 still contains 9. Fix: use the biggest square: √(36 × 2) = 6√2.","Not checking a surd equation. Fix: substitute every answer into the original and reject false roots."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"A 1 m square tile has a diagonal of exactly √2 m, so tilers and roofers meet surds daily. Engineers working on structures like the Moses Mabhida arch keep values exact, because early rounding makes a steel beam the wrong length. Quantity surveyors and architects check those numbers — careers open to you with Mathematics."},
    activity:"A 40-inch TV (that's the diagonal) is modelled as a square with side x, so x² + x² = 40².<br>(a) Find x in simplest surd form.<br>(b) Round x to 1 decimal place.<br>(c) Without a calculator, between which two integers does √800 lie?",
    activityAnswer:"(a) 2x² = 1 600 → x² = 800 → x = √(400 × 2) = <strong>20√2</strong> inches.<br>(b) 20 × 1.41421… = 28.28… ≈ <strong>28.3 inches</strong>.<br>(c) 28² = 784 and 29² = 841, so <strong>28 < √800 < 29</strong>, which matches (b).",
    examTip:"“Without a calculator” means show the perfect-square step, e.g. √72 = √(36 × 2); the answer alone loses the method mark. For recurring decimals show both lines and the subtraction. In surd equations the check that rejects the false root earns its own mark.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Rational number",d:"Can be written as a/b with b ≠ 0; decimal ends or repeats"},{w:"Irrational number",d:"Decimal never ends or repeats, e.g. π, √2"},{w:"Surd",d:"An irrational root such as √3 or ∛7"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"Which number is irrational?",opts:["√49","0.121212…","√11","−3/4"],a:2,et:"11 is not a perfect square, so √11 never ends or repeats. 0.1212… repeats, so it is rational."},
      {q:"Simplify √48",opts:["4√3","3√4","16√3","2√12"],a:0,et:"√48 = √(16×3) = 4√3. 2√12 is not fully simplified (√12 = 2√3)."},
      {q:"√60 lies between:",opts:["6 and 7","7 and 8","8 and 9","5 and 6"],a:1,et:"49 < 60 < 64, so 7 < √60 < 8."},
    ],
    conclusion:"You can sort numbers, turn recurring decimals into fractions, estimate and simplify surds and solve surd equations. In the exam: show the perfect-square step, stay exact, reject false roots. Sipho rounds once — at the till."},

  1: {emoji:"⚡", subtitle:"Grade 10–11 · Shortcuts for repeated multiplication",
    illusText:"A rumour shared by 3 people, each sharing with 3 more… after 10 rounds that's 3¹⁰ = 59 049 people. Exponents describe explosive growth.",
    hook:{title:"How fast does a voice note go viral?", body:"Thandi sends a funny voice note to 2 friends. Each of them forwards it to 2 more, and so on. After round 1: 2 people. Round 2: 4. Round 3: 8. After 20 rounds it is 2²⁰ = <strong>1 048 576</strong> people. Writing 2×2×2… twenty times is slow — exponent laws let you work with these numbers in one line."},
    intro:"In aⁿ, <strong>a</strong> is the base and <strong>n</strong> the exponent (how many times a is a factor). The laws only work when the <strong>bases are the same</strong>, so half the skill is writing numbers in prime bases: 81 = 3⁴, 12 = 2² × 3. Grade 10 uses integer exponents; Grade 11 adds rational exponents and k-substitution.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"✖️ Multiply → add, divide → subtract", body:"aᵐ × aⁿ = aᵐ⁺ⁿ, because 2³ × 2⁴ is seven 2s multiplied: 2⁷. aᵐ ÷ aⁿ = aᵐ⁻ⁿ, because common factors cancel: 5⁶ ÷ 5² = 5⁴.<br><br>Different bases? Break them into primes: 12ˣ = (2² × 3)ˣ = 2²ˣ × 3ˣ. But 2³ × 3⁴ can't combine.<br><br>When terms are <strong>added</strong>, take out a common factor: 2ˣ⁺² − 2ˣ = 2ˣ(4 − 1) = 3 × 2ˣ."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🔁 Power of a power, zero and negatives", body:"(aᵐ)ⁿ = aᵐⁿ, so (3²)³ = 3⁶. Also (ab)ⁿ = aⁿbⁿ: (2x)³ = 8x³ — the 2 gets cubed too!<br><br>Why a⁰ = 1? aⁿ ÷ aⁿ = 1, but the law gives aⁿ⁻ⁿ = a⁰. So a⁰ = 1 (a ≠ 0).<br><br>Likewise a² ÷ a⁵ = a⁻³, and cancelling gives 1/a³. So a⁻ⁿ = 1/aⁿ and 2⁻³ = 1/8. A negative exponent means <strong>flip</strong>, not “negative”."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🌱 Rational exponents (Grade 11)", body:"a^(1/n) = ⁿ√a, because (a^(1/n))ⁿ = a. For a^(m/n), root first, then power: 27^(2/3) = (∛27)² = 9.<br><br>The base must be positive: (−4)^(1/2) = √(−4) is non-real, so CAPS takes a > 0.<br><br>To solve x^(3/2) = 27, raise both sides to 2/3: x = 27^(2/3) = 9. These laws drive exponential graphs (next module) and logarithms in Grade 12."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Simplify (3x²y)³ ÷ 9x⁴y", body:"Cube every factor in the bracket: 27x⁶y³<br>Numbers: 27 ÷ 9 = 3<br>x: x⁶⁻⁴ = x²<br>y: y³⁻¹ = y²", answer:"<strong>3x²y²</strong>."},
      {title:"Simplify (2ˣ⁺² − 2ˣ) ÷ (3 · 2ˣ)", body:"The top is a subtraction, so factorise: 2ˣ · 2² − 2ˣ = 2ˣ(4 − 1) = 3 · 2ˣ<br>The fraction is now (3 · 2ˣ) ÷ (3 · 2ˣ): identical factors cancel.", answer:"<strong>1</strong>, for every x."},
      {title:"Solve 3²ˣ − 4 · 3ˣ + 3 = 0 (Grade 11)", body:"3²ˣ = (3ˣ)², so let k = 3ˣ: k² − 4k + 3 = 0<br>(k − 1)(k − 3) = 0 → k = 1 or k = 3<br>3ˣ = 1 = 3⁰ → x = 0; 3ˣ = 3¹ → x = 1<br>Check x = 1: 9 − 12 + 3 = 0 ✓", answer:"<strong>x = 0 or x = 1</strong>."},
    ],
    formula:"aᵐ·aⁿ = aᵐ⁺ⁿ · aᵐ/aⁿ = aᵐ⁻ⁿ · (aᵐ)ⁿ = aᵐⁿ · a⁰ = 1 · a⁻ⁿ = 1/aⁿ · a^(m/n) = (ⁿ√a)ᵐ",
    formulaNote:"Same base required. 2³ × 3⁴ cannot be combined with these laws.",
    mistakes:["2³ × 2⁴ = 4⁷. You're counting 2s, not multiplying bases. Fix: 2⁷.","Thinking 2⁻³ is negative. The minus means reciprocal. Fix: 1/8.","(2x)³ = 2x³. The power applies to every factor. Fix: 8x³.","2ˣ + 2ˣ = 2²ˣ. The laws are for multiplying. Fix: 2 · 2ˣ = 2ˣ⁺¹.","From k = −3, writing 3ˣ = −3 → x = −1. 3ˣ is always positive. Fix: “no solution” for that k."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Computer memory comes in powers of 2 (2³⁰ bytes ≈ 1 GB). Early in each COVID-19 wave, case numbers doubled every few days — the same pattern as Thandi's voice note. Store-card debt grows by compound interest, P(1 + i)ⁿ, and actuaries — among SA's best-paid careers — use these laws to price insurance."},
    activity:"(a) Solve 2ˣ⁺¹ + 2ˣ = 24.<br>(b) Solve x^(3/2) = 27.<br>(c) Simplify (12ˣ · 9) ÷ (3ˣ⁺² · 4ˣ⁻¹).",
    activityAnswer:"(a) 2ˣ(2 + 1) = 24 → 2ˣ = 8 = 2³ → <strong>x = 3</strong>. Check: 16 + 8 = 24 ✓<br>(b) x = 27^(2/3) = 3² = <strong>9</strong>. Check: (√9)³ = 27 ✓<br>(c) Top: 2²ˣ · 3ˣ · 3² = 2²ˣ · 3ˣ⁺². Bottom: 3ˣ⁺² · 2²ˣ⁻². The 3s cancel; 2²ˣ ÷ 2²ˣ⁻² = 2² = <strong>4</strong>.",
    examTip:"First move: same bases (125 = 5³, 64 = 2⁶). Added terms → common factor; 3²ˣ with 3ˣ → let k = 3ˣ. Marks go to the prime-base step and to equating exponents (x + 1 = 5), so write them. Give answers with positive exponents.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Base",d:"The number being multiplied repeatedly"},{w:"Exponent",d:"How many times the base is used as a factor"},{w:"Exponential equation",d:"The unknown is in the exponent, e.g. 2ˣ = 16"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"Simplify 3⁴ × 3²",opts:["9⁶","3⁸","3⁶","9⁸"],a:2,et:"Same base: add exponents → 3⁶. The base stays 3."},
      {q:"5⁻² equals:",opts:["−25","−10","1/25","1/10"],a:2,et:"A negative exponent means reciprocal: 5⁻² = 1/5² = 1/25."},
      {q:"If 2ˣ = 64, x =",opts:["5","6","8","32"],a:1,et:"64 = 2⁶, so x = 6."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Exponent laws turn long multiplications into quick additions. In the exam: use prime bases, factorise sums, use k-substitution for 3²ˣ-type equations, and show the step where you equate exponents."},

  2: {emoji:"🧮", subtitle:"Grade 10 · Expanding, factorising and simplifying",
    illusText:"Algebra is shorthand. Instead of saying “the price of any number of airtime vouchers”, you just say 29x.",
    hook:{title:"Spaza shop maths", body:"Mam' Dlamini sells bread at R18 and milk at R22. If a customer buys x loaves and y litres, the bill is <strong>18x + 22y</strong>. If 3 customers each buy the same, the total is 3(18x + 22y) = 54x + 66y. That's expanding brackets — and you just did it for a real till slip."},
    intro:"An <strong>algebraic expression</strong> combines numbers, variables and operations (3x² − 5x + 2). <strong>Like terms</strong> have the same variables and powers. <strong>Expanding</strong> removes brackets and <strong>factorising</strong> puts them back — opposites. Grade 10 adds a binomial times a trinomial, sums and differences of cubes, and adding algebraic fractions.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"📤 Expanding", body:"Think of a rectangle: the brackets are its sides, the expansion is its area. In the hook, 3 by (18x + 22y) splits into 54x and 66y.<br><br>(x + 3)(x − 5) = x² − 5x + 3x − 15 = x² − 2x − 15. And (a + b)² = a² + 2ab + b² — not a² + b²! The 2ab is the two middle pieces.<br><br>A binomial × trinomial gives 2 × 3 = 6 products before collecting. Count them so none goes missing."},
      {t:"teal", title:"📥 Factorising — the checklist", body:"Factorising runs the area model backwards. Follow this order:<br>1) <strong>Common factor</strong>: 6x² + 9x = 3x(2x + 3).<br>2) <strong>Two terms</strong>: a² − b² = (a + b)(a − b); a³ ± b³ = (a ± b)(a² ∓ ab + b²).<br>3) <strong>Three terms</strong>: trinomial. When a ≠ 1, test brackets: 2x² − x − 3 = (2x − 3)(x + 1).<br>4) <strong>Four terms</strong>: group in pairs.<br><br>Cube signs: <strong>same</strong> as the question, then <strong>opposite</strong>, then <strong>always positive</strong>. That trinomial never factorises further."},
      {t:"gold", title:"➗ Algebraic fractions", body:"Factorise fully, then cancel <strong>factors</strong>, never terms: (x² − 9)/(x + 3) = (x + 3)(x − 3)/(x + 3) = x − 3. Cancelling divides the <em>whole</em> top, so it needs a factor of the whole top.<br><br>A denominator can't be 0, so state restrictions: x ≠ −3.<br><br>To add fractions, use the LCD, as with ½ + ⅓. The LCD of (x − 1) and (x + 1) is x² − 1. You'll need this for equations with fractions next lesson."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Expand (x − 2)(x² + 3x − 1)", body:"x × each term: x³ + 3x² − x<br>−2 × each term: −2x² − 6x + 2<br>6 products ✓. Collect: x³ + (3x² − 2x²) + (−x − 6x) + 2", answer:"<strong>x³ + x² − 7x + 2</strong>."},
      {title:"Factorise 8x³ − 27, then x³ − 2x² − 9x + 18", body:"(a) 8x³ = (2x)³ and 27 = 3³, so a = 2x, b = 3:<br>(2x − 3)((2x)² + (2x)(3) + 3²)<br>(b) Four terms, group: x²(x − 2) − 9(x − 2) (check: −9 × −2 = +18 ✓)<br>= (x − 2)(x² − 9), and x² − 9 is a difference of squares.", answer:"(a) <strong>(2x − 3)(4x² + 6x + 9)</strong>. (b) <strong>(x − 2)(x + 3)(x − 3)</strong>."},
      {title:"Simplify 2/(x − 1) + 3/(x + 1)", body:"LCD = (x − 1)(x + 1)<br>Numerators: 2(x + 1) + 3(x − 1) = 2x + 2 + 3x − 3 = 5x − 1<br>Restrictions: x ≠ 1 and x ≠ −1.", answer:"<strong>(5x − 1)/(x² − 1)</strong>, x ≠ ±1."},
    ],
    formula:"(a+b)² = a²+2ab+b² · a²−b² = (a+b)(a−b) · a³±b³ = (a±b)(a²∓ab+b²)",
    formulaNote:"Always check a factorisation by expanding it back.",
    mistakes:["(x + 4)² = x² + 16. You've lost the middle term. Fix: (x + 4)(x + 4) = x² + 8x + 16.","(x + 6)/6 = x + 1. 6 isn't a factor of the whole top. Fix: only cancel factors.","Skipping the common factor in 2x² − 50. Fix: 2(x² − 25) = 2(x + 5)(x − 5).","x³ + 8 = (x + 2)(x² + 2x + 4). Wrong middle sign. Fix: (x + 2)(x² − 2x + 4).","Grouping as x²(x − 2) − 9(x + 2). Taking out −9 flips +18's sign. Fix: −9(x − 2)."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"A Soweto business owner pricing stock in a spreadsheet uses cost = 850 + 12x. Programmers write the same formulas in code, and every Physical Sciences formula, from v = u + at to Ohm's law, is an expression you rearrange. Engineers factorise first because it is quicker to compute and easier to check."},
    activity:"(a) Factorise fully: 5x³ − 20x.<br>(b) Factorise 3x² + 10x − 8.<br>(c) Simplify (x² − 4)/(x³ − 8).",
    activityAnswer:"(a) 5x(x² − 4) = <strong>5x(x + 2)(x − 2)</strong>.<br>(b) <strong>(3x − 2)(x + 4)</strong>. Check: 3x² + 12x − 2x − 8 ✓<br>(c) (x + 2)(x − 2) over (x − 2)(x² + 2x + 4). Cancel (x − 2): <strong>(x + 2)/(x² + 2x + 4)</strong>, x ≠ 2.",
    examTip:"“Factorise fully” means at least two steps — usually a common factor, then squares or a trinomial. For cubes, marks go to the first bracket and the trinomial. In fractions, show the factorised form before cancelling and state the restriction.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Like terms",d:"Same variables with the same exponents, e.g. 3x² and −7x²"},{w:"Expand",d:"Multiply out to remove brackets"},{w:"Factorise",d:"Write as a product of factors (brackets)"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"Expand (x + 2)(x + 6)",opts:["x² + 12","x² + 8x + 12","x² + 8x + 8","x² + 6x + 12"],a:1,et:"x² + 6x + 2x + 12 = x² + 8x + 12."},
      {q:"Factorise x² − 49",opts:["(x − 7)²","(x + 7)²","(x + 7)(x − 7)","x(x − 49)"],a:2,et:"Difference of squares: x² − 7² = (x + 7)(x − 7)."},
      {q:"Simplify (3x² + 6x)/(3x)",opts:["x + 2","x + 6","3x + 2","x² + 2"],a:0,et:"Factorise the top: 3x(x + 2). Cancel 3x → x + 2."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Expanding and factorising are the grammar of maths. In the exam: follow the checklist (common factor → squares or cubes → trinomial → grouping), cancel only factors, and state restrictions."},

  3: {emoji:"⚖️", subtitle:"Grade 10–11 · Finding the unknown that makes it true",
    illusText:"An equation is a balanced scale. Whatever you do to one side, do to the other — and the unknown has nowhere to hide.",
    hook:{title:"How many trips to break even?", body:"Bongani buys a second-hand Quantum taxi and pays R3 600 a week for the loan, fuel and driver. Each full trip from Bara to town makes him R300 profit. How many trips does he need per week to break even? <strong>300t = 3 600 → t = 12</strong>. Every business plan in South Africa starts with an equation like this."},
    intro:"A <strong>linear equation</strong> has the unknown to the power 1 and usually one solution. A <strong>quadratic</strong> has x² as the highest power and up to two real solutions. <strong>Simultaneous equations</strong> must both hold at once; <strong>literal equations</strong> are formulas you rearrange. Grade 11 adds one linear plus one quadratic. The golden rule: keep it balanced.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"📏 Linear and literal equations", body:"Remove brackets, collect x-terms on one side, divide. With fractions, multiply <strong>every</strong> term by the LCD: x/2 + x/3 = 10 → 3x + 2x = 60 → x = 12.<br><br>A literal equation is solved for one letter by undoing operations in reverse. A JoJo tank holds V = πr²h. Divide by πh: r² = V/(πh). Square-root: r = √(V/(πh)) — positive only, because a radius can't be negative."},
      {t:"teal", title:"✌️ Quadratics by factorising", body:"Make one side 0, factorise, then use the zero-product rule: if A × B = 0, then A = 0 or B = 0. x² − 5x + 6 = 0 → (x − 2)(x − 3) = 0 → x = 2 or 3.<br><br>Why 0? Only zero forces a factor to be zero. (x − 1)(x − 2) = 6 could be 2 × 3 or 1 × 6. Expand instead: x² − 3x − 4 = 0 → x = 4 or −1.<br><br>Never divide by x: in x² = 4x that throws away x = 0."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🤝 Simultaneous equations", body:"Two unknowns need two equations, and the answer must satisfy <strong>both</strong> — graphically, it's where the lines cross (you'll see this in Functions).<br><br><strong>Substitution:</strong> make one letter the subject and substitute. <strong>Elimination:</strong> match coefficients, then add or subtract.<br><br>Grade 11: with one linear and one quadratic, substitute the linear into the quadratic. You get up to two crossing points, each given as a pair (x; y)."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Solve (x + 1)/3 − (x − 2)/4 = 2", body:"Multiply every term by 12: 4(x + 1) − 3(x − 2) = 24<br>Expand (−3 × −2 = +6): 4x + 4 − 3x + 6 = 24<br>x + 10 = 24<br>x = 14<br>Check: 15/3 − 12/4 = 5 − 3 = 2 ✓", answer:"<strong>x = 14</strong>."},
      {title:"Solve x + 2y = 1 and x/3 + y/2 = 1", body:"Second equation × 6: 2x + 3y = 6<br>From the first: x = 1 − 2y<br>Substitute: 2(1 − 2y) + 3y = 6 → 2 − y = 6 → y = −4<br>x = 1 − 2(−4) = 9<br>Check: 9/3 + (−4)/2 = 3 − 2 = 1 ✓", answer:"<strong>x = 9 and y = −4</strong>."},
      {title:"Solve y = x + 1 and y = x² − 1 (Grade 11)", body:"Substitute: x + 1 = x² − 1<br>x² − x − 2 = 0 → (x − 2)(x + 1) = 0 → x = 2 or x = −1<br>y = x + 1 gives y = 3 and y = 0<br>Check in y = x² − 1: 4 − 1 = 3 ✓, 1 − 1 = 0 ✓", answer:"<strong>(2; 3) and (−1; 0)</strong>."},
    ],
    formula:"If A × B = 0 then A = 0 or B = 0",
    formulaNote:"This only works when one side is zero. (x − 1)(x − 2) = 6 does NOT mean x − 1 = 6.",
    mistakes:["Dividing x² = 4x by x loses x = 0. Fix: x(x − 4) = 0 → x = 0 or 4.","Factorising before getting 0 on one side. The rule needs “= 0”. Fix: move every term across first.","Multiplying only the fractions by the LCD and leaving the 2. Fix: every term × 12, so the right side is 24.","Finding x and stopping. Fix: back-substitute and give the pair (x; y).","Keeping a negative length. Fix: reject answers that make no sense, and say why."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Break-even points, pricing vetkoek, a nurse at Chris Hani Baragwanath Hospital working a dose from a formula. Simultaneous equations compare contracts: R99 + R1/min and R149 + R0.50/min cost the same at 100 minutes, so above that the second plan wins. Transnet's logistics planners solve hundreds together."},
    activity:"(a) A trip costs R150 per learner plus R1 200 for the bus. The class has R6 000. How many learners can go?<br>(b) A garden is 3 m longer than it is wide, area 40 m². Find its width and length.<br>(c) Make h the subject of V = πr²h.",
    activityAnswer:"(a) 150n + 1 200 = 6 000 → 150n = 4 800 → <strong>32 learners</strong>.<br>(b) x(x + 3) = 40 → x² + 3x − 40 = 0 → (x + 8)(x − 5) = 0. Reject −8: <strong>width 5 m, length 8 m</strong>.<br>(c) <strong>h = V/(πr²)</strong>.",
    examTip:"Show the factorised form and both solutions, then reject any that don't fit, with a reason. Number simultaneous equations (1) and (2) and say what you substitute — marks go to the substitution, the solved quadratic and each final pair. In word problems, define the variable and end with units.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Linear equation",d:"Highest power of the variable is 1"},{w:"Quadratic equation",d:"Highest power is 2; standard form ax² + bx + c = 0"},{w:"Root / solution",d:"A value that makes the equation true"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"Solve 4x − 7 = 13",opts:["x = 1.5","x = 5","x = 6","x = 20"],a:1,et:"4x = 20 → x = 5."},
      {q:"Solve (x + 4)(x − 1) = 0",opts:["x = 4 or 1","x = −4 or 1","x = −4 or −1","x = 4 or −1"],a:1,et:"x + 4 = 0 → x = −4; x − 1 = 0 → x = 1."},
      {q:"A number squared equals 3 times the number. Which are possible?",opts:["0 and 3","3 only","−3 and 3","0 only"],a:0,et:"x² = 3x → x(x − 3) = 0 → x = 0 or x = 3."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Equations answer “how many?” and “how much?”. In the exam: get 0 on one side before factorising, multiply every term by the LCD, give both values in simultaneous equations, and reject answers that make no sense."},

  4: {emoji:"↔️", subtitle:"Grade 10–11 · When the answer is a range, not a number",
    illusText:"“At least 18 to enter”, “no more than 60 km/h in town” — real rules are inequalities, not equations.",
    hook:{title:"How much data can you afford?", body:"Lerato has R150 for the month. She needs R40 for a bundle of minutes and wants to buy data at R12 per GB. How many GB can she buy? 12g + 40 ≤ 150 → 12g ≤ 110 → <strong>g ≤ 110/12 ≈ 9.17</strong>. So she can buy at most <strong>9 GB</strong>. The answer is a range — any amount up to 9 GB works."},
    intro:"An <strong>inequality</strong> compares two expressions with <, >, ≤ or ≥. Solve it like an equation, with <strong>one big rule</strong>: multiply or divide by a <strong>negative</strong> and you <strong>flip the sign</strong>. Grade 10 covers linear and double inequalities; Grade 11 adds quadratic inequalities, read off a parabola.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🔄 The flip rule — and why", body:"5 > 2 is true, but −5 > −2 is false, so it becomes −5 < −2. Multiplying by −1 reflects every number across 0, so the order reverses. That's why −3x < 12 gives x > −4.<br><br>Adding or subtracting only slides the number line — no flip.<br><br>Never multiply or divide by a <strong>variable</strong>: you don't know if it's negative."},
      {t:"teal", title:"📍 Showing the answer, double inequalities", body:"Number line: open dot ○ for < or >, closed dot ● for ≤ or ≥. Interval notation: x > −4 is (−4; ∞); 1 ≤ x < 5 is [1; 5). Round bracket = excluded; ∞ always gets one.<br><br>For −3 < 2x + 1 ≤ 7, do the same to all three parts: −4 < 2x ≤ 6, so −2 < x ≤ 3. Dividing by a negative flips both signs; then rewrite with the smaller number on the left."},
      {t:"gold", title:"⛰️ Quadratic inequalities (Grade 11)", body:"For x² − x − 6 ≤ 0, find the <strong>critical values</strong>: (x − 3)(x + 2) = 0 → −2 and 3. Sketch the smiling parabola. “≤ 0” means on or <strong>below</strong> the x-axis — between the roots: −2 ≤ x ≤ 3. For “> 0” you want the two outer pieces, above the axis.<br><br>x² > 4x: don't divide by x! x(x − 4) > 0, critical values 0 and 4, so <strong>x < 0 or x > 4</strong>. Test x = −1: 1 > −4 ✓ — dividing by x would have lost it."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Solve 3 − 2x < 9", body:"Subtract 3 (no flip): −2x < 6<br>Divide by −2 and flip: x > −3<br>Test x = 0: 3 < 9 ✓", answer:"<strong>x > −3</strong>, i.e. (−3; ∞)."},
      {title:"Solve −1 ≤ 2 − 3x ≤ 8", body:"Subtract 2 from all parts: −3 ≤ −3x ≤ 6<br>Divide by −3, flip both signs: 1 ≥ x ≥ −2<br>Rewrite smaller first.<br>Test x = 0: −1 ≤ 2 ≤ 8 ✓", answer:"<strong>−2 ≤ x ≤ 1</strong>, i.e. [−2; 1]."},
      {title:"The taxi owner's profit", body:"Daily profit (in R10s) is P = −2x² + 40x − 150 for x trips. When is P > 0?<br>Divide by −2 and flip: x² − 20x + 75 < 0<br>(x − 5)(x − 15) < 0 → critical values 5 and 15<br>“< 0” means below the axis: between them.<br>Test x = 10: −200 + 400 − 150 = 50 > 0 ✓", answer:"<strong>5 < x < 15</strong>: a profit with 6 to 14 trips a day."},
    ],
    formula:"Multiply/divide by a negative ⇒ flip the inequality sign",
    formulaNote:"Adding or subtracting (even negatives) never flips the sign.",
    mistakes:["−2x ≥ 6 → x ≥ −3. Test x = 0: 0 ≥ 6 is false. Fix: flip → x ≤ −3.","A closed dot for < or >. Closed means included. Fix: ○ for <, >; ● for ≤, ≥.","Dividing x² > 4x by x. x might be negative. Fix: x(x − 4) > 0.","Writing “x < 0 and x > 4”. No number does both. Fix: use “or” for outer pieces.","Answering 9.17 GB. Fix: round <strong>down</strong> for “at most”, up for “at least”."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Budgets, speed limits on the N3, medicine doses (“between 5 and 10 ml”) and bakkie load limits are inequalities. NSFAS too: household income of at most R350 000 a year. Analysts at Shoprite and Takealot solve systems of inequalities to route trucks within budget — that's linear programming."},
    activity:"(a) A lift's limit is 630 kg. The operator weighs 70 kg; each box 25 kg. How many boxes?<br>(b) Write −2 < x ≤ 3 in interval notation and list its integers.<br>(c) Solve x² + 2x − 8 ≥ 0.",
    activityAnswer:"(a) 70 + 25b ≤ 630 → 25b ≤ 560 → b ≤ 22.4 → <strong>at most 22 boxes</strong>.<br>(b) <strong>(−2; 3]</strong>: <strong>−1, 0, 1, 2, 3</strong>.<br>(c) (x + 4)(x − 2) ≥ 0, critical values −4 and 2, outer pieces: <strong>x ≤ −4 or x ≥ 2</strong>. Test x = 0: −8 ≥ 0 is false, so 0 is excluded ✓",
    examTip:"Give the answer in the form asked: number line, interval or list of integers — the wrong form loses marks. For quadratic inequalities, marks go to the factors, the critical values and the final interval; a quick sketch counts as working, so draw it.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Inequality",d:"Statement using <, >, ≤ or ≥"},{w:"Interval notation",d:"Range written with brackets, e.g. (−2; 5]"},{w:"Closed dot",d:"Endpoint is included (≤ or ≥)"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"Solve −4x > 20",opts:["x > −5","x < −5","x > 5","x < 5"],a:1,et:"Divide by −4 and flip: x < −5."},
      {q:"Which interval means 2 < x ≤ 7?",opts:["[2; 7]","(2; 7)","(2; 7]","[2; 7)"],a:2,et:"2 is excluded (round bracket), 7 is included (square bracket)."},
      {q:"How many integers satisfy 0 ≤ x < 4?",opts:["3","4","5","Infinitely many"],a:1,et:"0, 1, 2 and 3 → four integers."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Inequalities describe every option that works. In the exam: flip when multiplying or dividing by a negative, never divide by x, read quadratic inequalities off a sketch, and answer in the form asked."},

  5: {emoji:"🎯", subtitle:"Grade 11 · The formula that solves any quadratic",
    illusText:"Some quadratics refuse to factorise. The quadratic formula never fails — and its discriminant tells you what kind of answer to expect before you start.",
    hook:{title:"When will the ball land?", body:"A goalkeeper at Orlando Stadium kicks a ball. Its height (m) after t seconds is h = −5t² + 20t + 1. When does it hit the ground? Set h = 0: −5t² + 20t + 1 = 0. This doesn't factorise nicely — but the quadratic formula gives <strong>t ≈ 4.05 s</strong>."},
    intro:"For any ax² + bx + c = 0 (a ≠ 0), x = [−b ± √(b² − 4ac)] / 2a. It comes from <strong>completing the square</strong>, which is also a method on its own. <strong>Δ = b² − 4ac</strong>, the <strong>discriminant</strong>, gives the nature of the roots without solving: real or non-real, rational or irrational, equal or unequal.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🧩 Completing the square → the formula", body:"x² + 6x is almost (x + 3)² = x² + 6x + 9. So add <strong>(half of b)²</strong> to both sides, get (x + p)² = q, and square-root.<br><br>On ax² + bx + c = 0: divide by a → x² + (b/a)x = −c/a. Add (b/(2a))²: (x + b/(2a))² = (b² − 4ac)/(4a²). Square-root: x + b/(2a) = ±√(b² − 4ac)/(2a). Subtract b/(2a) — that's the formula. The ± is why there can be two roots."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🔢 Using the formula", body:"Standard form, identify a, b, c <strong>with signs</strong>, substitute in brackets, round only at the end.<br><br>2x² − 3x − 4 = 0: a = 2, b = −3, c = −4. x = [3 ± √(9 + 32)]/4 = [3 ± √41]/4, so x ≈ 2.35 or −0.85.<br><br>Traps: −(−3) = +3, (−3)² = +9, −4(2)(−4) = +32."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🔍 Nature of roots (Δ)", body:"Δ decides how the parabola meets the x-axis:<br>Δ < 0 → <strong>non-real</strong>, misses: x² − 4x + 7 = 0 has Δ = −12.<br>Δ = 0 → <strong>real, equal</strong>, touches: x² + 6x + 9 = 0 has Δ = 0, root −3.<br>Δ > 0, perfect square → <strong>real, unequal, rational</strong> (it factorises).<br>Δ > 0, not a square → <strong>real, unequal, irrational</strong>.<br>The rational rule needs rational a, b, c.<br><br>With k: x² + 6x + k = 0 has equal roots when 36 − 4k = 0 → k = 9."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Solve x² + 4x − 2 = 0 by completing the square", body:"x² + 4x = 2<br>Half of 4 is 2; add 2² = 4: x² + 4x + 4 = 6<br>(x + 2)² = 6<br>x + 2 = ±√6 → x = −2 ± √6 (√6 ≈ 2.449)", answer:"<strong>x = −2 ± √6 ≈ 0.45 or −4.45</strong>."},
      {title:"Show that x² − (k + 2)x + 2k = 0 always has real roots", body:"a = 1, b = −(k + 2), c = 2k<br>Δ = (k + 2)² − 8k = k² + 4k + 4 − 8k = k² − 4k + 4 = (k − 2)²<br>A square is never negative, so Δ ≥ 0 for every real k.", answer:"Roots are <strong>always real</strong> — and rational for rational k, since Δ is a perfect square. Indeed (x − 2)(x − k) = 0."},
      {title:"Two machines", body:"Two machines at a Durban bottling plant finish a job together in 2 h 24 min; one takes 2 h longer alone. Let the faster take x h.<br>2.4 h = 12/5 h, so 1/x + 1/(x + 2) = 5/12<br>× 12x(x + 2): 12(x + 2) + 12x = 5x(x + 2)<br>5x² − 14x − 24 = 0<br>Δ = 196 + 480 = 676 = 26² → x = (14 ± 26)/10 = 4 or −1.2<br>Reject −1.2. Check: 1/4 + 1/6 = 5/12 ✓", answer:"<strong>The slower machine takes 6 h</strong> (the faster takes 4 h)."},
    ],
    formula:"x = [−b ± √(b² − 4ac)] / 2a &nbsp;·&nbsp; Δ = b² − 4ac",
    formulaNote:"The whole numerator is divided by 2a — not just the root.",
    mistakes:["b = 3 when the term is −3x. The sign belongs to b. Fix: b = −3.","Typing −3² gives −9. Fix: type (−3)² = 9.","x = 3 ± √41/4. The whole numerator is over 2a. Fix: (3 ± √41)/4.","Using the formula on x² + 4x = 2 with c = 0. Fix: x² + 4x − 2 = 0, c = −2.","Saying Δ = 0 gives “one root”. Fix: “real, equal roots” — full wording earns the mark."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Sports scientists model a kick like the goalkeeper's. The MeerKAT and SKA dishes in the Karoo use parabolic curves to focus radio waves, and engineers solve quadratics to design them. Businesses find break-even prices with quadratics — and Δ first tells them whether a break-even exists."},
    activity:"(a) Solve −5t² + 20t + 1 = 0 from the hook.<br>(b) Give the nature of the roots of 3x² − 6x + 3 = 0, then solve it.<br>(c) For which k does x² + 2x + k = 0 have non-real roots?",
    activityAnswer:"(a) t = [−20 ± √420]/(−10), √420 ≈ 20.494 → t ≈ 4.05 or −0.05. Reject negative time: <strong>about 4.05 s</strong>.<br>(b) Δ = 36 − 36 = 0 → <strong>real, equal</strong>. 3(x − 1)² = 0 → <strong>x = 1</strong>.<br>(c) Δ = 4 − 4k < 0 → <strong>k > 1</strong>.",
    examTip:"Write the formula, then substitute with brackets — that earns a mark even if arithmetic slips. For nature of roots show Δ, its sign, and full wording. “Show the roots are real for all k” ends with Δ written as a square, ≥ 0.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Discriminant (Δ)",d:"b² − 4ac — decides the nature of the roots"},{w:"Real roots",d:"Solutions that are real numbers (graph cuts or touches the x-axis)"},{w:"Non-real roots",d:"Δ < 0; no x-intercepts"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"For x² + 2x + 5 = 0, Δ equals:",opts:["24","−16","16","−24"],a:1,et:"Δ = 2² − 4(1)(5) = 4 − 20 = −16."},
      {q:"If Δ = 0, the roots are:",opts:["Non-real","Real and unequal","Real and equal","Irrational"],a:2,et:"Δ = 0 gives two real, equal roots — the graph just touches the x-axis."},
      {q:"For which k does x² − 8x + k = 0 have equal roots?",opts:["k = 8","k = 16","k = −16","k = 64"],a:1,et:"Δ = 64 − 4k = 0 → k = 16."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Completing the square and the formula solve any quadratic; Δ previews the roots. In the exam: substitute with brackets, round at the end and use the full nature-of-roots wording."},
};

// ─── Module 1: Functions & Graphs ───────────────────────────────────────────
LESSON_DB.math.m1 = {
  0: {emoji:"🔁", subtitle:"Grade 10 · Input in, exactly one output out",
    illusText:"A vending machine is a function: press B4, you get one specific drink — every time.",
    hook:{title:"The taxi fare machine", body:"Imagine a Durban taxi association charged R15 to get in plus R2 per km. Tell the driver the distance and there would be exactly <strong>one</strong> fare: f(d) = 2d + 15. A 10 km trip is f(10) = R35. If the same trip could cost R35 today and R80 tomorrow under the same rule, nobody would trust it. One input, one output — that is a <strong>function</strong>."},
    intro:"A <strong>function</strong> is a rule that assigns <strong>each input exactly one output</strong>. We write y = f(x): x is the input (independent variable) and f(x) the output (dependent variable). The allowed inputs form the <strong>domain</strong>; the outputs produced form the <strong>range</strong>. Every graph in this module is built on these ideas.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"✅ Function or not?", body:"Draw arrows from inputs to outputs. Learner → ID number: one arrow each, so a function. Mother → her children: one mother can point to three children, so <strong>not</strong> a function. Always ask: does any <em>input</em> have two outputs?<br><br>(Grade 12 preview) <strong>One-to-one</strong>: each output has one input (learner → ID). <strong>Many-to-one</strong>: inputs share an output (learner → province; Thandi and Sipho both map to KZN) — still a function. On a graph, use the <strong>vertical line test</strong>: if an upright line cuts the graph twice, it fails. The circle x² + y² = 25 fails at x = 3, hitting (3; 4) and (3; −4)."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🧾 Notation and four representations", body:"f(x) = 3x − 1 reads “f of x”, not “f times x”. f(4) = 3(4) − 1 = 11. f(a + 1) = 3(a + 1) − 1 = 3a + 2. f(x) = 8 is the reverse job: 3x − 1 = 8 → x = 3.<br><br>CAPS wants you to move between <strong>words</strong>, a <strong>table</strong>, a <strong>formula</strong> and a <strong>graph</strong>. Distance is <strong>continuous</strong> (7.3 km is possible), so draw a solid line; number of passengers is <strong>discrete</strong>, so plot separate dots."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🗺️ Domain and range", body:"Taxi: distance can't be negative, so domain d ≥ 0; the cheapest fare is R15, so range f ≥ 15. Write sets in <strong>set-builder</strong> form {x : x ≥ 2, x ∈ ℝ} or <strong>interval</strong> form [2; ∞). Square bracket = endpoint included; ∞ always gets a round one.<br><br>For y = x² the domain is ℝ but the range is y ≥ 0, since a square is never negative. Domain illustration (not a CAPS graph to sketch): y = √(x − 2) needs x − 2 ≥ 0, so x ≥ 2."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"f(x) = x² − 2x. Find f(−3) and f(a + 1).", body:"f(−3) = (−3)² − 2(−3) — brackets around −3.<br>= 9 + 6 = 15.<br>f(a + 1) = (a + 1)² − 2(a + 1) — replace x with the whole bracket.<br>= a² + 2a + 1 − 2a − 2 — expand.<br>= a² − 1 — the 2a terms cancel.", answer:"<strong>f(−3) = 15</strong>, <strong>f(a + 1) = a² − 1</strong>."},
      {title:"g(x) = 5 − 2x. For which x is g(x) = −7?", body:"This asks for an input, so solve: 5 − 2x = −7.<br>−2x = −12 → x = 6.<br>Check: g(6) = 5 − 12 = −7 ✓.", answer:"<strong>x = 6</strong>."},
      {title:"Taxi fare: table → formula → graph", body:"Suppose a Pinetown rank showed: 0 km → R15, 5 km → R25, 10 km → R35.<br>Fare rises R10 per 5 km = R2 per km (equal steps, so linear).<br>At 0 km it's R15, so f(d) = 2d + 15.<br>Graph: solid line from a closed dot at (0; 15) — distance is continuous.<br>How far for R61? 2d + 15 = 61 → d = 23.", answer:"<strong>f(d) = 2d + 15</strong>, domain <strong>d ≥ 0</strong>, range <strong>f ≥ 15</strong>; R61 buys <strong>23 km</strong>."},
    ],
    mistakes:["Writing (−3)² as −9: the calculator squares 3 first. Fix: always type the brackets.","Mixing up f(5) and f(x) = 5. The first substitutes, the second solves. Fix: see where the number sits.","Swapping domain (x) and range (y). Fix: D before R, like x before y.","Joining dots on a discrete graph. Fix: ask “can the input be 2.5?” If not, leave dots."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"The SARS income-tax table is a function: each taxable income gives exactly one tax amount. Prepaid electricity, petrol pumps and currency converters on your banking app are too. In CAT and Web Programming you'll code functions that return one output — the same idea data analysts and developers use daily."},
    activity:"A plumber in Soweto charges R350 call-out plus R420 per hour.<br>(a) Write the cost as a function C(h).<br>(b) Find C(2.5).<br>(c) A bill is R2 030. How many hours?<br>(d) State a sensible domain and range.",
    activityAnswer:"(a) <strong>C(h) = 420h + 350</strong>.<br>(b) 420 × 2.5 + 350 = 1 050 + 350 = <strong>R1 400</strong>.<br>(c) 420h + 350 = 2 030 → 420h = 1 680 → <strong>h = 4</strong>.<br>(d) Domain h ≥ 0; range C ≥ 350 (the call-out fee).",
    examTip:"f(value) means substitute; f(x) = value means solve. Write the substitution line with brackets — it earns a method mark even if the arithmetic slips. Give domain and range as inequalities with the right variable: “f ≥ 15”, not just “15”.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Function",d:"Each input has exactly one output"},{w:"Domain",d:"All allowed input (x) values"},{w:"Range",d:"All output (y) values produced"},{w:"Vertical line test",d:"If a vertical line cuts a graph more than once, it is not a function"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"If f(x) = 4x + 3, then f(2) =",opts:["9","11","14","20"],a:1,et:"4(2) + 3 = 11. 20 comes from working out 4(2 + 3), but multiplication happens before the + 3."},
      {q:"Which is NOT a function?",opts:["Person → birth date","Car → number plate","Learner → favourite subjects","Item → barcode"],a:2,et:"A learner can have several favourite subjects: one input, many outputs. Many people share a birthday, but that's many-to-one, which is still a function."},
      {q:"Range of y = x² + 1:",opts:["y ≥ 0","y ≥ 1","All real y","y ≤ 1"],a:1,et:"x² ≥ 0, so x² + 1 ≥ 1. y ≥ 0 is the range of x² before the +1 lifts it."},
    ],
    conclusion:"A function gives each input exactly one output. Substitute for f(value), solve for f(x) = value, and state domain and range as inequalities. In exams, show the bracketed substitution line."},

  1: {emoji:"📈", subtitle:"Grade 10 · Straight-line relationships",
    illusText:"Fixed fee + rate per unit = straight line. Your cellphone contract, electricity bill and taxi fare all live on y = mx + c.",
    hook:{title:"Which contract is cheaper?", body:"Contract A: R99 a month plus R1 per minute. Contract B: R0 a month plus R2.50 per minute. A: y = x + 99. B: y = 2.5x. Equal when x + 99 = 2.5x → 1.5x = 99 → x = 66 minutes. Under 66 min, B is cheaper; over, A wins. Two straight lines crossing."},
    intro:"A <strong>linear function</strong> is y = mx + c; CAPS also writes <strong>y = ax + q</strong>, the same family as the parabola and hyperbola to come. <strong>m</strong> is the gradient — the change in y when x goes up by 1. <strong>c</strong> is the y-intercept — the fixed part when x = 0.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"📐 Why a steady rate is straight", body:"Prepaid electricity at about R3.50 per kWh: 0 → R0, 10 → R35, 20 → R70. Equal steps in x give equal steps in y, so the points line up. That rate is the gradient: m = (y₂ − y₁)/(x₂ − x₁) = R3.50 per kWh.<br><br>m > 0 rises, m < 0 falls, bigger |m| is steeper; changing the sign of a reflects the line in the y-axis. q slides it up or down. y = 3 is horizontal (m = 0); x = 3 is vertical (undefined gradient) and not even a function."},
      {t:"teal", title:"✏️ Sketching and finding equations", body:"<strong>Dual-intercept method</strong>: x = 0 gives (0; c); y = 0 gives the x-intercept. Plot both and join with a ruler. y = 3x − 6 → (0; −6) and (2; 0).<br><br>Backwards from a sketch: read c off the y-axis, then use another point for m. Through (0; 4) and (−2; 0): m = 4 ÷ 2 = 2, so y = 2x + 4. Trap: in y = 4 − 3x, m = −3, not 4."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🔗 Intersections", body:"Graphs meet where they share x and y, so set the equations equal: 2x − 1 = −x + 8 → x = 3, then y = 5. Point (3; 5). Always substitute back for y.<br><br>Parallel lines have equal gradients; perpendicular lines have m₁ × m₂ = −1. Both return in the lessons Distance, Midpoint & Gradient and Inclination & Equation of a Line."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Sketch y = −2x + 4", body:"x = 0: y = 4 → (0; 4).<br>y = 0: 2x = 4 → x = 2 → (2; 0).<br>m = −2, so it falls left to right ✓.<br>Join with a ruler, extend, label.", answer:"A falling line through <strong>(0; 4)</strong> and <strong>(2; 0)</strong>."},
      {title:"Equation through (1; 5) and (3; 11)", body:"m = (11 − 5)/(3 − 1) = 6 ÷ 2 = 3.<br>Substitute (1; 5): 5 = 3(1) + c → c = 2.<br>Check (3; 11): 3(3) + 2 = 11 ✓.", answer:"<strong>y = 3x + 2</strong>."},
      {title:"App ride vs metered taxi", body:"App: R10 + R7 per km. Meter: R25 + R5 per km.<br>Set equal: 7d + 10 = 5d + 25.<br>2d = 15 → d = 7.5 km.<br>Cost: 7(7.5) + 10 = R62.50; check 5(7.5) + 25 = R62.50 ✓.<br>The app starts lower but climbs faster.", answer:"Same at <strong>7.5 km (R62.50)</strong>; the app is cheaper for shorter trips."},
    ],
    formula:"y = mx + c (CAPS: y = ax + q) &nbsp;·&nbsp; m = (y₂ − y₁)/(x₂ − x₁)",
    formulaNote:"Keep the order consistent: if you start with y₂, start with x₂.",
    mistakes:["(y₂ − y₁)/(x₁ − x₂) flips the sign. Fix: keep the same point on top and bottom.","Reading c from y = 4 − 3x as −3. Fix: rewrite as y = −3x + 4.","Stopping at x for an intersection. Fix: substitute back to get y.","Calling x = 3 a gradient-0 line. Fix: x = k is vertical; y = k is horizontal."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Many municipalities charge electricity in blocks: each block is a straight line and the gradient jumps as you use more. Break-even in Business Studies, car-hire quotes and speed on a distance–time graph are all gradients. Quantity surveyors and logistics planners compare cost lines daily."},
    activity:"Prepaid electricity costs about R3.50 per kWh. A neighbour pays R2.50 per kWh plus R200 a month.<br>(a) Write the prepaid cost C for k kWh.<br>(b) Cost of 150 kWh?<br>(c) How many kWh does R350 buy?<br>(d) When do both cost the same?",
    activityAnswer:"(a) <strong>C = 3.5k</strong>.<br>(b) 3.5 × 150 = <strong>R525</strong>.<br>(c) 350 ÷ 3.5 = <strong>100 kWh</strong>.<br>(d) 3.5k = 2.5k + 200 → <strong>k = 200 kWh</strong> (R700 each). Above that, the neighbour's plan is cheaper.",
    examTip:"Label every intercept and intersection with coordinates, and the line with its equation — unlabelled points earn nothing. Show the gradient formula with numbers substituted for the method mark. End word problems with a sentence: “the app is cheaper under 7.5 km”.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Gradient (m)",d:"Rate of change: rise ÷ run"},{w:"y-intercept (c)",d:"Where the line cuts the y-axis (x = 0)"},{w:"Point of intersection",d:"The (x; y) where two graphs meet"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"Gradient of y = 7 − 2x:",opts:["7","2","−2","−7"],a:2,et:"Rewrite as y = −2x + 7 → m = −2. 7 is the y-intercept, not the gradient."},
      {q:"x-intercept of y = 3x − 12:",opts:["(0; −12)","(4; 0)","(−4; 0)","(12; 0)"],a:1,et:"0 = 3x − 12 → x = 4. (0; −12) is the y-intercept — an x-intercept always has y = 0."},
      {q:"A line perpendicular to y = 4x + 1 has gradient:",opts:["4","−4","1/4","−1/4"],a:3,et:"m₁ × m₂ = −1 → m₂ = −1/4. Flip AND change sign; 4 would give a parallel line."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Lines model a fixed start plus a steady rate. Sketch from both intercepts, find m before c, and set equations equal to intersect. In exams, label every point with coordinates."},

  2: {emoji:"🏀", subtitle:"Grade 10 · The shape of a throw",
    illusText:"Throw a ball, spray a hosepipe, look at the Moses Mabhida arch — you're looking at a parabola.",
    hook:{title:"The Moses Mabhida arch", body:"The arch over Durban's stadium is roughly a parabola, about 106 m high. Put the origin under the top and a simple model is y = −0.0041x² + 106 (metres). Ground level: x² = 106 ÷ 0.0041 ≈ 25 854, so x ≈ ±161 m — a model span of about 320 m. Engineers estimate heights and spans this way before they build."},
    intro:"The Grade 10 parabola is y = ax² + q. <strong>a</strong> sets the shape: a > 0 opens up (minimum), a < 0 opens down (maximum); bigger |a|, narrower. <strong>q</strong> shifts it up or down: turning point (0; q), axis of symmetry x = 0.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"😊 Why it turns", body:"Table for y = x²: x = −2, −1, 0, 1, 2 gives 4, 1, 0, 1, 4. The values mirror each other because (−2)² = 2², so the <strong>axis of symmetry</strong> is x = 0.<br><br>A square is never negative, so x² ≥ 0 and the lowest value is 0 at x = 0: a <strong>minimum</strong>. A negative a flips every value, giving a <strong>maximum</strong>. The steps (1, 3, 5 …) grow, so the curve bends smoothly — never a V."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🎚️ What a and q do", body:"Changing the sign of a reflects the graph in the x-axis. y = 3x² is narrower than y = x²; y = ½x² is wider. q moves the turning point to (0; q) without changing the width.<br><br>Domain is always ℝ. Range: y ≥ q if a > 0, y ≤ q if a < 0. x-intercepts: x² = −q/a; if that's negative, there are none (y = x² + 1)."},
      {t:"gold", title:"📍 From sketches, and lines meeting curves", body:"From a sketch: read q at the turning point, then substitute another point to find a.<br><br>With a line g on the same axes, set f(x) = g(x) and factorise (Algebra and Equations). “For which x is g(x) ≥ f(x)?” means where the line is on or above the curve: answer with an x-interval between the intersections, not with points."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Sketch y = −x² + 9", body:"a = −1: frown with a maximum.<br>Turning point and y-intercept: (0; 9).<br>x² = 9 → x = ±3.<br>Smooth curve through the three points, symmetric about x = 0.", answer:"Turning point <strong>(0; 9)</strong>, x-intercepts <strong>(±3; 0)</strong>, range <strong>y ≤ 9</strong>."},
      {title:"Find the equation from a sketch", body:"Turning point (0; −4), through (2; 4).<br>q = −4, so y = ax² − 4.<br>Substitute: 4 = a(2)² − 4 → 4a = 8 → a = 2.<br>Check: 2(4) − 4 = 4 ✓.", answer:"<strong>y = 2x² − 4</strong>."},
      {title:"Where a line beats a parabola", body:"f(x) = x² − 4, g(x) = x + 2.<br>x² − 4 = x + 2 → x² − x − 6 = 0.<br>(x − 3)(x + 2) = 0 → x = 3 or x = −2.<br>g(3) = 5, g(−2) = 0.<br>Test x = 0: g = 2 > f = −4, so the line is above between them.", answer:"Meet at <strong>(−2; 0) and (3; 5)</strong>; g(x) ≥ f(x) for <strong>−2 ≤ x ≤ 3</strong>."},
    ],
    formula:"y = ax² + q · turning point (0; q) · axis of symmetry x = 0",
    mistakes:["Range of y = −x² + 9 as y ≥ 9. It opens down. Fix: check the sign of a: y ≤ 9.","x² = 9 giving only x = 3. Fix: write ± every time.","Drawing a V. Fix: plot extra points near the turning point.","Answering “g(x) ≥ f(x)” with points. Fix: give the x-interval, −2 ≤ x ≤ 3."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"DStv dishes are parabolic: signals bounce to one point, where the LNB sits. Headlights use the same property in reverse. Sport scientists model a Springbok's kick at goal with parabolas, and civil engineers use them for arches and bridge cables."},
    activity:"A beachfront fountain's water follows h = −0.5x² + 2 (metres).<br>(a) Maximum height?<br>(b) Width of the splash zone?<br>(c) Height 1 m from the centre?<br>(d) Sensible domain and range?",
    activityAnswer:"(a) Turning point (0; 2): <strong>2 m</strong>.<br>(b) 0.5x² = 2 → x² = 4 → x = ±2: width <strong>4 m</strong>.<br>(c) −0.5(1)² + 2 = <strong>1.5 m</strong>.<br>(d) Domain −2 ≤ x ≤ 2; range 0 ≤ h ≤ 2.",
    examTip:"Sketch marks go to shape, x-intercepts, turning point and a label — show all four. For “f(x) ≥ g(x)”, write an x-interval and check whether endpoints are included. Show the line where you substitute a point to find a.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Parabola",d:"Graph of a quadratic function"},{w:"Turning point",d:"The highest or lowest point of the parabola"},{w:"Axis of symmetry",d:"Vertical line through the turning point"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"y = −4x² + 1 has a:",opts:["Minimum of 1","Maximum of 1","Minimum of −4","Maximum of −4"],a:1,et:"a < 0 → opens down → maximum at q = 1. The −4 sets width and direction, not height."},
      {q:"x-intercepts of y = x² − 25:",opts:["±5","±25","5 only","None"],a:0,et:"x² = 25 → x = ±5. “5 only” forgets that (−5)² is also 25."},
      {q:"Which parabola is the narrowest?",opts:["y = x²","y = ½x²","y = 3x²","y = −2x²"],a:2,et:"Larger |a| means narrower; |3| is largest. The minus in −2x² only flips it."},
    ],
    conclusion:"a sets direction and width; q sets the turning point (0; q). Use ± for x-intercepts and read inequalities between intersection x-values. In exams, label shape, intercepts and turning point."},

  3: {emoji:"🔀", subtitle:"Grade 10 · When one goes up, the other goes down",
    illusText:"Share a R600 prize among more friends and each gets less. That inverse relationship draws a hyperbola.",
    hook:{title:"Splitting the taxi hire", body:"Hiring a minibus for a matric dance costs R1 200. Shared by n learners, each pays y = 1 200/n (n is a whole number, so the real graph is dots on the curve). 10 learners: R120. 20: R60. 100: R12. Double the group, halve the share. It never reaches R0 and n can't be 0 — a <strong>hyperbola</strong> with asymptotes."},
    intro:"The Grade 10 hyperbola is y = a/x + q: two branches that never touch the <strong>asymptotes</strong> x = 0 and y = q. a > 0 puts the branches in quadrants 1 and 3 relative to the asymptotes; a < 0 in quadrants 2 and 4.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🚧 Why the asymptotes exist", body:"y = 1/x: x = 0.1 → 10, x = 0.01 → 100, x = −0.01 → −100. Near 0, y explodes, and at 0 there's no value (no dividing by zero): asymptote x = 0.<br><br>Far out, x = 1 000 → 0.001: a/x shrinks towards 0 but never equals it, so a/x + q never equals q. Draw both asymptotes dotted."},
      {t:"teal", title:"↗️ Quadrants and axes of symmetry", body:"a > 0: positive x gives positive a/x, so branches sit top-right and bottom-left. y = −2/x lies in quadrants 2 and 4.<br><br>Two <strong>axes of symmetry</strong> pass through (0; q): <strong>y = x + q</strong> and <strong>y = −x + q</strong>. For y = 4/x − 2: y = x − 2 and y = −x − 2. Grade 11 adds y = a/(x + p) + q."},
      {t:"gold", title:"📍 Intercepts and finding a, q", body:"No y-intercept. x-intercept: a/x = −q → x = −a/q (q ≠ 0). Domain x ≠ 0; range y ≠ q.<br><br>From a sketch: q from the horizontal asymptote, then one point gives a. Through (−2; 3) with q = 0: 3 = a/(−2) → a = −6. With q = 0, xy is constant — Boyle's law, pV = constant, in Physical Sciences."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Sketch y = 4/x − 2", body:"Asymptotes: x = 0 and y = −2 (dotted).<br>x-intercept: 4/x = 2 → x = 2.<br>a > 0: branches top-right and bottom-left.<br>Points: (1; 2), (−1; −6), (−2; −4).<br>Axes of symmetry: y = x − 2 and y = −x − 2.", answer:"Asymptotes <strong>x = 0, y = −2</strong>; x-intercept <strong>(2; 0)</strong>; axes <strong>y = x − 2</strong>, <strong>y = −x − 2</strong>."},
      {title:"Find a and q from a sketch", body:"Asymptote y = 1, point (2; 3).<br>q = 1, so y = a/x + 1.<br>3 = a/2 + 1 → a/2 = 2 → a = 4.<br>Check: 4/2 + 1 = 3 ✓.", answer:"<strong>a = 4, q = 1</strong>: y = 4/x + 1."},
      {title:"Speed vs time on the N3", body:"120 km: t = 120/v.<br>80 km/h: 120 ÷ 80 = 1.5 h.<br>100 km/h: 1.2 h — saves 18 min.<br>120 km/h: 1 h — saves only 12 min more.<br>The curve flattens towards its asymptote.", answer:"<strong>1.5 h</strong>, <strong>1.2 h</strong>, <strong>1 h</strong>: each extra 20 km/h buys less time."},
    ],
    formula:"y = a/x + q · asymptotes x = 0 and y = q · axes of symmetry y = x + q, y = −x + q",
    mistakes:["Letting the curve touch an asymptote. Fix: a/x is never 0, and x is never 0.","Giving a y-intercept. Fix: x = 0 is undefined, so write “none”.","Axes of symmetry as y = ±x when q ≠ 0. Fix: they pass through (0; q): y = ±x + q.","Wrong quadrants. Fix: test x = 1, giving y = a + q."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Inverse proportion runs travel times, cost-sharing and bulk pricing when a spaza owner buys at Makro. Boyle's law gives a hyperbola that engineers use for gas cylinders and scuba gear, and fleet planners use t = distance/speed to set delivery windows."},
    activity:"A stokvel pays out R24 000 equally among its members.<br>(a) Write the payout P per member for m members.<br>(b) Payout for 12 members? 16?<br>(c) How many members if each gets R800?<br>(d) What does the asymptote P = 0 mean?",
    activityAnswer:"(a) <strong>P = 24 000/m</strong>.<br>(b) <strong>R2 000</strong> and <strong>R1 500</strong>.<br>(c) 24 000 ÷ 800 = <strong>30 members</strong>.<br>(d) More members always means a smaller share, but never R0.",
    examTip:"Draw dotted asymptotes first and label them with equations, then the intercept and a point on each branch. Write axes of symmetry as equations (“y = x − 2”). Show the substitution line, e.g. 3 = a/2 + 1, for the method mark.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Hyperbola",d:"Graph of y = a/x + q"},{w:"Asymptote",d:"A line the graph approaches but never touches"},{w:"Inverse proportion",d:"As one quantity doubles, the other halves"},{w:"Axis of symmetry",d:"For y = a/x + q: the lines y = x + q and y = −x + q"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"The horizontal asymptote of y = 3/x + 5 is:",opts:["y = 3","x = 5","y = 0","y = 5"],a:3,et:"The horizontal asymptote is y = q = 5. y = 0 is only right when there's no +5 lifting the graph."},
      {q:"y = −2/x lies in quadrants:",opts:["1 and 3","2 and 4","1 and 2","3 and 4"],a:1,et:"a < 0 → quadrants 2 and 4. Test x = 1: y = −2, in quadrant 4."},
      {q:"x-intercept of y = 6/x − 3:",opts:["(2; 0)","(−2; 0)","(3; 0)","None"],a:0,et:"6/x = 3 → x = 2. (−2; 0) comes from a sign slip moving the −3 across."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Hyperbolas show inverse relationships. Draw the dotted asymptotes x = 0 and y = q, add the axes y = ±x + q, then plot points. In exams, label every asymptote and intercept."},

  4: {emoji:"🚀", subtitle:"Grade 10 · Growth and decay",
    illusText:"Linear growth adds the same amount each time. Exponential growth multiplies — and it always wins in the end.",
    hook:{title:"The R1 that doubles", body:"R10 000 today, or R1 that doubles every day for 30 days? Day 10 is only R512. But day 30: 2²⁹ = <strong>R536 870 912</strong>. Exponential growth starts slow, then explodes — the maths of compound interest, viral TikToks and outbreaks."},
    intro:"The Grade 10 exponential is y = a·bˣ + q, b > 0, b ≠ 1. With a > 0, b > 1 is <strong>growth</strong> and 0 < b < 1 is <strong>decay</strong>. The curve approaches the asymptote y = q but never touches it.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🚧 Why it never reaches the asymptote", body:"y = 2ˣ: x = −2, −1, 0, 1, 2 gives ¼, ½, 1, 2, 4. Each step left halves y, and halving a positive number never reaches 0. So bˣ > 0 always, and y = 2ˣ + q stays off y = q.<br><br>A line adds the same amount each step; an exponential multiplies by the same factor. That's simple versus compound interest. Trap: on the left, the curve looks as if it lands on the asymptote — on a sketch, keep a visible gap."},
      {t:"teal", title:"📈 Growth, decay and reflections", body:"(½)ˣ = 2⁻ˣ, so y = (½)ˣ is y = 2ˣ reflected in the y-axis. A negative a reflects the curve in its asymptote: y = −2ˣ lies below the x-axis.<br><br>Domain ℝ. Range y > q if a > 0, y < q if a < 0 — strict, because y = q is never reached."},
      {t:"gold", title:"📍 Features and equations", body:"y-intercept: b⁰ = 1, so y = a + q. x-intercept: solve a·bˣ + q = 0; y = 2ˣ + 1 has none.<br><br>From a graph: q from the asymptote, a from the y-intercept, b from another point (b² = 9 → b = 3, never −3). Next: A = P(1 + i)ⁿ (Compound Interest) and A = P(1 − i)ⁿ (Depreciation) are this graph."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Sketch y = 2ˣ − 4", body:"Asymptote y = −4 (dotted).<br>y-intercept: 1 − 4 = −3.<br>x-intercept: 2ˣ = 4 = 2² → x = 2.<br>b > 1, a > 0: rising curve above the asymptote.", answer:"Asymptote <strong>y = −4</strong>, intercepts <strong>(0; −3)</strong>, <strong>(2; 0)</strong>, range <strong>y > −4</strong>."},
      {title:"Find a and q, then the range", body:"y = a·2ˣ + q through (0; 3), asymptote y = 1.<br>q = 1.<br>y-intercept a + q = 3 → a = 2.<br>a > 0, so the curve is above y = 1.", answer:"<strong>y = 2·2ˣ + 1</strong>, range <strong>y > 1</strong>."},
      {title:"Phone value", body:"A R12 000 phone loses 20% a year: V = 12 000(0.8)ⁿ.<br>Losing 20% leaves 80%: b = 0.8, decay.<br>0.8³ = 0.512.<br>12 000 × 0.512 = R6 144.<br>A flat R2 400 a year would leave R4 800: 20% of a shrinking value is less each year.", answer:"After 3 years: <strong>R6 144</strong>."},
    ],
    formula:"y = a·bˣ + q · asymptote y = q · y-intercept (0; a + q)",
    mistakes:["Curve crossing the asymptote. Fix: bˣ is never 0 — let it flatten.","Thinking (0.8)ⁿ grows. Fix: 0 < b < 1 is decay.","Using b = −3 from b² = 9. Fix: the base must be positive, so b = 3.","Range y ≥ q. Fix: use y > q — the asymptote is never reached.","y-intercept of 3ˣ + 2 as (0; 2). Fix: 3⁰ = 1, so y = 3."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Store cards, home loans and retirement annuities all run on exponential maths — knowing it is how you avoid snowballing debt. Actuaries price insurance and pensions with it (Actuarial Science is offered at universities such as UCT, Wits, Stellenbosch and Pretoria), and epidemiologists used it to track COVID-19."},
    activity:"A town of 50 000 grows 3% a year: P = 50 000(1.03)ⁿ.<br>(a) Population after 1 year?<br>(b) After 10 years?<br>(c) After 10 years if it added a flat 1 500 a year?<br>(d) Which is bigger, and why?",
    activityAnswer:"(a) 50 000 × 1.03 = <strong>51 500</strong>.<br>(b) 1.03¹⁰ ≈ 1.344 → ≈ <strong>67 196</strong>.<br>(c) 50 000 + 15 000 = <strong>65 000</strong>.<br>(d) Exponential, by about 2 196: each 3% is taken of a bigger population.",
    examTip:"Label the dotted asymptote, the y-intercept and any x-intercept. To find an equation, show q from the asymptote then a from the y-intercept — two method marks. Ranges are strict: y > q.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Exponential growth",d:"Multiplying by the same factor b > 1 each step"},{w:"Exponential decay",d:"Multiplying by a factor 0 < b < 1 each step"},{w:"Asymptote",d:"y = q — the curve approaches but never reaches it"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"Which shows decay?",opts:["y = 3ˣ","y = (1.5)ˣ","y = (0.6)ˣ","y = 2ˣ + 1"],a:2,et:"0 < 0.6 < 1 → decay. y = 2ˣ + 1 still grows: the +1 lifts the curve but the base is 2."},
      {q:"y-intercept of y = 3ˣ + 2:",opts:["(0; 2)","(0; 3)","(0; 5)","(0; 1)"],a:1,et:"3⁰ + 2 = 1 + 2 = 3. (0; 2) treats 3⁰ as 0 — any positive base to the power 0 is 1."},
      {q:"Asymptote of y = 5·2ˣ − 1:",opts:["y = 5","y = 0","x = −1","y = −1"],a:3,et:"The asymptote is y = q = −1. The 5 only stretches the curve."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Exponentials multiply: b > 1 grows, 0 < b < 1 decays. Asymptote y = q, y-intercept a + q. In exams, label the dotted asymptote and use strict inequalities for the range."},

  5: {emoji:"🌊", subtitle:"Grade 10–11 · Patterns that repeat",
    illusText:"Tides at Durban harbour, the hours of daylight in Cape Town and the electricity in your wall socket all repeat like a sine wave.",
    hook:{title:"The tides at Durban harbour", body:"A ship's captain needs to know when the water is deep enough to enter the harbour. The level rises and falls about twice a day — a <strong>sine curve</strong>. “When is the water above the safe depth?” is the exam's “for which x is f(x) > k?”"},
    intro:"On 0° ≤ x ≤ 360°, y = sin x and y = cos x wave between −1 and 1 with <strong>period</strong> 360°; y = tan x has period 180° and <strong>asymptotes</strong> at 90° and 270°. <strong>Grade 10</strong>: a and q. <strong>Grade 11</strong>: k and p, on −360° ≤ x ≤ 360°, at most two parameters at a time.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"〰️ Where the wave comes from", body:"Walk a point round a circle of radius 1. At angle θ its height is sin θ (y/r with r = 1). Height against angle: 0, 1, 0, −1, 0 at 0°, 90°, 180°, 270°, 360° — the sine wave. Cos gives 1, 0, −1, 0, 1.<br><br>tan θ = sin θ/cos θ, so where cos θ = 0 (x = 90° + k·180°) tan has asymptotes. It has <strong>no amplitude</strong> and repeats every 180°."},
      {t:"teal", title:"📏 Grade 10: a and q", body:"Amplitude = |a|: y = 3 sin x runs from −3 to 3. Negative a flips it: y = −2 sin x peaks at 270°. q lifts it: y = cos x + 2 has range 1 ≤ y ≤ 3.<br><br>Range rule: a(1) + q and a(−1) + q. Trap: a never changes the period."},
      {t:"gold", title:"↔️ Grade 11: k and p", body:"In sin(kx), kx reaches 360° at x = 360°/k, so that's the period: cos 2x has period 180°. In sin(x − 30°) the peak moves to x − 30° = 90°, x = 120°: a shift <strong>right</strong>.<br><br>Two graphs on one axes: solve f(x) = g(x) for intersections (Trig Equations); “f(x) > g(x)” is an x-interval."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Grade 10: range of y = 2 cos x − 1", body:"cos x runs from −1 to 1, so use both extremes.<br>Max: 2(1) − 1 = 1, at 0° and 360°.<br>Min: 2(−1) − 1 = −3, at 180°.<br>Amplitude 2, period 360°, shifted down 1.", answer:"<strong>−3 ≤ y ≤ 1</strong>."},
      {title:"Grade 11: f(x) = cos(x − 60°), g(x) = sin 2x on [0°; 180°]", body:"Periods: 360° and 180°.<br>f(0°) = cos(−60°) = 0.5; f = 0 at x = 150°. g = 0 at 0°, 90°, 180°.<br>sin 2x = cos(90° − 2x) (co-function).<br>cos(x − 60°) = cos(90° − 2x) → x − 60° = ±(90° − 2x) + k·360°.<br>+: 3x = 150° + k·360°; k = 0: x = 50°; k = 1: x = 170°. −: x = 30°.<br>Check 30°: cos(−30°) = sin 60° ✓. Test 0°: f = 0.5 > g = 0. Test 40°: f ≈ 0.94 < g ≈ 0.98; test 180°: f = −0.5 < g = 0.", answer:"Meet at <strong>30°, 50°, 170°</strong>; f(x) > g(x) for <strong>0° ≤ x < 30° or 50° < x < 170°</strong>."},
      {title:"Grade 11: daylight in Cape Town", body:"D ≈ 2.2 cos(30m)° + 12.1, m = months after December (a model to read, not to sketch).<br>Period: 360° ÷ 30 = 12 months ✓.<br>Longest (cos = 1): 14.3 h.<br>Shortest, 30m = 180°, m = 6 (June): 9.9 h.<br>March (m = 3): cos 90° = 0, so D = 12.1 h — near the equinox.", answer:"<strong>14.3 h</strong> in December, <strong>9.9 h</strong> in June."},
    ],
    formula:"Amplitude = |a| · Period of sin kx, cos kx = 360°/k · Period of tan kx = 180°/k",
    mistakes:["Mixing sin and cos starts. Fix: sin starts at 0, cos at 1.","tan crossing its asymptotes. Fix: draw the dotted lines at 90° and 270° first.","Shifting sin(x − 30°) left. Fix: find the peak: x = 120°, so right.","Amplitude of 2 sin x + 3 as 5. Fix: q moves the wave; amplitude is still 2.","Thinking a changes the period. Fix: a stretches up and down; only k changes the period."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Eskom's AC supply is a 50 Hz sine wave — electrical engineers design transformers and rooftop solar inverters around it. Sound engineers shape amapiano bass as waves, and oceanographers predict tides for Transnet's ports."},
    activity:"For y = −2 sin x, 0° ≤ x ≤ 360°:<br>(a) Amplitude?<br>(b) Maximum and where?<br>(c) Range?<br>(d) Range of y = −2 sin x + 1?",
    activityAnswer:"(a) |−2| = <strong>2</strong>.<br>(b) <strong>2</strong> at <strong>270°</strong> (−2 × −1).<br>(c) <strong>−2 ≤ y ≤ 2</strong>.<br>(d) <strong>−1 ≤ y ≤ 3</strong>.",
    examTip:"Plot the five key x-values, label axes in degrees and mark interval endpoints. Marks go to shape, intercepts, turning points and endpoints. For “f(x) > g(x)”, answer with x-intervals and check endpoints. In Grade 11, state the period and shift first.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Amplitude",d:"Half the vertical distance between max and min"},{w:"Period",d:"Horizontal length of one full cycle"},{w:"Asymptote (tan)",d:"Vertical lines x = 90°, 270° where tan is undefined"},{w:"Horizontal shift (p)",d:"Grade 11: y = sin(x + p) moves the graph p° left (right if p is negative)"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"The maximum value of y = 4 sin x is:",opts:["4","1","−4","360"],a:0,et:"sin x ≤ 1, so 4 sin x ≤ 4. 1 is the maximum of sin x before the 4 stretches it."},
      {q:"Period of y = cos 2x:",opts:["90°","180°","360°","720°"],a:1,et:"360° ÷ 2 = 180°. 720° comes from multiplying — a bigger k squeezes the wave."},
      {q:"y = tan x has an asymptote at:",opts:["0°","180°","360°","90°"],a:3,et:"cos 90° = 0, so tan 90° is undefined. At 0°, 180° and 360°, tan x = 0 — x-intercepts."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Sin and cos come from the unit circle; tan has asymptotes where cos x = 0. a: amplitude; q: up or down; k: period; p: sideways. In exams, answer f(x) > g(x) as x-intervals."},
};

// ─── Module 2: Finance & Sequences ──────────────────────────────────────────
LESSON_DB.math.m2 = {
  0: {emoji:"🪜", subtitle:"Grade 10–12 · Adding the same amount every time",
    illusText:"Seats in a stadium, rungs on a ladder, a savings challenge — when you add the same amount each step, you have an arithmetic sequence.",
    hook:{title:"The 52-week savings challenge", body:"Ayanda from Soshanguve saves R20 in week 1, R30 in week 2, R40 in week 3… adding R10 every week. How much does she put away in week 52, and how much in total? Week 52: <strong>R530</strong>. Total for the year: <strong>R14 300</strong> — enough for a laptop. Two formulas do it in a minute."},
    intro:"An <strong>arithmetic sequence</strong> has a <strong>common difference d</strong>: each term = previous term + d. The first term is a. The n-th term is Tₙ = a + (n − 1)d. The <strong>sum</strong> of the first n terms (an arithmetic series, Grade 12) is Sₙ = n/2[2a + (n − 1)d].",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🔍 Spot it and find Tₙ", body:"5, 8, 11, 14… → d = 3. 20, 15, 10… → d = −5. Check: T₂ − T₁ = T₃ − T₂.<br><br>Why Tₙ = a + (n − 1)d? To reach term 1 you add d zero times; term 2, once; term 3, twice. So term n needs d added <strong>one time fewer</strong> than its position: (n − 1) times. For 5, 8, 11…: Tₙ = 5 + 3(n − 1) = 3n + 2. Which term is 101? 3n + 2 = 101 → n = 33.<br><br>Tₙ = 3n + 2 is linear (gradient 3): a constant first difference always gives a straight-line pattern."},
      {t:"teal", title:"➕ Why the sum formula works", body:"Write the sum forwards and backwards:<br>Sₙ = a + (a + d) + … + [a + (n − 1)d]<br>Sₙ = [a + (n − 1)d] + … + (a + d) + a<br>Add the two lines. Every column adds to the same total, 2a + (n − 1)d, and there are n columns. So 2Sₙ = n[2a + (n − 1)d], which gives Sₙ = n/2[2a + (n − 1)d].<br><br>With last term l: Sₙ = n/2(a + l). Gauss: 1 + 2 + … + 100 = 50 × 101 = 5 050."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🔁 Working backwards", body:"First, <strong>Tₙ = Sₙ − Sₙ₋₁</strong>. If Sₙ = n² + 4n, then T₅ = S₅ − S₄ = 45 − 32 = 13.<br><br>Second, finding <strong>n from a sum</strong> gives a quadratic (Sₙ contains n²). Reject negative or fractional roots — n counts terms. See Example 2."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Easy: Ayanda's week 52", body:"a = 20, d = 30 − 20 = 10, n = 52.<br>T₅₂ = 20 + (52 − 1) × 10 = 20 + 510.", answer:"T₅₂ = <strong>R530</strong>. Her total for the year is S₅₂ = 52/2[2(20) + 51(10)] = 26 × 550 = <strong>R14 300</strong>."},
      {title:"Exam standard: how many terms?", body:"How many terms of 5 + 8 + 11 + … add up to 440?<br>a = 5, d = 3, Sₙ = 440.<br>440 = n/2[2(5) + (n − 1)3] (substitute into Sₙ)<br>880 = n(10 + 3n − 3) (multiply both sides by 2)<br>880 = 3n² + 7n<br>3n² + 7n − 880 = 0 (standard form)<br>n = [−7 ± √(49 + 10 560)]/6 = (−7 ± 103)/6<br>n = 16 or n = −55/3 (reject: not a natural number)<br>Check: S₁₆ = 8[10 + 45] = 440 ✓", answer:"<strong>16 terms</strong>."},
      {title:"Real life: stadium seats", body:"Row 1 of a section at Moses Mabhida Stadium has 24 seats, and each row behind has 2 more. How many seats in 30 rows?<br>a = 24, d = 2, n = 30.<br>S₃₀ = 30/2[2(24) + 29(2)] (substitute into Sₙ)<br>= 15[48 + 58] = 15 × 106.", answer:"<strong>1 590 seats</strong>."},
    ],
    formula:"Tₙ = a + (n − 1)d · Sₙ = n/2[2a + (n − 1)d]",
    mistakes:["<strong>Using n instead of (n − 1).</strong> T₅₂ = 20 + 52 × 10 = 540 adds d once too often. Fix: use (n − 1).","<strong>Mixing up term and sum.</strong> “In week 52?” is T₅₂; “altogether?” is S₅₂. Fix: underline the question word.","<strong>Keeping an impossible n.</strong> n = −55/3 or 12.4 cannot count terms. Fix: reject them in writing; if no whole n exists, the number is not in the sequence.","<strong>Wrong sign of d.</strong> In 20, 15, 10… d = −5, not 5. Fix: d = T₂ − T₁."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Salary scales with fixed annual notches are arithmetic sequences. Quantity surveyors use Sₙ to count bricks in stepped walls and seats in tiered stadiums. A stokvel that raises its monthly contribution by a fixed amount can plan the year's payout with Sₙ."},
    activity:"A gym in Polokwane runs a promotion: R100 in month 1, then R15 more each month.<br>(a) What do you pay in month 12?<br>(b) What do you pay in total for the first year?<br>(c) In which month is the fee exactly R400?",
    activityAnswer:"a = 100, d = 15.<br>(a) T₁₂ = 100 + 11 × 15 = <strong>R265</strong>.<br>(b) S₁₂ = 12/2[200 + 11 × 15] = 6 × 365 = <strong>R2 190</strong>.<br>(c) 100 + 15(n − 1) = 400 → 15(n − 1) = 300 → n − 1 = 20 → <strong>month 21</strong>.",
    examTip:"Write a = …, d = …, n = … before substituting. If a question asks “which term”, your answer must be a whole number n. For “prove Sₙ”, write both lines (forwards and backwards) and show the addition.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Common difference (d)",d:"Constant amount added each step"},{w:"General term (Tₙ)",d:"Formula for any term in position n"},{w:"Series",d:"The sum of the terms of a sequence"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"d for 17, 13, 9, 5… is:",opts:["4","−4","−3","13"],a:1,et:"13 − 17 = −4."},
      {q:"T₂₀ of 3, 7, 11… is:",opts:["79","80","83","76"],a:0,et:"3 + 19 × 4 = 79."},
      {q:"1 + 2 + 3 + … + 50 =",opts:["1 250","1 275","2 500","2 550"],a:1,et:"50/2 × (1 + 50) = 25 × 51 = 1 275."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Arithmetic sequences grow by steady steps. Know a, d and n to find any term or total, prove Sₙ by adding it forwards and backwards, and solve a quadratic when the sum is given."},

  1: {emoji:"✖️", subtitle:"Grade 12 · Multiplying by the same amount every time",
    illusText:"A rumour that triples every hour, a ball that bounces to half its height — geometric sequences multiply.",
    hook:{title:"The chain message", body:"Kabelo in Mamelodi sends a scam-warning voice note to 3 friends, each forwards it to 3 more, and so on. Round 8 alone reaches 3 × 3⁷ = <strong>6 561 people</strong>, and the total after 8 rounds is <strong>9 840</strong>. Multiplying patterns explode — that's why fake news and warnings spread so fast on WhatsApp."},
    intro:"A <strong>geometric sequence</strong> has a <strong>common ratio r</strong>: each term = previous term × r. Tₙ = a·rⁿ⁻¹. The sum of n terms is Sₙ = a(rⁿ − 1)/(r − 1). If −1 < r < 1 the terms shrink and the series has a <strong>sum to infinity</strong>: S∞ = a/(1 − r). It is the engine behind compound interest and annuities.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🔍 Spot it and find Tₙ", body:"2, 6, 18, 54… → r = 3. 80, 40, 20… → r = ½. Check: T₂ ÷ T₁ = T₃ ÷ T₂. Term n has been multiplied by r one time fewer than its position, hence rⁿ⁻¹.<br><br>Finding n needs <strong>logs</strong>. Which term of 2, 6, 18… is 4 374? 2 × 3ⁿ⁻¹ = 4 374 → 3ⁿ⁻¹ = 2 187 → n − 1 = log 2 187 ÷ log 3 = 7 → n = 8.<br><br>Given two terms, divide them to remove a. If T₂ = 6 and T₅ = 162, then ar⁴ ÷ ar = r³ = 27, so r = 3 and a = 2."},
      {t:"teal", title:"➕ Why the sum formula works", body:"Write Sₙ = a + ar + ar² + … + arⁿ⁻¹. Multiply every term by r: rSₙ = ar + ar² + … + arⁿ. Subtract the first line from the second. Everything in the middle cancels, leaving rSₙ − Sₙ = arⁿ − a. Factorise: Sₙ(r − 1) = a(rⁿ − 1), so Sₙ = a(rⁿ − 1)/(r − 1), r ≠ 1.<br><br>For 2, 6, 18…: S₆ = 2(3⁶ − 1)/(3 − 1) = 2 × 728/2 = 728. For r < 1, use a(1 − rⁿ)/(1 − r)."},
      {t:"gold", title:"♾️ Sum to infinity", body:"If −1 < r < 1, rⁿ gets closer and closer to 0 as n grows (½, ¼, ⅛…). So Sₙ = a(1 − rⁿ)/(1 − r) settles on S∞ = a/(1 − r). If r ≥ 1 or r ≤ −1, rⁿ does not shrink, and the sum has no limit — it <strong>diverges</strong>.<br><br>A surprising CAPS example: 0.999… = 0.9 + 0.09 + 0.009 + … with a = 0.9, r = 0.1, so S∞ = 0.9/0.9 = 1. Yes, 0.999… is exactly 1."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Easy: chain message, round 8", body:"Each person forwards to 3 more, so a = 3, r = 3.<br>T₈ = 3 × 3⁸⁻¹ = 3 × 2 187.<br>S₈ = 3(3⁸ − 1)/(3 − 1) = 3 × 6 560/2.", answer:"T₈ = <strong>6 561</strong> people in round 8; S₈ = <strong>9 840</strong> people in total."},
      {title:"Exam standard: two terms given", body:"In a geometric sequence T₃ = 12 and T₆ = 96. Find S₁₀.<br>ar² = 12 and ar⁵ = 96.<br>Divide: ar⁵ ÷ ar² = r³ = 96 ÷ 12 = 8 → r = 2.<br>Substitute back: a × 4 = 12 → a = 3.<br>S₁₀ = 3(2¹⁰ − 1)/(2 − 1) = 3 × 1 023.", answer:"<strong>S₁₀ = 3 069</strong>."},
      {title:"Challenging: the bouncing ball", body:"A ball is dropped from 4 m and bounces to 50% of its previous height each time. Find the total vertical distance it travels.<br>Down first: 4 m.<br>Each bounce goes up and comes down: heights 2, 1, 0.5… (a = 2, r = ½).<br>Up-and-down total = 2 × S∞ = 2 × 2/(1 − ½) = 2 × 4 = 8 m.<br>Total = 4 + 8.", answer:"<strong>12 m</strong>. (The first drop only goes down.)"},
    ],
    formula:"Tₙ = a·rⁿ⁻¹ · Sₙ = a(rⁿ − 1)/(r − 1) · S∞ = a/(1 − r), −1 < r < 1",
    mistakes:["<strong>Using rⁿ instead of rⁿ⁻¹.</strong> T₅ of 2, 6, 18… is 2 × 3⁴ = 162, not 2 × 3⁵ = 486. Fix: the exponent is one less than the term number.","<strong>Calculating S∞ when r = 2.</strong> 3/(1 − 2) = −3 is nonsense. Fix: check −1 < r < 1 first and write “S∞ does not exist” otherwise.","<strong>Writing (ar)ⁿ⁻¹.</strong> Only r is raised to the power. Fix: work out rⁿ⁻¹ first, then multiply by a.","<strong>Dividing the wrong way to find r.</strong> T₁ ÷ T₂ gives 1/r. Fix: r = later term ÷ earlier term."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Compound interest is a geometric sequence, and so is a post going viral. Early in each COVID-19 wave, South Africa's case numbers grew roughly geometrically — doubling every few days — which is why the NICD tracked doubling times. Pyramid schemes promise returns that need recruits to multiply by r every round — they soon run out of recruits and collapse."},
    activity:"Use the sequence 5, 10, 20, 40…<br>(a) Find T₉.<br>(b) Find the smallest n for which Sₙ > 10 000.<br>(c) Use a sum to infinity to write 0.444… as a fraction.",
    activityAnswer:"(a) a = 5, r = 2. T₉ = 5 × 2⁸ = <strong>1 280</strong>.<br>(b) 5(2ⁿ − 1) > 10 000 → 2ⁿ − 1 > 2 000 → 2ⁿ > 2 001 → n > log 2 001 ÷ log 2 ≈ 10.97 → <strong>n = 11</strong>. Check: S₁₀ = 5 115 and S₁₁ = 10 235.<br>(c) 0.444… = 0.4 + 0.04 + 0.004 + …, a = 0.4, r = 0.1. S∞ = 0.4/0.9 = <strong>4/9</strong>.",
    examTip:"For “for which values of x does the series converge?”, set −1 < r < 1 and solve the inequality for x. Example: Σ(x − 1)ᵏ has r = x − 1, so −1 < x − 1 < 1 gives 0 < x < 2. Ratio, inequality, answer: three marks.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Common ratio (r)",d:"Constant multiplier from one term to the next"},{w:"Convergent series",d:"−1 < r < 1; the sum approaches a fixed value"},{w:"Sum to infinity",d:"S∞ = a/(1 − r)"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"r for 64, 16, 4… is:",opts:["4","−48","¼","−¼"],a:2,et:"16 ÷ 64 = ¼."},
      {q:"T₅ of 2, 6, 18… is:",opts:["54","162","486","30"],a:1,et:"2 × 3⁴ = 162."},
      {q:"S∞ of 10 + 5 + 2.5 + … =",opts:["15","17.5","20","Doesn't exist"],a:2,et:"r = ½ → 10/(1 − ½) = 20."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Geometric sequences multiply. Prove Sₙ by multiplying by r and subtracting, use logs to find n, and only find S∞ when −1 < r < 1."},

  2: {emoji:"🏦", subtitle:"Grade 10 · Interest on the original amount only",
    illusText:"Before you borrow from a mashonisa, know how to calculate what it really costs.",
    hook:{title:"The mashonisa loan", body:"A neighbour in KwaMashu borrows R1 000 from a mashonisa (informal lender) and must pay back R1 300 at month-end. That's R300 interest on R1 000 in one month = <strong>30% per month</strong>. Over a year that's 360% simple interest — R4 600 to pay back on R1 000."},
    intro:"<strong>Simple interest</strong> is calculated only on the original amount (principal) every period. Formula: A = P(1 + in), where A is the final amount, P the principal, i the interest rate as a decimal and n the number of periods (years when i is per year). Interest earned = A − P. This lesson also covers hire purchase and <strong>exchange rates</strong>.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🧾 Why simple interest is a straight line", body:"Expand the formula: A = P + Pin. The interest Pi is the same every period, so the amount grows by equal steps — an arithmetic sequence, and a straight-line graph y = mx + c with gradient Pi and y-intercept P.<br><br>The mashonisa loan: A = 1 000 + 300n. After 1 month R1 300, after 12 months 1 000 + 3 600 = R4 600.<br><br>A <strong>timeline</strong> helps: mark “now” with P, mark the end with A, and write i and n on the arrow."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🔄 Working backwards", body:"Substitute and solve the linear equation.<br>How long for R5 000 to grow to R6 500 at 6% simple? 6 500 = 5 000(1 + 0.06n) → 1.3 = 1 + 0.06n → n = 5 years.<br>Rate: R4 000 grows to R4 960 in 4 years → 1.24 = 1 + 4i → i = 0.06 = 6% p.a."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🛒 Hire purchase and the law", body:"On hire purchase (HP) you pay a deposit, and simple interest is charged on the <strong>whole balance for the whole term</strong> — even though you pay some of it back every month. That is why HP is expensive.<br><br>The National Credit Act protects borrowers: lenders must be registered with the National Credit Regulator, interest and fees are capped by law, and reckless lending is not allowed. An unregistered mashonisa charging 30% a month is breaking the law."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Easy: a fixed deposit", body:"R2 000 is invested at 7% p.a. simple interest for 3 years.<br>P = 2 000, i = 0.07, n = 3.<br>A = 2 000(1 + 0.07 × 3) = 2 000 × 1.21.", answer:"A = <strong>R2 420</strong>; interest = R420."},
      {title:"Exam standard: HP on a fridge", body:"A R8 000 fridge at a furniture store in Mthatha: 10% deposit, the rest at 18% p.a. simple interest over 2 years. Find the monthly instalment.<br>Deposit = 0.1 × 8 000 = R800.<br>Balance = 8 000 − 800 = R7 200.<br>A = 7 200(1 + 0.18 × 2) = 7 200 × 1.36 = R9 792.<br>Instalment = 9 792 ÷ 24 months.", answer:"<strong>R408 per month</strong>. Total paid: 800 + 9 792 = R10 592 for an R8 000 fridge."},
      {title:"Real life: exchange rates", body:"Lerato needs US$350 for a school robotics trip. The bank sells dollars at R17.50 per $1.<br>Cost = 350 × 17.50 = R6 125.<br>Two months later the rand weakens to R19.00 per $1.<br>Cost = 350 × 19.00 = R6 650.<br>Difference = 6 650 − 6 125 = R525.<br>Going the other way, R5 000 at R17.50/$ buys 5 000 ÷ 17.50 ≈ $285.71.", answer:"The trip costs <strong>R6 125</strong> at first and <strong>R6 650</strong> after the rand weakens — R525 more for the same dollars."},
    ],
    formula:"A = P(1 + in) · Interest = A − P",
    formulaNote:"Change the % to a decimal (7% → 0.07) and make sure n is in years if i is per year.",
    mistakes:["<strong>Using 7 instead of 0.07.</strong> R2 000(1 + 7 × 3) = R44 000 — clearly impossible. Fix: divide the percentage by 100.","<strong>Using months with an annual rate.</strong> n = 18 with 6% p.a. multiplies the interest by 12. Fix: write n = 18/12 = 1.5 years.","<strong>Forgetting the deposit in HP.</strong> Interest is charged on the balance after the deposit. Fix: write deposit and balance as separate lines first.","<strong>Dividing instead of multiplying with exchange rates.</strong> $350 ÷ 17.50 = 20 is not the rand price. Fix: rands = dollars × (rands per dollar)."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Store accounts, HP and short-term loans use simple interest. Exchange rates hit every family: petrol, cellphones and medicine are priced in dollars, so a weaker rand pushes up prices at the till. Forex traders and importers work with them daily."},
    activity:"A laptop costs R9 000 cash, or R1 000 deposit and R450 a month for 24 months.<br>(a) What is the total cost on credit, and how much extra is that?<br>(b) Treat the extra as simple interest on the R8 000 balance for 2 years. What annual rate is that?<br>(c) The same laptop costs US$480 online at R18.20 per $1, plus R650 shipping. Is it cheaper than R9 000 cash?",
    activityAnswer:"(a) Credit total = 1 000 + 450 × 24 = <strong>R11 800</strong>. Extra = 11 800 − 9 000 = <strong>R2 800</strong>.<br>(b) 2 800 = 8 000 × i × 2 → i = 2 800 ÷ 16 000 = 0.175 = <strong>17.5% p.a.</strong><br>(c) 480 × 18.20 = R8 736; plus R650 = <strong>R9 386</strong>. That is R386 more than buying locally for cash.",
    examTip:"In HP questions, show the deposit, balance, total amount and instalment as separate lines — each earns a mark. For exchange rates, write the rate with units (R17.50/$) to show why you multiplied.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Principal (P)",d:"The original amount borrowed or invested"},{w:"Simple interest",d:"Interest only on the principal"},{w:"Hire purchase",d:"Buying on credit with a deposit and fixed instalments"},{w:"Exchange rate",d:"The price of one currency in another, e.g. R17.50 per US$1"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"R3 000 at 5% p.a. simple for 4 years gives:",opts:["R3 600","R3 646.52","R3 150","R600"],a:0,et:"3 000(1 + 0.05 × 4) = R3 600."},
      {q:"At R18.40 per US dollar, how much does $250 cost in rand?",opts:["R13.59","R268.40","R4 600","R460"],a:2,et:"Rands = 250 × 18.40 = R4 600. R13.59 comes from dividing — but one dollar costs more than one rand, so the rand amount must be bigger than 250."},
      {q:"R2 000 earns R480 simple interest in 3 years. Rate?",opts:["6%","8%","12%","24%"],a:1,et:"480 = 2 000 × i × 3 → i = 0.08 = 8%."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Simple interest grows in a straight line and is easy to be fooled by. Show every HP step, multiply for exchange rates, and always work out the total cost before signing."},

  3: {emoji:"💰", subtitle:"Grade 10–11 · Interest on interest",
    illusText:"“Compound interest is the eighth wonder of the world. He who understands it, earns it; he who doesn't, pays it.”",
    hook:{title:"Start saving at 18 or at 28?", body:"Nomsa from Gqeberha invests R5 000 at 8% p.a. compounded monthly when she turns 18. After 3 years it's <strong>R6 351.19</strong> — without adding a cent. The extra R1 351 came from interest earning interest. The same maths works against you on a credit card."},
    intro:"With <strong>compound interest</strong>, interest is added to the balance and then also earns interest. A = P(1 + i)ⁿ, where i is the rate <strong>per compounding period</strong> and n is the <strong>number of periods</strong>. Monthly compounding: i = annual rate ÷ 12, n = years × 12. You'll also meet inflation, population growth, effective rates and timelines.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"📈 Why compound growth curves upwards", body:"Each period the balance is multiplied by (1 + i), so the amounts form a geometric sequence and the graph is an exponential curve — the y = abˣ graph from Functions with b = 1 + i.<br><br>R10 000 at 10% p.a.: simple gives R13 000 after 3 years and compound R13 310 — only R310 apart. After 30 years, simple gives R40 000 but compound gives R174 494. Time is the secret ingredient.<br><br>The same formula models <strong>inflation</strong> (prices grow at i) and <strong>population growth</strong>. At 6% inflation prices double in about 12 years, because 1.06¹² ≈ 2."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🔎 Nominal vs effective rate", body:"A <strong>nominal</strong> rate is the quoted annual rate, e.g. 12% p.a. compounded monthly. The bank actually adds 1% every month, and each 1% earns interest too. The <strong>effective</strong> rate is the true one-year growth:<br>1 + i_eff = (1 + i_nom/m)ᵐ, where m is the number of compounding periods per year.<br>12% monthly: (1.01)¹² − 1 = 0.1268 → 12.68% p.a. effective.<br><br>The effective rate is always higher than the nominal rate (unless m = 1), because of interest on interest within the year. Use it to compare offers that compound differently. Periods: annually 1, half-yearly 2, quarterly 4, monthly 12, daily 365."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🗓️ Timelines with changes", body:"Real accounts change: the rate drops, or someone withdraws money. Draw a timeline in years, mark every change, and work in <strong>segments</strong>. Grow the money to the first change, adjust it (new rate, or subtract a withdrawal), then carry that value forward.<br><br>CAPS warns: keep full calculator values between segments (use the ANS or memory button). Rounding R56 308.1209… to R56 308 at each step changes the final answer by about 70c — enough to lose the accuracy mark. Example 3 shows the full method."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Easy: simple vs compound", body:"R10 000 at 10% p.a. for 3 years.<br>Simple: A = 10 000(1 + 0.1 × 3) = 10 000 × 1.3.<br>Compound (annually): A = 10 000(1.1)³ = 10 000 × 1.331.", answer:"Simple R13 000; compound <strong>R13 310</strong>."},
      {title:"Exam standard: compare two offers", body:"Save R10 000 for 2 years: 9% p.a. compounded annually, or 8.8% p.a. compounded monthly?<br>Offer A: 10 000(1.09)² = R11 881.00.<br>Offer B: i = 0.088/12, n = 24 → 10 000(1 + 0.088/12)²⁴ ≈ R11 916.73.<br>Effective rate of B: (1 + 0.088/12)¹² − 1 ≈ 9.16% p.a., higher than 9%.", answer:"The lower nominal rate wins: <strong>R11 916.73</strong> vs R11 881.00."},
      {title:"Challenging: a CAPS timeline", body:"R50 000 is invested at 8% p.a. compounded quarterly for 18 months. Then the rate changes to 6% p.a. compounded monthly. R10 000 is withdrawn at the end of year 2. Find the value at the end of year 4.<br>0 → 1.5 years: 50 000(1 + 0.08/4)⁶ = R56 308.12<br>1.5 → 2 years: × (1 + 0.06/12)⁶ = R58 018.62<br>Withdraw: 58 018.62 − 10 000 = R48 018.62<br>2 → 4 years: × (1.005)²⁴", answer:"<strong>R54 124.66</strong> (calculator values kept throughout)."},
    ],
    formula:"A = P(1 + i)ⁿ · 1 + i_eff = (1 + i_nom/m)ᵐ",
    formulaNote:"Round only at the very end — using 1.0067 instead of 1 + 0.08/12 puts Nomsa's answer about R7.58 out.",
    mistakes:["<strong>Annual rate with monthly n.</strong> 5 000(1.08)³⁶ gives almost R80 000 for Nomsa — nonsense. Fix: i = 0.08/12 when n = 36.","<strong>Rounding i/12 early.</strong> 0.0067 instead of 0.08/12 changes the answer. Fix: type 0.08 ÷ 12 into the bracket.","<strong>Comparing nominal rates.</strong> 9% annually looks better than 8.8% monthly but isn't. Fix: convert both to effective rates.","<strong>Ignoring the timeline order.</strong> Subtracting a withdrawal from the original P instead of from the value at that date. Fix: grow the money to the date of the withdrawal first.","<strong>Using simple interest when it says “compounded”.</strong> Fix: the word compounded always means A = P(1 + i)ⁿ."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Tax-free savings accounts, retirement annuities, stokvel bank accounts, credit cards and home loans all compound. Statistics South Africa reports CPI inflation every month, and pension funds use it to grow payouts. Actuaries use this formula daily."},
    activity:"(a) A loaf of bread costs R18. Inflation is 6% p.a. What will it cost in 5 years?<br>(b) Find the effective rate of 12% p.a. compounded monthly.<br>(c) A town near Rustenburg has 40 000 people and grows at 2.5% p.a. Estimate the population after 10 years.",
    activityAnswer:"(a) 18(1.06)⁵ ≈ <strong>R24.09</strong>.<br>(b) (1 + 0.12/12)¹² − 1 = 1.01¹² − 1 ≈ 0.1268 → <strong>12.68% p.a.</strong><br>(c) 40 000(1.025)¹⁰ ≈ 51 203.4 → about <strong>51 203 people</strong> (round people to a whole number).",
    examTip:"Write i and n separately before substituting, e.g. “i = 0.08/12, n = 36”. It prevents the most common error and earns method marks. For timeline questions, draw the timeline — examiners often award a mark for it — and label each segment's rate and length.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Compound interest",d:"Interest calculated on principal plus previous interest"},{w:"Nominal rate",d:"Quoted annual rate before compounding"},{w:"Effective rate",d:"Actual annual growth after compounding"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"R1 000 at 10% p.a. compounded annually for 2 years:",opts:["R1 200","R1 210","R1 100","R1 221"],a:1,et:"1 000 × 1.1² = R1 210."},
      {q:"For 6% p.a. compounded monthly over 4 years, n =",opts:["4","12","48","24"],a:2,et:"4 years × 12 months = 48 periods."},
      {q:"Which earns the most at the same nominal rate?",opts:["Annual","Half-yearly","Quarterly","Monthly"],a:3,et:"More frequent compounding gives more interest."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Compound interest grows exponentially. Match i and n to the compounding period, compare offers with effective rates, and work timelines in segments with full calculator accuracy."},

  4: {emoji:"🚗", subtitle:"Grade 11 · How things lose value",
    illusText:"The moment you drive a new car off the showroom floor, it's worth less. Depreciation measures how much.",
    hook:{title:"Is the taxi still worth its loan?", body:"A taxi owner on the Bree Street rank in Johannesburg buys a new minibus for R450 000. With reducing-balance depreciation of 15% p.a., after 4 years it's worth about <strong>R234 903</strong>. If he still owes more than that on the loan, selling won't cover the debt. Depreciation helps owners plan when to replace vehicles and equipment."},
    intro:"<strong>Depreciation</strong> is the loss in value of an asset over time. <strong>Straight-line</strong> (simple) depreciation loses the same amount each year: A = P(1 − in). <strong>Reducing-balance</strong> (compound) depreciation loses a percentage of the current value each year: A = P(1 − i)ⁿ. They mirror simple and compound interest, with a minus sign.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"📉 Straight-line", body:"Every year the asset loses i × P — the same rand amount, worked out on the <strong>original</strong> cost. So the graph is a straight line with a negative gradient, and it hits zero after 1/i years. At 15% p.a., the R450 000 taxi loses R67 500 a year and reaches zero after 1/0.15 ≈ 6.67 years.<br><br>In Accounting this is the cost-price method, used for equipment such as furniture and computers."},
      {t:"teal", title:"📉 Reducing balance", body:"Every year the asset loses i of its <strong>current</strong> value, so it keeps (1 − i) of it. The drops get smaller: the taxi loses R67 500 in year 1 but R57 375 in year 2 (15% of R382 500). The graph is an exponential decay curve.<br><br>Why does it never reach zero? (1 − i) is between 0 and 1, and a positive number multiplied by a positive number stays positive. The curve gets closer to the x-axis (an asymptote) but never touches it — the same idea as y = bˣ with 0 < b < 1.<br><br>Accounting calls this the diminishing-balance method, worked on the carrying value."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🔍 Book value, scrap value and finding i", body:"<strong>Book value</strong> (carrying value) = cost minus depreciation so far. <strong>Scrap value</strong> = what the asset is worth at the end of its useful life.<br><br>To find the rate, substitute and solve. Straight-line gives a linear equation. Reducing balance needs a root: R20 000 worth R10 240 after 3 years → (1 − i)³ = 0.512 → 1 − i = ∛0.512 = 0.8 → i = 20%.<br><br>To find how long until the value drops below a target, try whole years on your calculator (Grade 11), or use logs in Grade 12."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Easy: straight-line taxi", body:"R450 000 at 15% p.a. straight-line for 4 years.<br>A = 450 000(1 − 0.15 × 4) = 450 000 × 0.4.", answer:"<strong>R180 000</strong> — compare R234 902.81 on reducing balance."},
      {title:"Exam standard: find both rates", body:"Machinery bought for R10 000 is worth R5 000 after 4 years. Find the rate for each method.<br>Straight-line: 5 000 = 10 000(1 − 4i) → 0.5 = 1 − 4i → 4i = 0.5 → i = 0.125.<br>Reducing balance: 5 000 = 10 000(1 − i)⁴ → (1 − i)⁴ = 0.5 → 1 − i = ⁴√0.5 ≈ 0.840896 → i ≈ 0.159104.", answer:"<strong>12.5% p.a. straight-line</strong>; <strong>15.91% p.a. reducing balance</strong>. Reducing balance needs a higher rate because each year's % is taken from a smaller value."},
      {title:"Challenging: when does it drop below R200 000?", body:"The R450 000 taxi depreciates at 15% p.a. reducing balance. When is it first worth less than R200 000?<br>Try n = 4: 450 000(0.85)⁴ = R234 902.81 (still above).<br>Try n = 5: 450 000(0.85)⁵ = R199 667.39 (below).", answer:"During the <strong>5th year</strong>: R199 667.39 at the end of year 5."},
    ],
    formula:"Straight-line: A = P(1 − in) · Reducing balance: A = P(1 − i)ⁿ",
    mistakes:["<strong>Using (1 + i).</strong> That's growth, not loss: the taxi would be worth more each year. Fix: depreciation always has a minus.","<strong>Mixing up the methods.</strong> Using P(1 − i)ⁿ when the question says straight-line. Fix: circle the method word before you start.","<strong>Accepting a negative straight-line value.</strong> 450 000(1 − 0.15 × 8) = −R90 000 is impossible. Fix: the value stops at R0.","<strong>Dividing to find i in reducing balance.</strong> 0.5 ÷ 4 gives the straight-line rate, not the reducing-balance one. Fix: take the n-th root of (A/P).","<strong>Rounding the root early.</strong> ⁴√0.5 rounded to 0.84 gives 16%, not 15.91%. Fix: keep the full calculator value."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Accountants record depreciation on fixed assets in every company's financial statements, and SARS allows wear-and-tear deductions on business assets. Insurers pay out a car's current value, not what you paid."},
    activity:"A tractor costs R600 000 and depreciates at 12% p.a.<br>(a) Find its value after 5 years using straight-line depreciation.<br>(b) Find its value after 5 years using reducing balance.<br>(c) Using straight-line, in which year does its value reach R0?",
    activityAnswer:"(a) 600 000(1 − 0.12 × 5) = 600 000 × 0.4 = <strong>R240 000</strong>.<br>(b) 600 000(0.88)⁵ ≈ <strong>R316 639.15</strong>. Reducing balance leaves more after 5 years.<br>(c) 1 − 0.12n = 0 → n = 1/0.12 ≈ 8.33. After 8 years it is worth 600 000 × 0.04 = R24 000, so it reaches R0 during the <strong>9th year</strong>.",
    examTip:"Look for the words “straight-line” / “simple decline” vs “reducing balance” / “compound decline” — they decide which formula to use. Questions that give two values and ask for i are common: show the substitution, then the root or division, then the % answer to 2 decimal places.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Depreciation",d:"Loss in value of an asset over time"},{w:"Straight-line",d:"Same amount lost every year"},{w:"Reducing balance",d:"Same percentage of the current value lost every year"},{w:"Book value",d:"Value of an asset after depreciation so far"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"A R10 000 phone depreciates 20% p.a. straight-line. Value after 2 years?",opts:["R6 400","R6 000","R8 000","R4 000"],a:1,et:"10 000(1 − 0.2 × 2) = R6 000."},
      {q:"Same phone, 20% reducing balance, 2 years:",opts:["R6 400","R6 000","R8 000","R3 600"],a:0,et:"10 000 × 0.8² = R6 400."},
      {q:"Reducing-balance value can reach zero:",opts:["After 1/i years","Never","After 10 years","When i = 50%"],a:1,et:"(1 − i)ⁿ is always positive for 0 < i < 1, so it never reaches zero."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Straight-line loses the same amount and hits zero; reducing balance loses a percentage and never does. Read the method word, use roots to find i, and test whole years for “when”."},

  5: {emoji:"🏠", subtitle:"Grade 12 · Regular payments: saving and paying off loans",
    illusText:"A home loan, a car loan, a retirement annuity — all are annuities: equal payments at regular intervals.",
    hook:{title:"What does that car really cost?", body:"Zanele, a nurse at Bara Hospital, finances a R200 000 car at 12% p.a. compounded monthly over 5 years. Her instalment is <strong>R4 448.89</strong> a month. Over 60 months she pays R266 933 — that's <strong>R66 933 in interest</strong>. Annuity formulas show this before you sign."},
    intro:"An <strong>annuity</strong> is a series of equal payments made at regular intervals. <strong>Future value</strong> (saving towards a goal): F = x[(1 + i)ⁿ − 1]/i. <strong>Present value</strong> (a loan you pay off): P = x[1 − (1 + i)⁻ⁿ]/i. x is the payment, i the rate per period and n the number of payments. Both formulas assume the first payment is made one period from now.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"💵 Future value — where F comes from", body:"Save x at the end of every month for n months. The last payment earns no interest; the one before earns one month's; the first earns n − 1 months'. So F = x + x(1 + i) + x(1 + i)² + … + x(1 + i)ⁿ⁻¹.<br><br>That is a geometric series with a = x and r = 1 + i. Using Sₙ = a(rⁿ − 1)/(r − 1): F = x[(1 + i)ⁿ − 1]/i.<br><br>Use F for retirement annuities, saving for a deposit or a <strong>sinking fund</strong> — money a business sets aside each month to replace a machine."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🏦 Present value — loans", body:"Use P when you get the money now and pay it back: a bond, car loan or student loan. P is the loan amount.<br><br>The <strong>outstanding balance</strong> after k payments is the present value of the payments still left. So with n − k payments left, balance = x[1 − (1 + i)^−(n−k)]/i.<br><br>To find <strong>n</strong>, rearrange so (1 + i)⁻ⁿ is alone, then use logs. R25 000 at 11% p.a. monthly repaid at R800 a month: (1 + i)⁻ⁿ = 1 − 25 000i/800 → n ≈ 36.99, so 37 payments, the last one slightly smaller."},
      {t:"gold", title:"⚖️ Thinking critically", body:"A longer term means lower instalments, more interest. Zanele's loan over 3 years costs R6 642.86 a month and about R39 143 in interest — roughly R27 800 less than over 5 years.<br><br>If payments start later, grow the loan first, then use P.<br><br><strong>Pyramid schemes</strong> pay early members with new members' money and promise returns no annuity could produce. When recruitment slows, they collapse and late joiners lose their money. A stokvel is not one: members get back their own pooled money."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Easy: saving R500 a month", body:"Thando saves R500 at the end of every month at 6% p.a. compounded monthly for 5 years.<br>x = 500, i = 0.06/12 = 0.005, n = 60.<br>F = 500[(1.005)⁶⁰ − 1]/0.005.", answer:"<strong>F = R34 885.02</strong>. She paid in R30 000, so R4 885.02 is interest."},
      {title:"Exam standard: Zanele's car loan", body:"P = R200 000, i = 0.12/12 = 0.01, n = 60. Find x.<br>200 000 = x[1 − 1.01⁻⁶⁰]/0.01<br>x = 200 000 × 0.01 ÷ (1 − 1.01⁻⁶⁰).<br>Total paid = 60 × 4 448.89 = R266 933.40.", answer:"x = <strong>R4 448.89</strong> a month; interest ≈ <strong>R66 933</strong>."},
      {title:"Challenging: outstanding balance", body:"Sipho borrows R25 000 at 11% p.a. compounded monthly, repaid over 5 years.<br>i = 0.11/12, n = 60.<br>x = 25 000 × (0.11/12) ÷ [1 − (1 + 0.11/12)⁻⁶⁰] ≈ R543.56.<br>After 24 payments, 36 remain.<br>Balance = 543.56…[1 − (1 + 0.11/12)⁻³⁶]/(0.11/12).", answer:"Instalment <strong>R543.56</strong>; balance after 24 payments ≈ <strong>R16 602.99</strong> (with R543.56 rounded you get R16 602.97 — both are accepted)."},
    ],
    formula:"F = x[(1 + i)ⁿ − 1]/i · P = x[1 − (1 + i)⁻ⁿ]/i",
    formulaNote:"Payments and compounding must use the same period (both monthly).",
    mistakes:["<strong>Using F for a loan.</strong> F answers “how much will I have?”, not “what must I pay back?”. Fix: money received now → P.","<strong>Dropping the negative exponent.</strong> 1 − (1 + i)ⁿ gives a negative answer. Fix: P has (1 + i)⁻ⁿ.","<strong>Balance = loan minus payments made.</strong> 25 000 − 24 × 543.56 ignores interest. Fix: use the PV of the remaining payments.","<strong>Rounding n down.</strong> n = 36.99 means 37 payments, not 36. Fix: round up and note the last payment is smaller."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Bonds, car finance, student loans and retirement annuities all run on these formulas; bank credit analysts and financial planners use them daily. Before signing, ask for the pre-agreement statement and quotation, which must show the total cost of credit."},
    activity:"You can afford R2 500 a month at 11% p.a. compounded monthly.<br>(a) How big a loan can you afford over 6 years?<br>(b) How much interest would you pay on that loan?<br>(c) If you borrow R100 000 instead, how many payments of R2 500 are needed?",
    activityAnswer:"i = 0.11/12.<br>(a) P = 2 500[1 − (1 + 0.11/12)⁻⁷²]/(0.11/12) ≈ <strong>R131 343</strong> (round down to the nearest rand).<br>(b) Paid 2 500 × 72 = R180 000. Interest ≈ 180 000 − 131 343 = <strong>R48 657</strong>.<br>(c) 100 000 = 2 500[1 − (1 + i)⁻ⁿ]/i → (1 + i)⁻ⁿ = 1 − 100 000i/2 500 ≈ 0.63333 → n = −log 0.63333 ÷ log(1 + i) ≈ 50.06 → <strong>51 payments</strong> (the last one is small).",
    examTip:"First decide: is the money received now (P) or at the end (F)? Write that decision down. Then write x, i, n and the substitution. For “n” questions, show the log step and explain why you round up.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Annuity",d:"Equal payments at regular intervals"},{w:"Future value",d:"Value of all payments at the end, including interest"},{w:"Present value",d:"Today's value of future payments — the loan amount"},{w:"Outstanding balance",d:"Present value of the payments still to be made"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"A home loan uses which formula?",opts:["Future value","Present value","Simple interest","Straight-line"],a:1,et:"You receive the money now and repay it: present value."},
      {q:"For monthly payments over 20 years, n =",opts:["20","120","240","12"],a:2,et:"20 × 12 = 240 payments."},
      {q:"A longer loan term usually means:",opts:["Lower instalment, more total interest","Higher instalment, less interest","Same total interest","No interest"],a:0,et:"You pay less per month but for longer, so the total interest goes up."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Annuities are geometric series of payments. Use F for saving and P for loans, find balances as the PV of what's left, round n up, and always draw the timeline."},
};

// ─── Module 3: Trigonometry ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
LESSON_DB.math.m3 = {
  0: {emoji:"📐", subtitle:"Grade 10 · Trig ratios, special angles and the Cartesian plane",
    illusText:"How high is that cellphone tower? How steep is that ramp? With one angle and one side, trig tells you.",
    hook:{title:"Is the ramp wheelchair-friendly?", body:"SA building regulations say a wheelchair ramp should rise no more than 1 m for every 12 m along the ground. What angle is that? tan θ = 1/12 → θ ≈ <strong>4.8°</strong>. A 10° ramp is too steep and dangerous. One calculator button checks whether a ramp meets the rule."},
    intro:"Label the sides of a right-angled triangle relative to θ: <strong>hypotenuse</strong> (opposite the 90°), <strong>opposite</strong> and <strong>adjacent</strong>. Then: sin θ = O/H, cos θ = A/H, tan θ = O/A — <strong>SOH CAH TOA</strong>. Grade 10 adds the reciprocals <strong>cosec θ = H/O</strong>, <strong>sec θ = H/A</strong> and <strong>cot θ = A/O</strong>, special angles, simple equations and angles up to 360°.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🔢 Why the ratio never changes", body:"A ramp rising 1 m over 12 m and one rising 2 m over 24 m have the same angle. The triangles are <strong>similar</strong>, so opposite ÷ adjacent is 1/12 in both. A trig ratio depends only on the angle, never on the size of the triangle.<br><br>To find a side, pick the ratio linking known and wanted sides. A 6 m ladder at 70° to the ground: sin 70° = h/6 → h ≈ 5.64 m. To find an angle, use the inverse: 50 m from a 30 m tower, tan θ = 30/50 → θ ≈ 31.0°.<br><br><strong>Elevation</strong> is measured up from the horizontal, <strong>depression</strong> down from it; they are equal alternate angles."},
      {t:"teal", title:"📏 Special angles and simple equations", body:"Rebuild 30°, 45° and 60° in seconds. Half a unit square gives a 45° triangle with hypotenuse √2: sin 45° = cos 45° = 1/√2, tan 45° = 1. Half an equilateral triangle of side 2 has sides 1, √3, 2: sin 30° = cos 60° = ½, cos 30° = sin 60° = √3/2, tan 30° = 1/√3, tan 60° = √3.<br><br>Equations (Grade 10: acute angles only): isolate the ratio. 2 cos x = 1 → cos x = ½ → x = 60°. For tan 2x = 1.2, find 2x first, then halve.<br><br>Caution: 2 sin θ ≠ sin 2θ. 2 sin 30° = 1 but sin 60° ≈ 0.866."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🧭 Off the triangle: x, y and r", body:"Join a point P(x; y) to the origin. θ is measured anticlockwise from the positive x-axis, and r = √(x² + y²) is always positive. Then sin θ = y/r, cos θ = x/r, tan θ = y/x — SOHCAHTOA in Q1, but now θ runs from 0° to 360°.<br><br>The signs of x and y give the <strong>CAST</strong> diagram: Q1 All positive, Q2 Sin, Q3 Tan, Q4 Cos. For P(−3; 4): r = 5, sin θ = 4/5, cos θ = −3/5, tan θ = −4/3. This sketch drives Grade 11 identities and reduction formulae."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Special angles (no calculator)", body:"Find sin 30° + cos 60°.<br>Triangle with sides 1, √3, 2: sin 30° = 1/2 and cos 60° = 1/2.", answer:"sin 30° + cos 60° = <strong>1</strong>."},
      {title:"5 sin θ + 4 = 0 with θ ∈ [90°; 270°]", body:"Find 5 cos θ + 3 tan θ without a calculator.<br>sin θ = −4/5, so y = −4, r = 5.<br>sin negative and θ ∈ [90°; 270°] → Q3, x negative.<br>x² = 25 − 16 = 9 → x = −3.<br>5(−3/5) + 3(−4/−3) = −3 + 4.", answer:"5 cos θ + 3 tan θ = <strong>1</strong>."},
      {title:"The tower and the double angle", body:"From 50 m away, the top of a 60 m tower has elevation 2x. Find x.<br>tan 2x = O/A = 60/50 = 1.2.<br>2x = tan⁻¹(1.2) ≈ 50.19°, so x ≈ 25.10°.<br>Trap check: 2 tan 25.1° ≈ 0.94, not 1.2.", answer:"x ≈ <strong>25.1°</strong>."},
    ],
    formula:"sin θ = O/H · cos θ = A/H · tan θ = O/A",
    formulaNote:"Calculator in DEGREE mode (D on screen) — the most common cause of wrong answers.",
    mistakes:["RAD mode: sin 30 shows −0.988. Fix: check for D on the screen.","Labelling O and A from the wrong angle — they swap when θ moves. Fix: mark θ first.","Writing sin = 0.5. A ratio needs its angle. Fix: write sin 30° = 0.5.","Treating tan 2x = 1.2 as tan x = 0.6 — the 2 is inside the angle. Fix: solve for 2x, then halve.","Giving r a minus sign. r is a distance. Fix: only x and y take signs, from CAST."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"A pilot landing at OR Tambo follows a 3° glide slope: 10 km out, the plane should be about 10 000 × tan 3° ≈ 524 m up. Land surveyors, roofers setting roof pitch and engineers designing legal ramps use SOHCAHTOA daily."},
    activity:"a) A 40 m kite string makes 55° with the ground. How high is the kite?<br>b) Solve √3 tan x = 1 for acute x, without a calculator.<br>c) If 13 cos θ − 5 = 0 and θ ∈ [180°; 360°], find sec θ + tan θ.",
    activityAnswer:"a) h = 40 sin 55° ≈ <strong>32.8 m</strong>.<br>b) tan x = 1/√3 = tan 30°, so x = <strong>30°</strong>.<br>c) cos θ = 5/13: x = 5, r = 13. cos positive in [180°; 360°] → Q4, y = −√(169 − 25) = −12. sec θ = 13/5, tan θ = −12/5. Sum = <strong>1/5</strong>.",
    examTip:"In “without a calculator” questions, the x, y, r sketch in the right quadrant earns marks, and so does the third side with its sign. The interval tells you the quadrant. Leave fractions and surds.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Hypotenuse",d:"Longest side, opposite the right angle"},{w:"Angle of elevation",d:"Angle measured up from the horizontal"},{w:"Inverse trig",d:"sin⁻¹, cos⁻¹, tan⁻¹ give the angle from a ratio"},{w:"Reciprocal ratios",d:"cosec θ = 1/sin θ, sec θ = 1/cos θ, cot θ = 1/tan θ"},{w:"CAST",d:"Which ratios are positive in quadrants 1 to 4"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"In a right triangle, opposite = 3 and hypotenuse = 5. sin θ =",opts:["3/4","4/5","3/5","5/3"],a:2,et:"sin = opposite/hypotenuse = 3/5. 4/5 would be cos θ (adjacent 4 by Pythagoras), and 5/3 is cosec θ."},
      {q:"tan 45° =",opts:["0","√2","½","1"],a:3,et:"In a 45° right triangle the two legs are equal, so opposite/adjacent = 1. √2 is the hypotenuse of that triangle, not a ratio."},
      {q:"To find an angle from tan θ = 0.75 you use:",opts:["tan⁻¹(0.75)","tan 0.75","0.75 × 45","sin⁻¹(0.75)"],a:0,et:"The inverse tan gives the angle: tan⁻¹(0.75) ≈ 36.9°. Fun fact: that's the angle in a 3–4–5 triangle, which builders use to set out square corners."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Trig ratios depend only on the angle. Label the triangle or sketch x, y, r in the right quadrant. In the exam, show the sketch and leave surds."},

  1: {emoji:"🧩", subtitle:"Grade 11 · Two ways of writing the same thing",
    illusText:"R1 = 100c. Different forms, same value. Trig identities are swaps you can always make.",
    hook:{title:"Simplifying before you build", body:"Sound engineers mixing a track at a Joburg studio work with wave formulas full of sin² and cos². Using one identity, sin²θ + cos²θ = 1, a messy expression collapses to a single number — fewer calculations, fewer errors. In the exam, identities turn scary proofs into a few clean lines."},
    intro:"An <strong>identity</strong> is true for every valid angle, not just one. The two you must know: <strong>tan θ = sin θ / cos θ</strong> (cos θ ≠ 0) and <strong>sin²θ + cos²θ = 1</strong>, which also gives sin²θ = 1 − cos²θ and cos²θ = 1 − sin²θ. CAPS wants you to derive them, prove with them and state where an identity is undefined.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🔁 Where the identities come from", body:"Use the Grade 10 sketch: P(x; y), distance r from the origin.<br><br>Quotient identity: sin θ/cos θ = (y/r) ÷ (x/r) = y/x = tan θ. It fails when cos θ = 0 (θ = 90°, 270°, …).<br><br>Square identity: Pythagoras gives x² + y² = r². Divide every term by r²: (x/r)² + (y/r)² = 1, so cos²θ + sin²θ = 1. Test θ = 30°: ¾ + ¼ = 1. It holds in every quadrant."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🟰 Spotting the swaps", body:"Replace tan with sin/cos: tan θ · cos θ = sin θ. Look for 1 − sin²θ = cos²θ and 1 − cos²θ = sin²θ, and for a difference of squares: 1 − sin²x = (1 − sin x)(1 + sin x).<br><br>That gives the <strong>conjugate trick</strong>: with 1 − sin x in a denominator, multiply top and bottom by 1 + sin x; the bottom becomes cos²x. Trap: sin²x means (sin x)², not sin(x²)."},
      {t:"gold", title:"📝 How to prove — and when it is undefined", body:"An identity is what you are proving, so you may not assume it: no adding to both sides, no cross-multiplying. Work on <strong>one side only</strong> (usually the more complicated one): change to sin and cos, find common denominators, factorise, use the identities, cancel factors, and stop when you reach the other side.<br><br>“Where is it undefined?” means: where is a denominator zero, or tan undefined? Set each to 0 and solve. This leads into trig equations next."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Simplify sin x/tan x", body:"Replace tan x with sin x/cos x.<br>sin x ÷ (sin x/cos x) = sin x × cos x/sin x.<br>Cancel the common factor sin x.", answer:"sin x/tan x = <strong>cos x</strong>."},
      {title:"Prove cos x/(1 − sin x) = (1 + sin x)/cos x", body:"Work on the LHS; multiply top and bottom by the conjugate (1 + sin x).<br>LHS = cos x(1 + sin x)/[(1 − sin x)(1 + sin x)]<br>= cos x(1 + sin x)/(1 − sin²x) (difference of squares)<br>= cos x(1 + sin x)/cos²x (square identity)<br>= (1 + sin x)/cos x (cancel one cos x).<br>Check x = 30°: both sides ≈ 1.732.", answer:"LHS = (1 + sin x)/cos x = <strong>RHS</strong> ✓"},
      {title:"Prove (1 − cos²x)/(sin x cos x) = tan x, and say where it is undefined", body:"LHS = sin²x/(sin x cos x) (square identity)<br>= sin x/cos x (cancel sin x)<br>= tan x = RHS ✓<br>Undefined when the denominator sin x cos x = 0, so sin x = 0 or cos x = 0 (tan x is also undefined when cos x = 0).<br>sin x = 0 at 0°, 180°, 360°, …; cos x = 0 at 90°, 270°, …", answer:"Undefined for <strong>x = k·90°, k ∈ ℤ</strong>."},
    ],
    formula:"tan θ = sin θ/cos θ · sin²θ + cos²θ = 1",
    mistakes:["Working on both sides like an equation — that assumes what you must prove. Fix: work down one side only.","Writing sin²x as sin x², which is the sine of x squared. Fix: sin²x = (sin x)².","Cancelling terms: (sin x + cos x)/sin x is not 1 + cos x. Fix: split it into 1 + cos x/sin x.","Ignoring undefined values: you may divide by cos x only if cos x ≠ 0. Fix: list where denominators are zero.","Writing 1 − sin x = cos x. Only squares work. Fix: test with x = 30°."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Eskom's grid runs on 50 Hz alternating current; electrical engineers use trig identities to show that balanced three-phase power stays steady instead of pulsing. Audio and cellphone-signal engineers simplify wave equations with the same identities."},
    activity:"a) Simplify (sin²θ − 1)/cos θ.<br>b) Prove 1/cos x − cos x = sin x tan x.<br>c) For which values of x in [0°; 360°] is the identity in (b) undefined?",
    activityAnswer:"a) sin²θ − 1 = −(1 − sin²θ) = −cos²θ. So −cos²θ/cos θ = <strong>−cos θ</strong>.<br>b) LHS = (1 − cos²x)/cos x (common denominator) = sin²x/cos x = sin x · (sin x/cos x) = sin x tan x = RHS ✓<br>c) Both sides divide by cos x, so it is undefined when cos x = 0: <strong>x = 90° or 270°</strong>.",
    examTip:"Write LHS = … and keep going until you reach the RHS; end with “= RHS”. Marks go to each correct identity used and to the final step, so a clear structure earns marks even if you get stuck. For “undefined” questions, show the denominator = 0 and solve it.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Identity",d:"An equation true for all valid values of the variable"},{w:"Quotient identity",d:"tan θ = sin θ / cos θ"},{w:"Square identity",d:"sin²θ + cos²θ = 1"},{w:"Conjugate",d:"1 + sin x pairs with 1 − sin x to make 1 − sin²x"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"1 − cos²θ =",opts:["sin θ","tan²θ","sin²θ","cos²θ"],a:2,et:"From sin²θ + cos²θ = 1. “sin θ” is the common slip: only the squares appear in the identity."},
      {q:"tan θ · cos θ =",opts:["sin θ","cos²θ","1","tan²θ"],a:0,et:"(sin θ/cos θ) × cos θ = sin θ, valid wherever cos θ ≠ 0."},
      {q:"In a proof you should:",opts:["Add the same thing to both sides","Work on one side until it equals the other","Square both sides","Cross-multiply"],a:1,et:"A proof shows LHS = RHS; it isn't solving an equation. Squaring both sides can even “prove” false statements, because −1 and 1 have the same square."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Two identities, derived from x, y and r, unlock almost every trig proof. Change to sin and cos, work on one side, and list where denominators are zero."},

  2: {emoji:"🎡", subtitle:"Grade 11 · Angles bigger than 90°",
    illusText:"Ferris wheels, turbine blades, clock hands — angles go past 90°. Reduction formulae bring them back.",
    hook:{title:"The wind turbine blade", body:"On a Jeffreys Bay wind farm a blade tip turns through 360°. At 210° it is below the hub. How far? You need sin 210°. Reduction formulae tell you sin 210° = −sin 30° = −½ — so a 50 m blade tip is 25 m <strong>below</strong> the hub. The sign tells you the direction."},
    intro:"Every angle has a <strong>reference angle</strong> in the first quadrant. The <strong>CAST</strong> diagram tells you which ratios are positive in each quadrant: Q1 <strong>A</strong>ll, Q2 <strong>S</strong>in, Q3 <strong>T</strong>an, Q4 <strong>C</strong>os. Reduction formulae rewrite sin, cos and tan of any angle in terms of the reference angle. CAPS wants you to know where they come from.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🪞 Where the reductions come from", body:"P(x; y) is at angle θ in Q1, distance r from O.<br><br><strong>180° − θ</strong>: reflect P in the y-axis to (−x; y). So sin(180° − θ) = y/r = sin θ, cos(180° − θ) = −x/r = −cos θ, tan(180° − θ) = −tan θ.<br><strong>180° + θ</strong>: rotate P through 180° to (−x; −y). So sin → −sin θ, cos → −cos θ, tan → (−y)/(−x) = tan θ.<br><strong>360° − θ or −θ</strong>: reflect in the x-axis to (x; −y). So sin → −sin θ, cos → cos θ, tan → −tan θ.<br><br>No table needed: the function stays and CAST gives the sign."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🔄 Co-functions: the 90° swaps", body:"Reflect P(x; y) in the line y = x and you get (y; x) at angle 90° − θ. So sin(90° − θ) = x/r = cos θ and cos(90° − θ) = sin θ.<br><br>Rotate P through 90° anticlockwise and it lands on (−y; x) at angle 90° + θ. So sin(90° + θ) = x/r = cos θ and cos(90° + θ) = −y/r = −sin θ.<br><br>Negative angles combine rules: sin(θ − 90°) = −sin(90° − θ) = −cos θ. Only 90° ± θ (and 270° ± θ) swap sin and cos; 180° ± θ and 360° − θ keep the function."},
      {t:"gold", title:"♾️ Big angles and “in terms of p”", body:"sin, cos and tan repeat every 360°, so subtract 360° until the angle is between 0° and 360°: sin 480° = sin 120° = sin 60°. tan repeats every 180°, so tan(540° + θ) = tan(180° + θ) = tan θ.<br><br>Exams often give a ratio as a letter. If sin 23° = p, sketch 23° with y = p, r = 1, so x = √(1 − p²). Reduce each angle to 23° and read off the sketch. Next: trig equations."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Evaluate sin 210° without a calculator", body:"210° = 180° + 30°, so the reference angle is 30°.<br>210° is in Q3, where only tan is positive, so sin is negative.<br>sin(180° + 30°) = −sin 30°.", answer:"sin 210° = −sin 30° = <strong>−½</strong>."},
      {title:"Simplify [sin(180° + x)·cos(90° − x)] / [tan(360° − x)·cos(180° − x)]", body:"Reduce each factor:<br>sin(180° + x) = −sin x<br>cos(90° − x) = sin x<br>tan(360° − x) = −tan x<br>cos(180° − x) = −cos x<br>Numerator: (−sin x)(sin x) = −sin²x.<br>Denominator: (−tan x)(−cos x) = tan x cos x = (sin x/cos x)·cos x = sin x.<br>−sin²x/sin x.", answer:"= <strong>−sin x</strong>."},
      {title:"If sin 23° = p, write cos 113° and tan 23° in terms of p", body:"Sketch: y = p, r = 1, so x = √(1 − p²) (23° is in Q1, x positive).<br>cos 113° = cos(90° + 23°) = −sin 23° = −p.<br>tan 23° = y/x = p/√(1 − p²).<br>Check: cos 113° ≈ −0.3907 = −sin 23° ✓", answer:"cos 113° = <strong>−p</strong> and tan 23° = <strong>p/√(1 − p²)</strong>."},
    ],
    formula:"CAST: Q1 All · Q2 Sin · Q3 Tan · Q4 Cos are positive",
    mistakes:["Writing sin 210° = +½ because sin 30° is positive. Fix: find the quadrant of the original angle (210° is in Q3), then use CAST.","Swapping sin to cos for 180° ± x. Fix: only 90° ± x (or 270° ± x) swaps.","Forgetting sin(−x) = −sin x while cos(−x) = cos x. Fix: treat −x as Q4.","Writing sin(x − 90°) = cos x. Fix: write it as −sin(90° − x) = −cos x.","In “in terms of p” questions, putting a minus on √(1 − p²) for an acute angle. Fix: in Q1 every side is positive."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Game developers in Cape Town studios rotate characters with these sign rules, and Eskom's alternating current is a sine wave whose sign flips 100 times a second at 50 Hz."},
    activity:"a) Without a calculator: cos 300° + sin 150°.<br>b) Simplify sin(x − 90°) · tan(540° + x).<br>c) If cos 40° = k, write sin 220° in terms of k.",
    activityAnswer:"a) cos 300° = cos(360° − 60°) = cos 60° = ½ (Q4). sin 150° = sin(180° − 30°) = sin 30° = ½ (Q2). Total = <strong>1</strong>.<br>b) sin(x − 90°) = −cos x and tan(540° + x) = tan(180° + x) = tan x. Product: −cos x · sin x/cos x = <strong>−sin x</strong>.<br>c) Sketch 40°: x = k, r = 1, so y = √(1 − k²). sin 220° = sin(180° + 40°) = −sin 40° = <strong>−√(1 − k²)</strong>.",
    examTip:"Write each reduction on its own line, e.g. “sin(180° + x) = −sin x”, before substituting — each one is usually a mark. In “in terms of p” questions, the sketch with the third side earns a mark — draw it.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Reference angle",d:"The acute angle between the terminal arm and the x-axis"},{w:"CAST",d:"Mnemonic for which ratios are positive in each quadrant"},{w:"Co-function",d:"sin and cos swap at 90° − x and 90° + x"},{w:"Period",d:"How often a ratio repeats: 360° for sin and cos, 180° for tan"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"cos(180° − x) =",opts:["cos x","−cos x","sin x","−sin x"],a:1,et:"180° − x is in Q2, where only sin is positive, so cos is negative. There's no 90°, so cos stays cos."},
      {q:"sin 330° =",opts:["½","√3/2","−√3/2","−½"],a:3,et:"330° = 360° − 30° → −sin 30° = −½. The reference angle is 30°, not 60°, so √3/2 values are wrong."},
      {q:"sin(90° − x) =",opts:["sin x","−sin x","cos x","−cos x"],a:2,et:"Co-function identity. That's where the name comes from: cosine is the “complement's sine”."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Reflections and rotations of (x; y) give every reduction formula. Keep the function for 180° and 360°, swap it for 90°, and let CAST give the sign. Show each reduction line."},

  3: {emoji:"🎯", subtitle:"Grade 11–12 · Finding all the angles that work",
    illusText:"sin x = ½ has infinitely many answers. A general solution captures them all in one line.",
    hook:{title:"When is the tide high enough?", body:"A Kalk Bay fishing boat needs 3 m of water. In a simple model, depth d = 2.5 + sin(30t°) metres, t hours after midnight. d ≥ 3 when sin(30t°) ≥ ½, so 30t is from 30° to 150°: t from 1 to 5. Repeating every 12 hours, the skipper can sail 01:00–05:00 and 13:00–17:00."},
    intro:"To solve a trig equation: isolate the trig ratio, find the <strong>reference angle</strong> (ignore the sign), use CAST to pick the quadrants, and write the <strong>general solution</strong> by adding k·360° (for sin and cos) or k·180° (for tan), where k ∈ ℤ. Grade 12 adds double- and compound-angle equations.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"♾️ Why there are infinitely many answers", body:"sin and cos repeat every 360°: if 30° works, so do 390° and −330°. So we add k·360°. tan repeats every 180°, so one quadrant plus k·180° covers it.<br><br>2 sin x = 1 → sin x = ½ → reference 30°; sin positive in Q1 and Q2 → x = 30° + k·360° or 150° + k·360°, k ∈ ℤ.<br><br>For a negative ratio, use the positive value: cos x = −½ → reference 60°; Q2 and Q3 → x = 120° or 240°, + k·360°."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🧮 Factorise — never divide by something that can be zero", body:"2 sin²x − sin x = 0 → sin x(2 sin x − 1) = 0 → sin x = 0 or ½. Dividing by sin x would lose every sin x = 0 solution.<br><br>Dividing by cos x is fine when it cannot be zero. In sin x = 2 cos x, cos x = 0 would force sin x = ±1 ≠ 0, so divide: tan x = 2.<br><br>Grade 12 link: cos 2x + sin x = 0 becomes 1 − 2 sin²x + sin x = 0, i.e. 2 sin²x − sin x − 1 = 0 — the same factorising skill."},
      {t:"gold", title:"📋 From general to specific solutions", body:"For an interval, write the general solution, then try k = −1, 0, 1, … For sin 2x = 0.6, 0° ≤ x ≤ 180°: 2x = 36.9° + k·360° or 143.1° + k·360°, so x = 18.4° + k·180° or 71.6° + k·180°. In range: 18.4° and 71.6°.<br><br>When sin meets cos, use sin A = cos(90° − A), then cos A = cos B → A = ±B + k·360°."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Solve tan x = −1, general solution", body:"Reference 45°; tan negative in Q2: 180° − 45° = 135°.<br>k·180° already includes the Q4 answer, 315°.", answer:"<strong>x = 135° + k·180°</strong>, k ∈ ℤ."},
      {title:"Solve 2 sin²x − sin x − 1 = 0", body:"Factorise like 2a² − a − 1 = (2a + 1)(a − 1):<br>(2 sin x + 1)(sin x − 1) = 0.<br>sin x = −½: reference 30°, Q3 and Q4 → 210° and 330°.<br>sin x = 1: x = 90°.", answer:"<strong>x = 90° + k·360°, 210° + k·360° or 330° + k·360°</strong>, k ∈ ℤ."},
      {title:"Solve sin(x + 30°) = cos 2x", body:"Co-function: sin(x + 30°) = cos(90° − (x + 30°)) = cos(60° − x).<br>So cos 2x = cos(60° − x), and cos A = cos B gives A = ±B + k·360°.<br>Case 1: 2x = 60° − x + k·360° → 3x = 60° + k·360° → x = 20° + k·120°.<br>Case 2: 2x = −(60° − x) + k·360° → 2x = −60° + x + k·360° → x = −60° + k·360°.<br>Check x = 20°: sin 50° = cos 40° ✓", answer:"<strong>x = 20° + k·120° or x = −60° + k·360°</strong>, k ∈ ℤ. In [0°; 360°]: 20°, 140°, 260° and 300°."},
    ],
    formula:"sin/cos: + k·360° · tan: + k·180° · k ∈ ℤ",
    mistakes:["Using sin⁻¹(−½) = −30° as the reference — it hides the quadrants. Fix: use the positive value, then CAST.","Leaving out “k ∈ ℤ”. Fix: always write it; it is a mark.","Dividing by sin x and losing sin x = 0. Fix: factorise instead.","Adding k·360° after halving for sin 2x. Fix: add it to 2x, then divide every term by 2.","Using k·360° for tan, missing half the answers. Fix: tan gets k·180°."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Transnet port controllers in Durban and Richards Bay time deep-draught ships with tide tables built from sine models, and solar installers solve trig equations for the sun's height through the day."},
    activity:"a) Solve 2 cos x = √3 for 0° ≤ x ≤ 360°.<br>b) Find the general solution of sin x = 2 cos x.<br>c) Use (b) to list the solutions in [−180°; 180°].",
    activityAnswer:"a) cos x = √3/2 → reference 30°; cos positive in Q1 and Q4 → x = <strong>30° or 330°</strong>.<br>b) cos x ≠ 0 here, so divide: tan x = 2 → <strong>x = 63.4° + k·180°</strong>, k ∈ ℤ.<br>c) k = 0: 63.4°; k = −1: −116.6°; k = 1: 243.4°, outside. So <strong>−116.6° and 63.4°</strong>.",
    examTip:"Reference angle, quadrants and general solution each earn a mark, so put them on separate lines and write “k ∈ ℤ”. For an interval, show the k values you tried.",
    keyterms:[{w:"General solution",d:"All solutions, written with + k·360° or + k·180°"},{w:"Reference angle",d:"The acute angle from the inverse of the positive ratio"},{w:"k ∈ ℤ",d:"k is any integer (…, −1, 0, 1, …)"},{w:"Specific solution",d:"The solutions that lie in a given interval"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"General solution of sin x = 0:",opts:["x = k·360°","x = k·180°","x = 90° + k·180°","x = k·90°"],a:1,et:"sin x = 0 at 0°, 180°, 360°, … so x = k·180°. k·360° misses 180°, and k·90° wrongly includes 90°, where sin x = 1."},
      {q:"cos x = −½. Which quadrants?",opts:["1 and 2","1 and 4","2 and 3","3 and 4"],a:2,et:"cos is negative where x is negative: Q2 and Q3, giving 120° and 240°."},
      {q:"For tan, the general solution adds:",opts:["k·90°","k·360°","k·45°","k·180°"],a:3,et:"tan has period 180°: tan(180° + x) = tan x, because both x and y change sign."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Isolate, reference angle, CAST, general solution with k ∈ ℤ. Factorise instead of dividing by zero-able terms. In the exam, show each step and list specific solutions from k."},

  4: {emoji:"🔺", subtitle:"Grade 11 · Triangles without a right angle",
    illusText:"Most real triangles — farm plots, roof trusses, a ship's course — don't have a 90° angle. The sine and cosine rules handle them all.",
    hook:{title:"Measuring a farm plot", body:"A farmer in the Free State has a triangular field. Two fences measure 40 m and 55 m and meet at 70°. Using trig, the area is <strong>≈ 1 034 m²</strong> and the third fence is <strong>≈ 55.9 m</strong> — no need to walk it with a tape measure. Land surveyors do this every day."},
    intro:"In any triangle ABC (side a opposite angle A, etc.): <strong>Sine rule</strong>: a/sin A = b/sin B = c/sin C. <strong>Cosine rule</strong>: a² = b² + c² − 2bc cos A. <strong>Area rule</strong>: Area = ½ab sin C (two sides and the angle between them). All three proofs are examinable.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"✏️ Proving the area and sine rules", body:"In △ABC, draw height AD = h ⊥ BC. In △ABD: h = c sin B. In △ACD: h = b sin C.<br><br>Area rule: Area = ½ × base × height = ½ac sin B — no height needed.<br><br>Sine rule: c sin B = b sin C; divide by sin B sin C: b/sin B = c/sin C. A height from another vertex gives a/sin A. (If B is obtuse, sin(180° − B) = sin B keeps the proof.)"},
      {t:"teal", title:"✏️ Proving the cosine rule", body:"Let BD = x, so DC = a − x. In △ABD: c² = h² + x² and x = c cos B.<br><br>In △ACD: b² = h² + (a − x)² = h² + x² + a² − 2ax = c² + a² − 2ax. Substitute x: <strong>b² = a² + c² − 2ac cos B</strong>. (If B is obtuse, x = c cos B is negative and the same algebra still holds.)<br><br>If B = 90°, cos B = 0 and you get Pythagoras. For an angle: cos A = (b² + c² − a²)/2bc. Sides 5, 7, 8: the angle opposite 5 has cos A = 88/112 → A ≈ 38.2°."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🧭 Which rule when — and the ambiguous case", body:"Know a side and its opposite angle, plus one more side or angle → <strong>sine rule</strong>. Two sides and the included angle, or all three sides → <strong>cosine rule</strong>. Two sides and the included angle, and you want area → <strong>area rule</strong>.<br><br>Ambiguous case: sin θ = sin(180° − θ), so the obtuse partner of a sine-rule angle may also fit — if it plus the known angle is under 180°. This toolkit carries into 3D problems next."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Sine rule: find b", body:"In △ABC, a = 10, A = 40°, B = 65°.<br>a and A known → sine rule.<br>b = 10 sin 65°/sin 40° = 9.063/0.6428.", answer:"b ≈ <strong>14.1</strong>."},
      {title:"Ambiguous case: a = 8, b = 10, A = 40°", body:"sin B/b = sin A/a → sin B = 10 sin 40°/8 ≈ 0.8035.<br>B = sin⁻¹(0.8035) ≈ 53.5°, or B = 180° − 53.5° = 126.5°.<br>Test: 40° + 126.5° = 166.5° < 180°, so both triangles exist.<br>So C ≈ 86.5° or 13.5°.", answer:"<strong>B ≈ 53.5° or B ≈ 126.5°</strong> — two valid triangles."},
      {title:"Two boats leave Durban harbour", body:"Courses 50° apart; one boat travels 12 km, the other 15 km. Distance apart?<br>Two sides, included angle → cosine rule.<br>d² = 12² + 15² − 2(12)(15)cos 50°<br>= 144 + 225 − 360 cos 50°<br>= 369 − 231.40 = 137.60<br>d = √137.60.", answer:"d ≈ <strong>11.73 km</strong>."},
    ],
    formula:"a/sin A = b/sin B · a² = b² + c² − 2bc cos A · Area = ½ab sin C",
    mistakes:["Using the cosine rule with an angle that is not between the two known sides. Fix: in a² = b² + c² − 2bc cos A, angle A sits between b and c, opposite the side a you want.","No square root at the end. Fix: write “c² = …” then “c = …”.","Working out (369 − 360) cos 50°. Fix: multiply 360 cos 50° first.","Missing the obtuse angle in the ambiguous case. Fix: always test 180° minus your answer.","Rounding mid-way. Fix: store answers in memory."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"When a Free State farm is subdivided, a land surveyor measures sides and angles, and these rules produce the diagram filed with the Surveyor-General. NSRI skippers use the cosine rule for distances between vessels."},
    activity:"The Free State field has fences of 40 m and 55 m meeting at 70°.<br>a) Find the area of the field.<br>b) Find the length of the third fence.<br>c) Find the angle opposite the 40 m fence, and the third angle.",
    activityAnswer:"a) Area = ½ × 40 × 55 × sin 70° = 1 100 × 0.9397 ≈ <strong>1 033.7 m²</strong>.<br>b) c² = 40² + 55² − 2(40)(55)cos 70° = 4 625 − 4 400 × 0.3420 ≈ 3 120.1 → c ≈ <strong>55.9 m</strong>.<br>c) Sine rule: sin A = 40 sin 70°/55.858 ≈ 0.6729 → A ≈ <strong>42.3°</strong> (the obtuse option, 137.7°, plus 70° is over 180°, so it fails). Third angle = 180° − 70° − 42.3° = <strong>67.7°</strong>.",
    examTip:"Name the rule, write it, then substitute — that earns the method mark even after a calculator slip. For proofs, draw and label the height and give a reason per line.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Sine rule",d:"a/sin A = b/sin B = c/sin C"},{w:"Cosine rule",d:"a² = b² + c² − 2bc cos A"},{w:"Included angle",d:"The angle between two given sides"},{w:"Ambiguous case",d:"The sine rule can give an acute and an obtuse angle that both fit"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"You know two sides and the angle between them. To find the third side use:",opts:["Sine rule","SOHCAHTOA","Area rule","Cosine rule"],a:3,et:"Two sides and the included angle → cosine rule. The sine rule needs a side with its opposite angle, which you don't have."},
      {q:"Area of a triangle with sides 6 and 10 and included angle 30°:",opts:["30","60","15","25.98"],a:2,et:"½ × 6 × 10 × sin 30° = 30 × ½ = 15. 25.98 comes from using cos 30° by mistake."},
      {q:"The sine rule can give two possible angles because:",opts:["sin is negative in Q2","sin θ = sin(180° − θ)","Calculators round","Triangles have 3 angles"],a:1,et:"Both θ and 180° − θ have the same sine — the ambiguous case. sin is positive, not negative, in Q2."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Drop a height and all three rules follow. Choose by what you know, test the ambiguous case, and in the exam name the rule before you substitute."},

  5: {emoji:"🏗️", subtitle:"Grade 12 · Triangles in different planes",
    illusText:"A tower, two people on the ground and lines between them make triangles in different planes. Solve them one at a time.",
    hook:{title:"How high is the cellphone mast?", body:"Two technicians stand on flat ground. A is 80 m from the foot of a tower and sees the top at 28° elevation, so the tower is <strong>≈ 42.5 m</strong> tall. B stands 60 m from the foot, at 90° to A's line. Now B's angle of elevation is <strong>≈ 35.3°</strong> and the distance AB is 100 m. Each answer came from a separate flat triangle."},
    intro:"In 3D problems, the figure contains <strong>several triangles in different planes</strong>: usually a vertical one with the tower and a horizontal one on the ground. Strategy: identify the triangle that has enough information, solve it, then use that answer in the next triangle. Grade 12 papers often use a <strong>non-right ground triangle</strong> and ask you to “show that h = …”.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🧱 Vertical or horizontal?", body:"A vertical tower TP on level ground is perpendicular to every ground line through P, so ∠TPQ = ∠TPR = 90°. The ground triangle PQR usually has <strong>no</strong> right angle, even if it looks square — 3D sketches distort angles.<br><br>Redraw each triangle flat, labelled “vertical” or “ground”. The angle of elevation always sits in the vertical triangle, at the observer."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🔗 The shared side links the triangles", body:"The side in both triangles (often PQ) carries the answer across. Plan backwards: h in △TPQ needs PQ; PQ in △PQR needs a side and its opposite angle — the sine rule.<br><br>Right angle → SOHCAHTOA; none → sine or cosine rule. Keep the shared side unrounded in memory."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🔤 “Show that” with letters", body:"Follow the same route in letters. Ground: QR = d, ∠PQR = α, ∠PRQ = β, so ∠QPR = 180° − (α + β). Sine rule: PQ/sin β = d/sin(180° − (α + β)), and sin(180° − (α + β)) = sin(α + β) (a Grade 11 reduction). So PQ = d sin β/sin(α + β).<br><br>Vertical: tan θ = h/PQ, so h = PQ tan θ = d sin β tan θ/sin(α + β). Each line must show where the next factor comes from."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Tower height from A", body:"AQ = 80 m on the ground, angle of elevation 28°.<br>Vertical △TQA, right angle at Q: h = 80 tan 28°.", answer:"h ≈ <strong>42.5 m</strong>."},
      {title:"Tower on a non-right ground triangle", body:"TP is a vertical tower. On the ground, QR = 100 m, ∠PQR = 50° and ∠PRQ = 70°. The angle of elevation of T from Q is 25°. Find TP.<br>Ground △PQR: ∠QPR = 180° − 50° − 70° = 60°.<br>Sine rule: PQ/sin 70° = 100/sin 60° → PQ = 100 sin 70°/sin 60° ≈ 108.5 m.<br>Vertical △TPQ: TP = PQ tan 25° ≈ 108.506 × 0.4663.", answer:"TP ≈ <strong>50.6 m</strong>."},
      {title:"Show that h = d sin β tan θ / sin(α + β)", body:"Same set-up in letters: QR = d, ∠PQR = α, ∠PRQ = β, elevation of T from Q = θ.<br>In △PQR: ∠QPR = 180° − (α + β) (sum of angles).<br>PQ/sin β = d/sin[180° − (α + β)] (sine rule)<br>PQ = d sin β/sin(α + β) (reduction).<br>In △TPQ: h = PQ tan θ (∠TPQ = 90°).<br>Check: d = 100, α = 50°, β = 70°, θ = 25° gives 100 × sin 70° × tan 25°/sin 120° ≈ 50.6 ✓", answer:"<strong>h = d sin β tan θ / sin(α + β)</strong>."},
    ],
    mistakes:["Assuming a ground right angle because the sketch looks square. Fix: use 90° only if given or vertical.","Rounding the shared side early. Fix: keep PQ in memory.","Putting the elevation angle in the ground triangle. Fix: it is always in the vertical triangle.","Jumping to the answer in “show that”. Fix: one line per step, each with a reason.","Writing sin(180° − (α + β)) = −sin(α + β). Fix: Q2, so sin stays positive."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Before Vodacom or MTN builds a mast, engineers check line of sight to other towers across hills — 3D trig on real terrain. Mine surveyors and drone-mapping teams split 3D shapes into flat triangles the same way."},
    activity:"From a boat A at sea, the top T of a vertical 120 m cliff TF has an angle of elevation of 15°.<br>a) How far is the boat from the foot F of the cliff?<br>b) A second boat C is 300 m from F, and ∠AFC = 60° on the sea surface. How far apart are the boats?<br>c) Find the angle of elevation of T from C.",
    activityAnswer:"a) Vertical △TFA: tan 15° = 120/AF → AF = 120/tan 15° ≈ <strong>447.8 m</strong>.<br>b) Ground △AFC (no right angle) → cosine rule: AC² = 447.85² + 300² − 2(447.85)(300)cos 60° ≈ 156 212 → AC ≈ <strong>395.2 m</strong>.<br>c) Vertical △TFC: tan θ = 120/300 = 0.4 → θ ≈ <strong>21.8°</strong>.",
    examTip:"Write the triangle (“In △PQR:”) and the rule before each step. In “show that”, marks go to each step with its reason — the given final line earns nothing alone. The numerical part is then substitution.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Plane",d:"A flat surface — e.g. the ground or a vertical wall"},{w:"Angle of elevation",d:"Angle up from the horizontal"},{w:"Shared side",d:"A side common to two triangles that links the calculation"},{w:"Perpendicular to a plane",d:"A vertical tower makes 90° with every ground line through its foot"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"TP is a vertical tower 30 m tall. From Q on level ground the angle of elevation of T is 30°. QP =",opts:["15 m","17.3 m","51.96 m","60 m"],a:2,et:"tan 30° = 30/QP → QP = 30/tan 30° = 30√3 ≈ 51.96 m. 17.3 m comes from multiplying by tan 30° instead of dividing; 60 m is the slant line TQ."},
      {q:"On level ground QR = 80 m, ∠PQR = 50° and ∠PRQ = 70°. PQ =",opts:["75.2 m","86.8 m","92.4 m","69.3 m"],a:1,et:"∠QPR = 60°, so by the sine rule PQ = 80 sin 70°/sin 60° ≈ 86.8 m. 75.2 m forgets to divide by sin 60°. Fun fact: NSC papers often hide a non-right triangle like this on the ground under the tower."},
      {q:"TP is a vertical tower; Q and R are points on level ground. Which angle is definitely 90°?",opts:["∠TPQ","∠PQR","∠TQP","∠QTP"],a:0,et:"A vertical tower is perpendicular to every line on the ground through its foot, so ∠TPQ = 90°. ∠PQR lies on the ground and is usually not a right angle."},
    ],
    conclusion:"3D trig is 2D trig done in order: separate the vertical and ground triangles, find the shared side, keep it unrounded. In “show that” questions, give a reason on every line."},
};

// ─── Module 4: Euclidean Geometry ───────────────────────────────────────────
LESSON_DB.math.m4 = {
  0: {emoji:"📏", subtitle:"Grade 8–10 · The rules every angle follows",
    illusText:"Every roof truss, bridge and burglar bar is built from straight lines and triangles. A truss with 36° base angles has 108° at the top.",
    hook:{title:"Why roofs are made of triangles", body:"Look up at the roof of an RDP house in Soshanguve: the wooden trusses are triangles. A triangle can't change shape unless a side changes length, so it's rigid. A square frame can be pushed into a diamond with no side changing — that's why a gate gets a diagonal brace."},
    intro:"Geometry is built on a few facts. <strong>Angles on a straight line</strong> add to 180°. <strong>Angles around a point</strong> add to 360°. <strong>Vertically opposite angles</strong> are equal. In any <strong>triangle</strong> the interior angles add to 180°, and an exterior angle equals the sum of the two opposite interior angles. This lesson revises the Grade 8–9 results with their reasons and shows your first written congruency proof.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"↔️ Parallel lines (and the converse)", body:"Corresponding (F-shape): equal. Alternate (Z-shape): equal. Co-interior (C/U-shape): add to 180°. These only work if the lines are parallel, so the reason names them: (alt ∠s; AB ∥ CD).<br><br>The <strong>converse</strong> runs the other way: equal alternate or corresponding angles, or co-interior angles adding to 180°, prove the lines <strong>are</strong> parallel. Reasons: (alt ∠s =), (corresp ∠s =), (co-int ∠s supp)."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🔺 Triangles and polygons", body:"Why 180°? Draw a line through the top vertex parallel to the base. The base angles reappear at the top as alternate angles, and with the top angle they make a straight line.<br><br>Isosceles: two equal sides, and the angles opposite them are equal. Equilateral: all angles 60°.<br><br>Any polygon with n sides splits into (n − 2) triangles from one corner, so its angle sum is <strong>180°(n − 2)</strong>. A quadrilateral: 180° × 2 = 360°."},
      {t:"gold", title:"✅ Congruency", body:"Triangles are congruent if: SSS, SAS (angle between the sides), ∠∠S, or RHS.<br><br>Why not SSA? Take Â = 30°, AB = 10 and BC = 6. B is 10 × sin 30° = 5 from the other arm of Â, so a 6-unit side BC can meet that arm in <strong>two</strong> places: two triangles fit the same data. SSA fails; RHS works because the right angle pins it down.<br><br>SSS is also why triangles are rigid: three fixed sides allow only one shape."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Exterior angle (easy)", body:"In △ABC, Â = 50° and B̂ = 65°. Find the exterior angle at C.", answer:"Exterior ∠ at C = Â + B̂ (ext ∠ of △)<br>= 50° + 65° = <strong>115°</strong><br>Check: interior Ĉ = 180° − 115° = 65°, and 50° + 65° + 65° = 180° ✓"},
      {title:"Parallel lines with algebra (exam standard)", body:"AB ∥ CD. A transversal makes co-interior angles 3x + 10° and 2x + 20°. Find x and both angles.", answer:"3x + 10° + 2x + 20° = 180° (co-int ∠s; AB ∥ CD)<br>5x + 30° = 180°<br>5x = 150° → x = 30°<br>Angles: 3(30°) + 10° = <strong>100°</strong> and 2(30°) + 20° = <strong>80°</strong> (check: 100° + 80° = 180° ✓)"},
      {title:"A written congruency proof (challenging)", body:"In △ABC, AB = AC and AD bisects Â, with D on BC. Prove △ABD ≡ △ACD and hence BD = DC.", answer:"In △ABD and △ACD:<br>1. AB = AC (given)<br>2. Â₁ = Â₂ (AD bisects Â)<br>3. AD = AD (common)<br>∴ △ABD ≡ △ACD (SAS)<br>∴ <strong>BD = DC</strong> (≡ △s)<br>The angle lies between the two sides, so it is SAS, not SSA."},
    ],
    formula:"Triangle: ∠ sum = 180° · Ext ∠ = sum of opposite int ∠s · Polygon ∠ sum = 180°(n − 2) · a² + b² = c²",
    mistakes:["Using parallel-line rules when the lines aren't marked parallel. A sketch is not evidence. Fix: look for the arrows or ∥ before writing (alt ∠s).","Calling SSA a congruency condition. It can give two different triangles. Fix: check the angle sits between the two sides (SAS), or use RHS.","Giving a statement without a reason. It often loses the reason mark, and where S and R share one mark, you get nothing. Fix: a reason on every line.","Writing (alt ∠s; AB ∥ CD) when you are trying to prove AB ∥ CD. Fix: use the converse reason (alt ∠s =)."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Builders on a Cosmo City site use the 3-4-5 rule to square a foundation: 3² + 4² = 25 = 5², so lines of 3 m and 4 m with a 5 m diagonal meet at exactly 90°. Welders add diagonal struts to burglar bars so gates don't sag."},
    activity:"(a) In isosceles △PQR with PQ = PR, P̂ = 40°. Find Q̂ and R̂.<br>(b) Find the exterior angle at R and check it with the exterior angle rule.<br>(c) Find each interior angle of a regular hexagon (a honeycomb cell).",
    activityAnswer:"(a) Q̂ = R̂ (∠s opp equal sides) and Q̂ + R̂ = 140° (sum of ∠s in △), so each is <strong>70°</strong>.<br>(b) Exterior ∠ at R = 180° − 70° = <strong>110°</strong> (∠s on a str line). Check: 40° + 70° = 110° ✓ (ext ∠ of △).<br>(c) Angle sum = 180°(6 − 2) = 720°. Each angle = 720° ÷ 6 = <strong>120°</strong>.",
    examTip:"Every statement needs a CAPS reason in brackets, e.g. (alt ∠s; AB ∥ CD). A statement with no reason often loses the reason mark, and where S and R share one mark, you get nothing. In congruency, list three facts, then the condition: (SAS), (SSS), (∠∠S) or (RHS).",
    keyterms:[{w:"Transversal",d:"A line crossing two or more other lines"},{w:"Congruent",d:"Identical in shape and size"},{w:"Exterior angle",d:"Angle between one side and the extension of the next"},{w:"Converse",d:"A theorem turned around, e.g. equal alternate angles prove lines parallel"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"Two angles of a triangle are 47° and 68°. The third is:",opts:["65°","75°","115°","55°"],a:0,et:"180° − 47° − 68° = 65°."},
      {q:"Alternate angles are equal when:",opts:["Always","The lines are parallel","The triangle is isosceles","They are adjacent"],a:1,et:"Alternate angles are equal only with parallel lines."},
      {q:"Which is NOT a congruency condition?",opts:["SSS","SAS","SSA","RHS"],a:2,et:"SSA doesn't guarantee congruency."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Parallel lines, triangle sums and congruency are the building blocks of every proof. In the exam, give each statement its CAPS reason and match congruent triangles vertex for vertex."},

  1: {emoji:"🔷", subtitle:"Grade 10 · Four-sided shapes and their properties",
    illusText:"Phone screens, soccer pitches, A4 paper, kites at the beach — quadrilaterals are everywhere.",
    hook:{title:"Is that frame really a rectangle?", body:"A welder in Pietermaritzburg wants to check a gate frame is a perfect rectangle. Measuring angles is hard, so he measures the <strong>diagonals</strong>: opposite sides equal and diagonals equal → rectangle. For a 2 m by 1.2 m gate each diagonal should be √(2² + 1.2²) = √5.44 ≈ 2.33 m."},
    intro:"A quadrilateral's interior angles add to 360° (it splits into two triangles). The special ones form a family: <strong>parallelogram → rectangle, rhombus → square</strong>, plus the <strong>trapezium</strong> and <strong>kite</strong>. In Grade 10 you prove their properties, meet the <strong>midpoint theorem</strong> and solve riders.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"▱ Definition vs property", body:"A <strong>definition</strong> is the minimum you need: a parallelogram is a quadrilateral with both pairs of opposite sides parallel. Everything else is a <strong>property</strong> that must be proved.<br><br>Proof that opposite sides are equal: draw diagonal AC in ∥m ABCD. Then ∠BAC = ∠DCA (alt ∠s; AB ∥ DC), ∠BCA = ∠DAC (alt ∠s; AD ∥ BC) and AC is common, so △ABC ≡ △CDA (∠∠S). Hence AB = CD and BC = DA. The other properties are proved the same way.<br><br>The family: rectangle = parallelogram + a 90° angle (adds equal diagonals). Rhombus = parallelogram + two adjacent sides equal (adds diagonals ⊥). A square is both."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🪁 Kite, trapezium and the midpoint theorem", body:"Kite: two pairs of adjacent equal sides; diagonals ⊥; the main diagonal bisects the other. Trapezium: one pair of parallel sides.<br><br><strong>Midpoint theorem</strong>: the line joining the midpoints of two sides of a triangle is parallel to the third side and half its length. Why: in △ABC, D and E are the midpoints of AB and AC. Extend DE to F with DE = EF. AE = EC (E midpt), DE = EF (construction) and ∠AED = ∠CEF (vert opp ∠s), so △ADE ≡ △CFE (SAS). So CF = AD = DB, and ∠DAE = ∠FCE gives CF ∥ DB (alt ∠s =). One pair of sides equal and parallel → DBCF is a parallelogram, so DE ∥ BC and DE = ½DF = ½BC.<br><br>Converse: a line through one midpoint, parallel to a second side, bisects the third side."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🔎 Proving a parallelogram", body:"Show any one: both pairs of opposite sides parallel (the definition) or equal; one pair equal and parallel; diagonals bisect each other; or both pairs of opposite angles equal.<br><br>Trap: “one pair of opposite sides equal, the other pair parallel” is not enough — an isosceles trapezium has that."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Angles in a parallelogram (easy)", body:"In parallelogram ABCD, Â = 3x and B̂ = x + 20°. Find x.", answer:"Â + B̂ = 180° (co-int ∠s; AD ∥ BC)<br>3x + x + 20° = 180°<br>4x = 160° → x = <strong>40°</strong><br>So Â = 120° and B̂ = 60° (check: 120° + 60° = 180° ✓)"},
      {title:"Rhombus diagonals (exam standard)", body:"A rhombus has diagonals 12 cm and 16 cm. Find a side.", answer:"Diagonals of a rhombus bisect each other at 90° (diags of rhombus ⊥)<br>Halves: 6 cm and 8 cm, forming a right-angled triangle<br>side² = 6² + 8² = 36 + 64 = 100 (Pythagoras)<br>side = <strong>10 cm</strong>"},
      {title:"A Grade 10 rider (challenging)", body:"EFGH is a parallelogram with diagonals meeting at O. M and N lie on diagonal EG so that EM = NG. Prove MFNH is a parallelogram.", answer:"Plan: show diagonals MN and FH bisect each other.<br>1. EO = OG (diags of ∥m bisect)<br>2. EM = NG (given)<br>3. MO = EO − EM = OG − NG = ON<br>4. FO = OH (diags of ∥m bisect)<br>∴ <strong>MFNH is a parallelogram</strong> (diags bisect each other)"},
    ],
    mistakes:["Assuming a shape is a rectangle because it looks like one. Fix: prove a parallelogram, then one 90° angle or equal diagonals.","Saying a rhombus has equal diagonals. Among parallelograms, only rectangles (and squares) do. Fix: learn the family table, not a picture.","Forgetting that every square is a rectangle and a rhombus. A parent's properties belong to all its children.","Claiming “ABCD is a ∥m because AB = DC” with one pair. Fix: one pair must be equal <em>and</em> parallel.","Writing the midpoint theorem as “DE = BC”. Fix: DE = ½BC (midpt theorem)."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Paving contractors in Soweto check their string lines are rectangles with the equal-diagonal test. Folding security gates are parallelograms: opposite sides stay equal and parallel while the angles change."},
    activity:"KITE is a kite with KI = KE and TI = TE. Its diagonals meet at M (K̂₁ = ∠IKM, K̂₂ = ∠EKM).<br>(a) Prove △KIT ≡ △KET.<br>(b) Hence prove IM = ME.<br>(c) Prove that IE ⊥ KT (M̂₁ = ∠KMI, M̂₂ = ∠KME).",
    activityAnswer:"(a) KI = KE (given); TI = TE (given); KT common → △KIT ≡ △KET (SSS). So K̂₁ = K̂₂ (≡ △s).<br>(b) In △KIM and △KEM: KI = KE (given); K̂₁ = K̂₂ (proved); KM common → △KIM ≡ △KEM (SAS) → <strong>IM = ME</strong> (≡ △s).<br>(c) M̂₁ = M̂₂ (≡ △s) and M̂₁ + M̂₂ = 180° (∠s on a str line) → each 90°, so <strong>IE ⊥ KT</strong>.",
    examTip:"To name a special quadrilateral, prove it is a parallelogram first, then add the extra property (equal diagonals → rectangle; equal adjacent sides → rhombus). Riders score per correct statement and reason.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Bisect",d:"Cut into two equal parts"},{w:"Parallelogram",d:"Both pairs of opposite sides parallel"},{w:"Rhombus",d:"Parallelogram with all sides equal"},{w:"Midpoint theorem",d:"The line joining two midpoints of a △ is parallel to and half the third side"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"Diagonals of a rhombus:",opts:["Are equal","Bisect at 90°","Are parallel","Don't bisect"],a:1,et:"A rhombus's diagonals bisect each other at right angles."},
      {q:"Interior angles of any quadrilateral add to:",opts:["180°","270°","360°","540°"],a:2,et:"A quadrilateral splits into two triangles: 2 × 180° = 360°."},
      {q:"A parallelogram with a 90° angle is a:",opts:["Rhombus","Kite","Rectangle","Trapezium"],a:2,et:"One right angle forces all four to be 90° → rectangle."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Each special quadrilateral is a parallelogram plus something extra. In the exam, prove “parallelogram” first, then the extra property, with CAPS reasons."},

  2: {emoji:"⭕", subtitle:"Grade 11 · Angles inside circles",
    illusText:"Wheels, clocks, the centre circle at FNB Stadium — circles hide angle rules that never change.",
    hook:{title:"The best angle to shoot from", body:"At FNB Stadium, stand anywhere on a circle through both goalposts and you see the goal at the <strong>same angle</strong> — that's “angles in the same segment are equal”. Coaches and sports analysts can use this to find the widest shooting angle."},
    intro:"A <strong>chord</strong> joins two points on a circle; a <strong>cyclic quadrilateral</strong> has all four vertices on the circle. Some proofs are bookwork: the DBE Examination Guidelines list which may be asked (we understand these include the perpendicular-from-centre, angle-at-centre and cyclic-quad theorems — check your year's guidelines). Converses are used as reasons in riders, not proved.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🎯 The centre: perpendicular and double", body:"(line from centre ⊥ to chord): the line from the centre perpendicular to a chord bisects the chord. Proof: join the centre to both ends; the two right-angled triangles are congruent (RHS). Converse: the perpendicular bisector of a chord passes through the centre.<br><br>(∠ at centre = 2 × ∠ at circumference): proof, for the case where O lies inside ∠APB. Arc AB subtends ∠AOB at centre O and ∠APB at P. Join PO and extend to Q. Let P̂₁ = ∠APO, P̂₂ = ∠BPO, Ô₁ = ∠AOQ, Ô₂ = ∠BOQ.<br>OA = OP (radii) → ∠OAP = P̂₁ (∠s opp equal sides)<br>Ô₁ = ∠OAP + P̂₁ = 2P̂₁ (ext ∠ of △)<br>Likewise (OB = OP), Ô₂ = 2P̂₂, so ∠AOB = 2∠APB.<br><br>Special case: a diameter makes 180° at the centre, so the ∠ in a semicircle is 90°."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🔁 Same segment", body:"(∠s in the same seg): angles subtended by the same arc or chord, on the same side of it, are equal. Why: each one is half the same angle at the centre.<br><br>Trap: angles on <strong>opposite</strong> sides of the chord are not equal — they add to 180°."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🔲 Cyclic quadrilaterals and converses", body:"(opp ∠s of cyclic quad): opposite angles add to 180°. Proof (ABCD cyclic, centre O): join O to B and D; let Ô₁ be ∠BOD facing C and Ô₂ the ∠BOD facing A.<br>Ô₁ = 2Â and Ô₂ = 2Ĉ (∠ at centre = 2 × ∠ at circumference)<br>Ô₁ + Ô₂ = 360° (∠s round a pt), so Â + Ĉ = 180°.<br><br>(ext ∠ of cyclic quad): an exterior angle equals the interior opposite angle.<br><br>To <strong>prove</strong> a quadrilateral is cyclic, use a converse: opposite angles supplementary (converse opp ∠s of cyclic quad), exterior angle = interior opposite angle (converse ext ∠ of cyclic quad), or a side subtending equal angles on the same side (converse ∠s in the same seg)."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Centre angle (easy)", body:"Angle at the circumference is 35°. Find the angle at the centre on the same arc.", answer:"∠ at centre = 2 × 35° (∠ at centre = 2 × ∠ at circumference)<br>= <strong>70°</strong>"},
      {title:"Cyclic quad (exam standard)", body:"In cyclic quad ABCD, Â = 3x and Ĉ = x + 40°. Find Â and Ĉ.", answer:"Â + Ĉ = 180° (opp ∠s of cyclic quad)<br>3x + x + 40° = 180°<br>4x = 140° → x = 35°<br>Â = <strong>105°</strong>, Ĉ = <strong>75°</strong>."},
      {title:"Two chords (challenging, CAPS-style)", body:"Chord AB = 50 mm is 40 mm from centre O (OM ⊥ AB). Chord CD is 20 mm from O (ON ⊥ CD). Find the radius and CD.", answer:"AM = 25 mm (line from centre ⊥ to chord)<br>r² = 25² + 40² = 625 + 1600 = 2225 (Pythagoras)<br>r = √2225 ≈ <strong>47.2 mm</strong><br>CN² = r² − ON² = 2225 − 400 = 1825<br>CN = √1825 ≈ 42.72 mm<br>CD = 2 × CN ≈ <strong>85.4 mm</strong> (line from centre ⊥ to chord)"},
    ],
    formula:"∠ at centre = 2 × ∠ at circumference · ∠ in semicircle = 90° · opp ∠s of cyclic quad = 180°",
    mistakes:["Using the centre-angle theorem when the angles stand on different arcs. Fix: trace both angles to the same two endpoints.","Calling a quadrilateral cyclic when one vertex is the centre. All four vertices must lie on the circle.","Writing (line from centre ⊥ to chord) when only the midpoint was given. Fix: the reason is (line from centre to midpt of chord).","Using a theorem when you need its converse. To prove ABCD cyclic, write (converse opp ∠s of cyclic quad)."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Archaeologists rebuild a clay pot from one curved shard: the perpendicular bisectors of two chords cross at the centre."},
    activity:"AB is a diameter of a circle with centre O, and C is on the circumference. ∠CAB = 28°.<br>(a) Find ∠CBA.<br>(b) Find ∠COB.<br>(c) A chord is 8 cm from the centre of a circle of radius 10 cm. Find its length.",
    activityAnswer:"(a) ∠ACB = 90° (∠ in semi-circle). ∠CBA = 180° − 90° − 28° = <strong>62°</strong> (sum of ∠s in △).<br>(b) ∠COB and ∠CAB stand on arc CB, so ∠COB = 2 × 28° = <strong>56°</strong> (∠ at centre = 2 × ∠ at circumference).<br>(c) Half chord = √(10² − 8²) = √36 = 6 cm, so chord = <strong>12 cm</strong> (line from centre ⊥ to chord).",
    examTip:"Learn the exact CAPS reason wording, e.g. (∠ at centre = 2 × ∠ at circumference). A correct angle with a wrong reason often loses the reason mark, and where S and R share one mark, you get nothing. In bookwork proofs, draw and label the construction (join PO, extend to Q) — it carries a mark.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Chord",d:"Line segment joining two points on a circle"},{w:"Subtend",d:"An arc or chord “creates” an angle at a point"},{w:"Cyclic quadrilateral",d:"Quadrilateral with all vertices on a circle"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"The angle in a semicircle is:",opts:["45°","60°","180°","90°"],a:3,et:"A diameter makes 180° at the centre, so the angle at the circumference is half of that: 90°."},
      {q:"Opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral:",opts:["Are equal","Add to 180°","Add to 360°","Add to 90°"],a:1,et:"They are supplementary. They are equal only in special cases, such as a rectangle."},
      {q:"Centre angle is 140°. Circumference angle on the same arc:",opts:["70°","280°","140°","40°"],a:0,et:"Half of 140° = 70°. 280° comes from doubling instead of halving."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Double at the centre, equal in the same segment, supplementary in cyclic quads. In the exam, learn the bookwork proofs and the reasons word-for-word."},

  3: {emoji:"🛞", subtitle:"Grade 11 · Lines that just touch a circle",
    illusText:"A bicycle chain leaving the gear, a car tyre on the road — a tangent touches a circle at exactly one point, and the radius meets it at 90°.",
    hook:{title:"The tyre and the road", body:"A car tyre touches the road at one point. The road is a <strong>tangent</strong> to the wheel, and the spoke (radius) to that point is always at <strong>90°</strong> to the road. Engineers on roads like Chapman's Peak Drive use tangent rules so each straight runs smoothly into a bend."},
    intro:"A <strong>tangent</strong> touches a circle at exactly one point. Key facts: a tangent is <strong>perpendicular to the radius</strong> at the point of contact; two tangents from the same outside point are <strong>equal in length</strong>; and the <strong>tan-chord theorem</strong>: the angle between a tangent and a chord equals the angle in the alternate segment. We believe the tan-chord proof is examinable — check your year's DBE Examination Guidelines.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"⊥ Tangent ⊥ radius, and equal tangents", body:"(tan ⊥ radius): wherever a radius meets a tangent there is a 90° angle — ready for Pythagoras or trigonometry.<br><br>Why tangents from one point are equal: from outside point P draw tangents PA and PB to a circle with centre O. In △OAP and △OBP: OA = OB (radii), ∠OAP = ∠OBP = 90° (tan ⊥ radius), OP common. So △OAP ≡ △OBP (RHS) and PA = PB (tans from common pt). OAPB is a kite.<br><br>Converse (to prove a line IS a tangent): a line perpendicular to a radius at its endpoint on the circle is a tangent (converse tan ⊥ radius)."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🔁 Tan-chord theorem and its proof", body:"The angle between a tangent and a chord at the point of contact equals the angle the chord subtends in the <strong>alternate segment</strong> (tan-chord theorem).<br><br>Proof: tangent TA at A, chord AB, C on the far arc. Draw diameter AD and join DB. Let ∠BAT = x.<br>∠DAT = 90° (tan ⊥ radius) → ∠DAB = 90° − x<br>∠ABD = 90° (∠ in semi-circle)<br>∠ADB = 180° − 90° − (90° − x) = x (sum of ∠s in △)<br>∠ACB = ∠ADB = x (∠s in the same seg)."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🔎 Spotting the alternate segment", body:"Put your finger on the tangent angle. Its chord splits the circle in two; the alternate segment is the piece on the <strong>other</strong> side of the chord, where the matching angle stands on that same chord.<br><br>Converse: if the angle between a line and a chord equals the angle in the alternate segment, the line is a tangent (converse tan-chord theorem). Riders often combine this with the cyclic-quad theorems from the last lesson."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Tangent length (easy)", body:"A circle has radius 5 cm. P is 13 cm from the centre. Find the tangent length from P.", answer:"Radius ⊥ tangent (tan ⊥ radius), so the triangle is right-angled<br>t² = 13² − 5² = 169 − 25 = 144<br>t = <strong>12 cm</strong>"},
      {title:"Equal tangents (exam standard)", body:"PA and PB are tangents and ∠APB = 50°. Find ∠PAB.", answer:"PA = PB (tans from common pt)<br>∠PAB = ∠PBA (∠s opp equal sides)<br>∠PAB = (180° − 50°) ÷ 2 (sum of ∠s in △)<br>= <strong>65°</strong>"},
      {title:"Prove a tangent (challenging rider)", body:"A, B and C lie on a circle. P lies on CB extended beyond B. ∠APC = 30°, ∠ABC = 70° and ∠ACB = 40°. Prove that PA is a tangent to the circle.", answer:"Plan: show ∠PAB = ∠ACB.<br>1. ∠ABP = 180° − 70° = 110° (∠s on a str line)<br>2. ∠PAB = 180° − 30° − 110° = 40° (sum of ∠s in △)<br>3. ∠ACB = 40° (given)<br>4. ∠PAB = ∠ACB<br>∴ <strong>PA is a tangent</strong> (converse tan-chord theorem)"},
    ],
    formula:"tangent ⊥ radius · tangents from same point are equal · ∠ between tangent & chord = ∠ in alt segment",
    mistakes:["Choosing the angle in the wrong segment. Fix: the matching angle is across the chord, standing on that same chord.","Forgetting the 90° between radius and tangent. Fix: mark it the moment you see a tangent and a centre.","Assuming a line is a tangent without it being given or proved. Fix: prove it with a converse.","Mixing up the chord: a tangent at A makes different angles with AB and AC. Fix: name the chord you use.","Writing (tan-chord theorem) to prove a tangent. Fix: (converse tan-chord theorem)."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Mine conveyor belts in Mpumalanga run as tangents between pulleys; belt length = tangent lengths plus arcs. Transnet engineers join straight track to curves along a tangent so trains don't jerk."},
    activity:"(a) Two tangents from T touch a circle at A and B. TA = 3x − 2 and TB = x + 8. Find x and TA.<br>(b) ABCD is a cyclic quad and TA is a tangent at A, with ∠TAB = 70° and D in the alternate segment. Find ∠ADB.<br>(c) If also ∠ABD = 45°, find ∠BCD.",
    activityAnswer:"(a) 3x − 2 = x + 8 (tans from common pt) → 2x = 10 → x = <strong>5</strong>, TA = 3(5) − 2 = <strong>13</strong>.<br>(b) ∠ADB = ∠TAB = <strong>70°</strong> (tan-chord theorem).<br>(c) In △ABD: ∠BAD = 180° − 70° − 45° = 65° (sum of ∠s in △). ∠BCD = 180° − 65° = <strong>115°</strong> (opp ∠s of cyclic quad).",
    examTip:"When a tangent appears in a diagram, immediately mark the 90° with the radius and look for the tan-chord angle. Most marks come from these two facts. To prove a tangent, the last line must use a converse.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Tangent",d:"Line touching a circle at exactly one point"},{w:"Point of contact",d:"Where the tangent touches the circle"},{w:"Alternate segment",d:"The segment on the other side of the chord from the angle"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"The angle between a radius and a tangent at the point of contact is:",opts:["45°","60°","90°","180°"],a:2,et:"Tangent ⊥ radius."},
      {q:"Two tangents from an external point are:",opts:["Parallel","Equal in length","Perpendicular","Different lengths"],a:1,et:"Tangents from the same point are equal."},
      {q:"Radius 6, distance from centre to P is 10. Tangent length:",opts:["4","√136","16","8"],a:3,et:"√(10² − 6²) = √64 = 8. √136 comes from adding the squares instead of subtracting — the distance to the centre is the hypotenuse."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Tangents give right angles and equal lengths for free, and tan-chord links them to the alternate segment. In the exam, mark the 90° first and prove tangents with a converse."},

  4: {emoji:"🌳", subtitle:"Grade 12 · Same shape, different size",
    illusText:"You can measure a tree without climbing it — just compare shadows. That's similar triangles.",
    hook:{title:"Measure a tree with a shadow", body:"At 3 pm in Kirstenbosch, a 1.6 m learner casts a 2 m shadow. At the same moment a tree casts a 15 m shadow. The sun's rays make two <strong>similar triangles</strong>: tree height = 1.6 × 15 ÷ 2 = <strong>12 m</strong>."},
    intro:"<strong>Similar</strong> figures have the same shape: corresponding angles are equal and corresponding sides are in the same <strong>ratio</strong>. The <strong>proportionality theorem</strong>: a line parallel to one side of a triangle divides the other two sides in the same ratio. The midpoint theorem is its special case (ratio 1 : 1). The DBE Examination Guidelines list the proportionality and equiangular-triangles proofs as examinable (check your year's guidelines).",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"↔️ Proportionality theorem: the area proof", body:"In △ABC, DE ∥ BC with D on AB and E on AC. Prove AD/DB = AE/EC.<br>Join BE and CD, and use the fact that triangles with the same height have areas in the ratio of their bases.<br>area △ADE ÷ area △BDE = AD/DB (same height from E)<br>area △ADE ÷ area △CED = AE/EC (same height from D)<br>area △BDE = area △CED (same base DE, between the parallels DE ∥ BC)<br>So AD/DB = AE/EC (equal areas in the ratios above). In riders, quote this result as (line ∥ one side of △).<br><br>Trap: AD/DB is part-to-part. DE/BC is not equal to AD/DB — it equals AD/AB, from similar triangles."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🔺 Proving similarity", body:"Equiangular triangles are similar (|||; ∠∠∠). Two angles are enough: the third is forced by the 180° sum.<br><br>Proof outline (Â = D̂, B̂ = Ê, Ĉ = F̂): mark G on AB with AG = DE and H on AC with AH = DF.<br>△AGH ≡ △DEF (SAS) → ∠AGH = Ê = B̂<br>GH ∥ BC (corresp ∠s =)<br>AG/AB = AH/AC (line ∥ one side of △), so DE/AB = DF/AC. Similarly, DE/AB = EF/BC.<br><br>The converse: triangles whose sides are in the same ratio are similar (sides of △ in prop).<br><br>Write the letters in matching order: △ABC ||| △DEF means A↔D, B↔E, C↔F, so AB/DE = BC/EF = AC/DF."},
      {t:"gold", title:"📐 Pythagoras by similar triangles", body:"In △ABC with Â = 90°, draw AD ⊥ BC. Each small triangle is similar to the big one: △ABD ||| △CBA (B̂ common, 90° each). So AB/CB = BD/BA, giving <strong>AB² = BD · BC</strong>. Likewise AC² = DC · BC and AD² = BD · DC.<br><br>Add the first two: AB² + AC² = BC(BD + DC) = BC · BC = BC². That is Pythagoras, proved with similarity."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"The tree (easy)", body:"Learner 1.6 m, shadow 2 m. Tree shadow 15 m. Find the tree's height h.", answer:"The triangles are equiangular (sun's rays parallel, both upright) → similar<br>h/1.6 = 15/2<br>h = 1.6 × 7.5 = <strong>12 m</strong>"},
      {title:"Proportionality theorem (exam standard)", body:"DE ∥ BC, AD = 6, DB = 4, AE = 9. Find EC.", answer:"AD/DB = AE/EC (line ∥ one side of △)<br>6/4 = 9/EC<br>6 × EC = 36<br>EC = <strong>6</strong>"},
      {title:"Similarity in a right triangle (challenging)", body:"In △ABC, Â = 90° and AD ⊥ BC with BD = 4 and DC = 9. Find AD, AB² and AC², and check Pythagoras.", answer:"BC = 4 + 9 = 13<br>△ABD ||| △CAD (each has 90°, and ∠BAD = Ĉ, both = 90° − B̂), so AD² = BD · DC = 4 × 9 = 36 → AD = <strong>6</strong><br>AB² = BD · BC = 4 × 13 = <strong>52</strong><br>AC² = DC · BC = 9 × 13 = <strong>117</strong><br>Check: 52 + 117 = 169 = 13² = BC² ✓"},
    ],
    formula:"Similar △s: AB/DE = BC/EF = AC/DF · DE ∥ BC ⇒ AD/DB = AE/EC",
    mistakes:["Writing the triangles in the wrong order. Fix: list the equal angles first, then name both triangles in that order.","Using the proportionality theorem without a parallel line. Fix: DE ∥ BC must be given or proved.","Mixing AD/DB with AD/AB. Fix: part-to-part for the theorem; part-to-whole when comparing DE with BC.","Proving similar with only two sides in proportion. Fix: all three sides, or the angles."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"On a 1 : 200 000 map, towns 4.5 cm apart are 4.5 × 200 000 = 900 000 cm = 9 km apart. Every map is a similar figure."},
    activity:"(a) A 2 m pole casts a 3.2 m shadow. A building casts a 24 m shadow at the same time. How tall is the building?<br>(b) D and E are the midpoints of AB and AC, and DE = 7.5 cm. Find BC.<br>(c) In △ABC, DE ∥ BC, AD = 3, DB = 5, AC = 16 and BC = 24. Find AE and DE.",
    activityAnswer:"(a) h/2 = 24/3.2 = 7.5 → h = <strong>15 m</strong>.<br>(b) DE = ½BC (midpt theorem) → BC = <strong>15 cm</strong>.<br>(c) AE/EC = 3/5 (line ∥ one side of △), so AE = 3/8 × 16 = <strong>6</strong> (and EC = 10). △ADE ||| △ABC (∠∠∠), so DE/BC = AD/AB = 3/8 → DE = 3/8 × 24 = <strong>9</strong>.",
    examTip:"When proving triangles similar, list the three pairs of equal angles with reasons, then write △___ ||| △___ (∠∠∠) with the letters in matching order. Learn the proportionality proof as bookwork: the construction (join BE and CD) and the area reasons each carry marks.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Similar",d:"Same shape; equal angles, proportional sides"},{w:"Ratio",d:"Comparison of two quantities, e.g. 3 : 2"},{w:"Midpoint theorem",d:"Line joining midpoints is parallel to and half the third side"},{w:"Proportionality theorem",d:"A line parallel to one side of a △ divides the other two sides in the same ratio"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"D and E are midpoints of AB and AC. BC = 18. DE =",opts:["36","18","6","9"],a:3,et:"Midpoint theorem: DE = ½BC = 9. 36 comes from doubling instead of halving."},
      {q:"Similar triangles must have:",opts:["Equal sides","Equal angles","Equal areas","Equal perimeters"],a:1,et:"Equal angles and proportional (not equal) sides."},
      {q:"DE ∥ BC, AD = 4, DB = 2, AE = 6. EC =",opts:["2","12","3","4"],a:2,et:"4/2 = 6/EC → EC = 3. 12 comes from flipping one ratio (6 × 2)."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Parallel lines split sides in proportion, equiangular triangles are similar, and similarity proves Pythagoras. In the exam, learn the bookwork proofs and match the letters carefully."},

  5: {emoji:"🧠", subtitle:"Grade 10–12 · Thinking like a lawyer",
    illusText:"A proof is an argument where every step is backed by evidence. That's exactly how lawyers, scientists and coders think.",
    hook:{title:"Building an argument like a lawyer", body:"In the Johannesburg High Court, an advocate can't just say “my client is innocent”. Each claim needs evidence. Geometry riders work the same way: every statement needs a <strong>reason</strong> (a theorem or a given fact)."},
    intro:"A <strong>rider</strong> is an unfamiliar problem you solve using known theorems. A <strong>proof</strong> is a chain of statements, each with a reason, from the given to what you must prove. Grade 10 riders use parallelograms, Grade 11 circles and Grade 12 similarity. In the NSC, riders are where top marks are won.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🗺️ Read and mark the diagram", body:"Mark all given information on the diagram: equal sides, parallel lines, tangents, right angles. Then ask what each piece unlocks: tangent → 90° with the radius, and tan-chord; parallel lines → alternate, corresponding and co-interior angles; radii → isosceles triangles; a diameter → 90° in a semicircle.<br><br>Name angles so nobody can misread them. Where several angles meet at B, number them B̂₁, B̂₂, B̂₃ (as on the exam diagram), or use three letters, ∠ABD."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🔄 Work backwards", body:"Ask: what do I need to prove this? To prove ABCD is cyclic you need one of the converses: opposite angles add to 180° (converse opp ∠s of cyclic quad), an exterior angle equals the interior opposite angle (converse ext ∠ of cyclic quad), or AB subtends equal angles at C and D on the same side (converse ∠s in the same seg).<br><br>To prove lines parallel you need equal alternate or corresponding angles, or supplementary co-interior angles — e.g. B̂₁ = D̂₂ as alternate angles gives AB ∥ CD (alt ∠s =). To prove a tangent you need the converse tan-chord or a 90° with a radius."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🧾 Statement | Reason", body:"Write in two columns or with reasons in brackets. One statement per line; one reason per statement. Use only givens, earlier statements and accepted theorems.<br><br>Never use the thing you must prove as a reason — that is circular, like a witness vouching for themselves. And never use “it looks like” or a protractor reading: exam diagrams are not drawn to scale."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Use an isosceles triangle (easy)", body:"O is the centre; OA and OB are radii; ∠AOB = 100°. Find ∠OAB.", answer:"OA = OB (radii)<br>∠OAB = ∠OBA (∠s opp equal sides)<br>∠OAB = (180° − 100°) ÷ 2 (sum of ∠s in △)<br>= <strong>40°</strong>"},
      {title:"Parallel lines prove a cyclic quad (exam standard)", body:"In quadrilateral ABCD, AD ∥ BC, ∠ABC = 110° and ∠BCD = 110°. BC is extended to E. Prove ABCD is cyclic.", answer:"1. Â + ∠ABC = 180° (co-int ∠s; AD ∥ BC) → Â = 70°<br>2. ∠DCE = 180° − 110° = 70° (∠s on a str line)<br>3. ∠DCE = Â<br>∴ <strong>ABCD is cyclic</strong> (converse ext ∠ of cyclic quad)<br>Alternative: Â + ∠BCD = 70° + 110° = 180° (converse opp ∠s of cyclic quad)."},
      {title:"A tangent rider (challenging)", body:"A, B and C lie on a circle. TA is the tangent at A, with T on the opposite side of AB from C. D is on AB and E is on AC with DE ∥ TA. Let Â₁ = ∠TAB and D̂₁ = ∠ADE. Prove BCED is cyclic.", answer:"Plan: show the exterior angle D̂₁ of BCED equals the interior opposite angle Ĉ.<br>1. Â₁ = Ĉ (tan-chord theorem)<br>2. D̂₁ = Â₁ (alt ∠s; DE ∥ TA)<br>3. D̂₁ = Ĉ (both equal Â₁)<br>4. D̂₁ is the exterior angle of BCED at D, and Ĉ is the interior opposite angle<br>∴ <strong>BCED is cyclic</strong> (converse ext ∠ of cyclic quad)"},
    ],
    mistakes:["Using what you must prove as a reason. Fix: you may not use a cyclic-quad theorem on ABCD until you've proved it cyclic.","Reading values off the diagram (“it looks like 90°”). Fix: only givens and proved facts count.","Writing reasons in your own words. Fix: learn the CAPS list, e.g. (tan-chord theorem).","Confusing a theorem with its converse. Fix: use the converse when concluding “cyclic”, “parallel” or “tangent”.","Naming an angle just “B̂” where three angles meet. Fix: B̂₂ or ∠ABD."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Law students at UCT and Wits build arguments step by step, like a rider. Software developers debug by working backwards: “what must be true for this line to run?” Doctors diagnose from evidence the same way."},
    activity:"In △ABC, AB = AC and D is the midpoint of BC.<br>(a) Prove △ABD ≡ △ACD.<br>(b) Hence prove AD ⊥ BC.<br>(c) If B̂ = 52°, find ∠BAD.",
    activityAnswer:"(a) AB = AC (given); BD = DC (D is the midpoint); AD common → △ABD ≡ △ACD (SSS).<br>(b) ∠ADB = ∠ADC (≡ △s). They add to 180° (∠s on a str line), so each is 90° → <strong>AD ⊥ BC</strong>.<br>(c) In △ABD: ∠BAD = 180° − 90° − 52° = <strong>38°</strong> (sum of ∠s in △). Check: Ĉ = 52° too, so Â = 76° and ∠BAD = ½ × 76° = 38° ✓",
    examTip:"Even if you can't finish a rider, write every correct statement and reason you can find. Marks are awarded per correct step. Mark the diagram first, write your plan (“I need ext ∠ = int opp ∠”), then the proof. If a later part says “hence”, use the result just proved.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Rider",d:"A non-routine problem solved with known theorems"},{w:"Converse",d:"A theorem reversed, e.g. used to prove lines parallel or a quad cyclic"},{w:"Given",d:"Information you may use without proof"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"In △PQR you have proved PQ = PR. The next statement, Q̂ = R̂, needs which reason?",opts:["∠s opp equal sides","sides opp equal ∠s","It looks symmetric","SAS"],a:0,et:"The angles sit opposite the equal sides, so the reason is (∠s opp equal sides). “Sides opp equal ∠s” is the converse — used when you know the angles and want the sides."},
      {q:"To prove ABCD cyclic you may show:",opts:["AB = CD","Opp ∠s add to 180°","Diagonals are equal","AB ∥ CD"],a:1,et:"Opposite angles supplementary is a converse of the cyclic quad theorem."},
      {q:"OA and OB are radii. △OAB is:",opts:["Right-angled","Equilateral always","Scalene","Isosceles"],a:3,et:"Two equal sides (radii) → isosceles. It is only equilateral if AB also equals the radius."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Proofs are about evidence. Mark the diagram, work backwards from the goal, and back every statement with a theorem or its converse. In the exam, write every step you can — each earns marks."},
};

// ─── Module 5: Statistics & Probability ─────────────────────────────────────
LESSON_DB.math.m5 = {
  0: {emoji:"📊", subtitle:"Grade 10 · Mean, median and mode — and when each one lies",
    illusText:"“The average salary at our company is R27 917!” Is that true? Is it honest? Statistics decides.",
    hook:{title:"The misleading average", body:"A small Pietermaritzburg business has 5 workers earning R8 000, R9 000, R9 500, R10 000 and R11 000, and the owner earns R120 000. The <strong>mean</strong> salary is <strong>R27 917</strong> — but nobody except the boss earns close to that. The <strong>median</strong> is <strong>R9 750</strong>, a much fairer picture. Knowing which average to use protects you from being misled by adverts, politicians and employers."},
    intro:"<strong>Central tendency</strong> (mean, median, mode) describes the middle of data; <strong>dispersion</strong> (range, quartiles, IQR) describes the spread. Then come <strong>grouped data</strong>, <strong>percentiles</strong> and <strong>box plots</strong>.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"⚖️ Which average?", body:"The <strong>mean</strong> (sum ÷ n) uses every value, so one huge value drags it: the owner’s salary lifts it to R27 917. The <strong>median</strong> only cares about position — it stays R9 750 however much the owner earns.<br><br>Use the median for skewed data (incomes, Umhlanga house prices), the mean for roughly symmetric data, and the <strong>mode</strong> for categories (the busiest route at Bree taxi rank).<br><br>Trap: sort the data first. The median is at position (n + 1)/2; for even n, average the two middle values."},
      {t:"teal", title:"📦 Quartiles, percentiles, box plots", body:"Q₁ is the median of the lower half and Q₃ of the upper half (for odd n, leave the median out of both halves). <strong>Percentiles</strong> split data into 100: Q₁ is the 25th percentile, Q₃ the 75th.<br><br><strong>IQR</strong> = Q₃ − Q₁ (the middle 50%) and <strong>semi-IQR</strong> = ½(Q₃ − Q₁). The <strong>five-number summary</strong> (min; Q₁; median; Q₃; max) gives the <strong>box-and-whisker plot</strong>.<br><br>Skewness (a Grade 11 link): if the median is closer to Q₁ and the right whisker is long, the data is <strong>positively skewed</strong>."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🚨 Grouped data and outliers", body:"In a class like 40 ≤ x < 50 the actual marks are unknown, so each is taken at the <strong>midpoint</strong> (45) and the mean is Σ(f × x)/n. That is why it is only an <strong>estimated mean</strong>. Instead of an exact median or mode, give the <strong>modal class</strong> and the <strong>class containing the median</strong> (use running totals).<br><br><strong>Outliers</strong> lie far from the rest. A common convention (enrichment, not CAPS wording): below Q₁ − 1.5 × IQR or above Q₃ + 1.5 × IQR."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Salaries", body:"Mean and median of R8 000, R9 000, R9 500, R10 000, R11 000, R120 000?", answer:"Sum = 167 500, so mean = 167 500 ÷ 6 ≈ R27 917.<br>n = 6: median = (9 500 + 10 000) ÷ 2 = R9 750.<br><strong>Mean ≈ R27 917, median = R9 750</strong> — the median is fairer because the outlier inflates the mean."},
      {title:"Test marks and a box plot", body:"A Polokwane class scored 45, 52, 58, 61, 61, 67, 73, 80, 88. Give the five-number summary, IQR and semi-IQR, and comment on skewness.", answer:"Median = 5th value = 61.<br>Q₁ = (52 + 58) ÷ 2 = 55; Q₃ = (73 + 80) ÷ 2 = 76.5.<br>Summary: <strong>45; 55; 61; 76.5; 88</strong>.<br>IQR = 21.5; semi-IQR = <strong>10.75</strong>.<br>Q₃ − median = 15.5 > median − Q₁ = 6 → <strong>positively skewed</strong> (mean 65 > median 61 agrees)."},
      {title:"Grouped data", body:"Marks of 200 learners: 0 ≤ x < 20: 4 · 20 ≤ x < 30: 10 · 30 ≤ x < 40: 37 · 40 ≤ x < 50: 43 · 50 ≤ x < 60: 36 · 60 ≤ x < 70: 26 · 70 ≤ x < 80: 24 · 80 ≤ x ≤ 100: 20. Estimate the mean; find the modal class and the classes holding Q₁, the median and Q₃.", answer:"Midpoints 10, 25, 35, 45, 55, 65, 75, 90.<br>Σ(f × x) = 10 790, so mean ≈ 10 790 ÷ 200 = <strong>53.95%</strong>.<br>Modal class: <strong>40 ≤ x < 50</strong>.<br>Running totals: 4, 14, 51, 94, 130, 156, 180, 200.<br>Median (100th): <strong>50 ≤ x < 60</strong>. Q₁ (50th): <strong>30 ≤ x < 40</strong>. Q₃ (150th): <strong>60 ≤ x < 70</strong>."},
    ],
    formula:"Mean x̄ = Σx/n · Grouped: x̄ ≈ Σ(f × x)/n · IQR = Q₃ − Q₁ · Semi-IQR = ½(Q₃ − Q₁) · Range = max − min",
    mistakes:["Finding the median before sorting. Fix: order the data first.","Always trusting the mean. Fix: check for outliers or skew, then prefer the median.","Using a class boundary instead of the midpoint in Σ(f × x). Fix: midpoint = (lower + upper) ÷ 2.","Giving a grouped median as an exact number. Fix: name the interval, e.g. 50 ≤ x < 60."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Stats SA often reports <strong>median</strong> household income, because a few very rich households would inflate the mean. Bank analysts and district officials compare branches and schools’ matric results with box plots."},
    activity:"Six friends’ monthly data use (GB): 2, 3, 3, 4, 5, 25.<br>(a) Find the mean, median and mode.<br>(b) Give the five-number summary and IQR.<br>(c) Is 25 an outlier (1.5 × IQR)? Which average is typical?",
    activityAnswer:"(a) Mean = 42 ÷ 6 = 7; median = 3.5; mode = 3.<br>(b) 2; 3; 3.5; 5; 25. IQR = 2.<br>(c) 5 + 1.5 × 2 = 8 < 25: yes. The median — the outlier drags the mean to 7.",
    examTip:"For “which measure is more appropriate?”, the mark is for the reason: “the median, because the outlier inflates the mean.” Write “estimated mean” for grouped data and show the f × x column — it earns method marks.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Median",d:"Middle value of ordered data"},{w:"Interquartile range",d:"Q₃ − Q₁ — spread of the middle 50%"},{w:"Outlier",d:"A value far from the rest of the data"},{w:"Modal class",d:"The class interval with the highest frequency"},{w:"Five-number summary",d:"Minimum, Q₁, median, Q₃, maximum"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"Median of 3, 9, 4, 7, 5:",opts:["4","5","7","5.6"],a:1,et:"Order first: 3, 4, 5, 7, 9 → middle value 5. Choosing 4 means you took the middle of the unsorted list; 5.6 is the mean."},
      {q:"Best average for house prices in Sandton:",opts:["Mean","Mode","Range","Median"],a:3,et:"A few very expensive houses skew the mean upwards; the median is more representative. The range measures spread, not the centre."},
      {q:"Q₁ = 20, Q₃ = 35. IQR =",opts:["15","55","27.5","35"],a:0,et:"IQR = Q₃ − Q₁ = 35 − 20 = 15. 27.5 is the midpoint of the quartiles, not the spread."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Mean, median and mode describe the centre; IQR and box plots describe spread. In the exam, show the sorted data or f × x column and name the outlier or skew when you comment."},

  1: {emoji:"📏", subtitle:"Grade 11 · How spread out is the data?",
    illusText:"Two routes both average 20 minutes. One is reliable; the other is a lottery. Standard deviation tells them apart.",
    hook:{title:"Which taxi route is reliable?", body:"Route A to work takes 20, 22, 21, 19 and 18 minutes. Route B takes 10, 30, 15, 25 and 20 minutes. <strong>Both average 20 minutes</strong>. But route A's standard deviation is <strong>1.4 min</strong> while route B's is <strong>7.1 min</strong>. If you can't be late for an exam, you choose route A. Standard deviation measures how much you can trust the average."},
    intro:"<strong>Variance</strong> σ² = Σ(x − x̄)²/n; <strong>standard deviation</strong> σ is its square root, in the data’s units. Small σ: data hugs the mean. CAPS wants σ by hand and by calculator, and its meaning in context.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🧮 Why square?", body:"Deviations (x − x̄) always add to zero — route B: −10 + 10 − 5 + 5 + 0 = 0. Squaring makes them all positive, so they can’t cancel. Dividing by n gives the variance (in minutes²); the square root brings you back to minutes.<br><br>CAPS divides by <strong>n</strong>. Set out a table with columns x, x − x̄ and (x − x̄)², and check the middle column adds to 0."},
      {t:"teal", title:"📱 Calculator STAT mode", body:"STAT → 1-VAR, enter the data, read x̄ and <strong>σx</strong> — not sx, which divides by n − 1. Check n before trusting the answer.<br><br>A typical question: “How many values lie within one standard deviation of the mean?” Work out x̄ − σ and x̄ + σ and count the values between them. (Enrichment: for bell-shaped data about 68% lie there.)"},
      {t:"gold", title:"🔔 Shifting and stretching", body:"Add k to every value (every fare up R2): the mean rises by k, every deviation stays the same, so <strong>σ is unchanged</strong>. Multiply every value by k (a 10% raise, k = 1.1): the mean and <strong>σ are both multiplied by k</strong> (the variance by k²), because every deviation is stretched by k.<br><br>Interpret σ in context: “route B is usually about 7 minutes off its average”. (Enrichment, not CAPS: values beyond 2σ are often called unusual.)"},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Route A by hand", body:"Find σ for 20, 22, 21, 19, 18 minutes.", answer:"x̄ = 100 ÷ 5 = 20.<br>Deviations 0, 2, 1, −1, −2 (sum 0).<br>Squares 0, 4, 1, 1, 4 → 10.<br>σ² = 10 ÷ 5 = 2, so σ = √2 ≈ <strong>1.41 min</strong>. (Route B gives √50 ≈ 7.07.)"},
      {title:"Ten marks", body:"Mthatha learners scored 42, 48, 55, 57, 60, 63, 65, 70, 78, 92. (a) Find x̄ and σ. (b) How many lie within one σ of the mean?", answer:"(a) x̄ = 630 ÷ 10 = <strong>63</strong>; σx ≈ <strong>13.76</strong> (Σ(x − x̄)² = 1 894).<br>(b) 63 − 13.76 = 49.24 and 63 + 13.76 = 76.76.<br>Inside: 55, 57, 60, 63, 65, 70 → <strong>6 learners</strong>."},
      {title:"Flat or percentage increase?", body:"Five Bloemfontein guards earn R5 800, R6 000, R6 200, R6 400, R6 600. Find x̄ and σ, then after (a) a R500 increase each, (b) a 10% increase each.", answer:"x̄ = R6 200. Deviations ±400, ±200, 0 → squares sum 400 000; σ = √80 000 ≈ R282.84.<br>(a) x̄ = <strong>R6 700</strong>; deviations unchanged, σ ≈ <strong>R282.84</strong>.<br>(b) x̄ = 6 200 × 1.1 = <strong>R6 820</strong>; σ ≈ 282.84 × 1.1 ≈ <strong>R311.13</strong>.<br>A flat increase keeps the gaps the same; a percentage increase widens them in rands."},
    ],
    formula:"σ = √[Σ(x − x̄)²/n] · x + k: x̄ + k, σ same · kx: kx̄, kσ",
    mistakes:["Not squaring the deviations (they add to 0). Fix: square each one first.","Using sx instead of σx. Fix: CAPS uses σx.","Stopping at the variance. Fix: finish with the square root.","Thinking a flat increase raises σ. Fix: adding a constant never changes the spread.","Answering “comment on the spread” with only a number. Fix: say what it means — “route A’s times are more consistent”."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Bottling plants monitor σ of fill volumes — a rising σ means the machine needs servicing. Unit-trust fact sheets report “volatility”, the σ of returns. Cricket analysts compare Proteas batters on consistency, not only average."},
    activity:"(a) Find x̄ and σ of 3, 5, 5, 6, 8, 9 using a table.<br>(b) How many values are within one σ of x̄?<br>(c) Every value is doubled. New x̄ and σ?",
    activityAnswer:"(a) x̄ = 36 ÷ 6 = 6. Squares 9, 1, 1, 0, 4, 9 → 24; σ² = 4, σ = 2.<br>(b) 4 to 8: 5, 5, 6, 8 → <strong>4 values</strong>.<br>(c) Both double: x̄ = 12, σ = 4.",
    examTip:"P2 asks for x̄ and σ to two decimals, then the count within one σ. Write the interval x̄ ± σ out — it carries a mark. Comment in context: “Class A’s marks are more spread out, since σ_A > σ_B.”",
    keyterms:[{w:"Variance",d:"Average of squared deviations from the mean"},{w:"Standard deviation",d:"Square root of variance; typical distance from the mean"},{w:"Deviation",d:"x − x̄, how far a value is from the mean"},{w:"Normal distribution",d:"Symmetric bell-shaped spread of data (enrichment)"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"A small standard deviation means:",opts:["Data is spread out","Data is close to the mean","The mean is small","There are outliers"],a:1,et:"Small σ → values cluster around the mean. σ says nothing about whether the mean itself is small."},
      {q:"A set of marks has σ = 6. Every mark is increased by 5. The new standard deviation is:",opts:["11","30","6","5"],a:2,et:"Adding 5 to every mark moves the mean up by 5 too, so every deviation x − x̄ is unchanged and σ stays 6. Only multiplying every value would change σ."},
      {q:"Variance is 16. Standard deviation:",opts:["4","8","256","16"],a:0,et:"σ = √16 = 4. 8 is half of 16, a common slip; 256 squares instead of square-rooting."},
    ],
    conclusion:"σ is the square root of the average squared deviation. Adding a constant leaves it unchanged; multiplying scales it. In the exam, use σx, write out x̄ ± σ and comment in context."},

  2: {emoji:"📶", subtitle:"Grade 11 · Histograms, frequency polygons and ogives",
    illusText:"Raw numbers are hard to read. A histogram or ogive shows the shape of the data in one glance.",
    hook:{title:"Where do most learners score?", body:"A teacher has 40 learners' marks grouped into intervals. A <strong>histogram</strong> shows the biggest group of learners scored 40–60%. An <strong>ogive</strong> (cumulative frequency curve) instantly reads off the median (≈ 54%) and the top 25% (above ≈ 72%). Schools and the Department of Basic Education use exactly these graphs to analyse results."},
    intro:"A <strong>histogram</strong> shows grouped data with touching bars. A <strong>frequency polygon</strong> plots frequencies at class midpoints. An <strong>ogive</strong> plots <strong>cumulative frequency</strong> at each <strong>upper limit</strong> and gives the median, quartiles and percentiles.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"📊 Histogram and frequency polygon", body:"Grouped marks are continuous — 39.9 and 40 are neighbours — so histogram bars touch, unlike a bar graph of categories.<br><br>A <strong>frequency polygon</strong> plots each frequency at the class <strong>midpoint</strong>, joined by straight lines and closed at zero at the midpoints of the empty classes either side. Two polygons fit on one set of axes, ideal for comparing classes.<br><br>A long right tail means positively skewed (most values low, a few very high, like Joburg rents); a long left tail means negatively skewed (an easy test where most scored high)."},
      {t:"teal", title:"📈 Ogive", body:"An ogive answers “how many are below this value?” The class 40 ≤ x < 60 is only complete at 60, so cumulative frequency goes at the <strong>upper limit</strong>. Start at the anchor (lower limit of the first class; 0) and join with a smooth curve. Plotting at the midpoint would claim all 14 learners in that class scored below 50, which is wrong.<br><br>Read the median at n/2, Q₁ at n/4, Q₃ at 3n/4 and the pth percentile at p% of n: across from the y-axis to the curve, then down."},
      {t:"gold", title:"↔️ Skewness: histogram vs box plot", body:"Symmetric: mean ≈ median, median mid-box. Positively skewed: mean > median, long right tail, Q₃ − median > median − Q₁. Negatively skewed: the opposite.<br><br>The ogive links the two pictures: read off the five-number summary and draw the box plot. For our 40 learners, the estimated mean (2 160 ÷ 40 = 54%) ≈ median, so the data is roughly symmetric."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Cumulative frequency", body:"Marks of 40 learners: 0 ≤ x < 20: 4 · 20 ≤ x < 40: 6 · 40 ≤ x < 60: 14 · 60 ≤ x < 80: 10 · 80 ≤ x ≤ 100: 6. Find the ogive points.", answer:"Running totals: 4; 4 + 6 = 10; 10 + 14 = 24; 24 + 10 = 34; 34 + 6 = <strong>40</strong> (= n, a good check).<br>Plot each total at the upper limit of its class, because only there is the whole class counted.<br>Points at upper limits, from the anchor: <strong>(0; 0), (20; 4), (40; 10), (60; 24), (80; 34), (100; 40)</strong>."},
      {title:"Median and Q₃", body:"Estimate the median and Q₃.", answer:"Median: 20th learner, 10 into the class of 14 (40 ≤ x < 60).<br>40 + (10/14) × 20 ≈ <strong>54%</strong>.<br>Q₃: 30th learner, 6 into the class of 10 (60 ≤ x < 80).<br>60 + (6/10) × 20 = <strong>72%</strong>.<br>(Q₁: the 10th learner is exactly at 40%.)"},
      {title:"Polygon, “at least” and P₉₀", body:"(a) Frequency polygon points? (b) How many scored at least 60%? (c) Estimate the 90th percentile.", answer:"(a) (10; 4), (30; 6), (50; 14), (70; 10), (90; 6), closed at (−10; 0) and (110; 0) — the empty classes either side, a drawing convention.<br>(b) 40 − 24 = <strong>16 learners</strong>.<br>(c) 36th learner, 2 into the class of 6 (80 ≤ x ≤ 100): 80 + (2/6) × 20 ≈ <strong>87%</strong>."},
    ],
    formula:"Median at n/2 · Q₁ at n/4 · Q₃ at 3n/4 · pth percentile at (p/100) × n (from the ogive)",
    mistakes:["Plotting the ogive at midpoints. Fix: use upper limits.","Forgetting the anchor (lower limit; 0). Fix: start on the x-axis.","Gaps between histogram bars. Fix: continuous data → bars touch.","Answering “at least 60%” with the reading at 60. Fix: that counts those below; subtract from n.","Taking the answer from the y-axis. Fix: go across from n/2 to the curve, then down — the answer is on the x-axis."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Mark distributions are checked before NSC results are released — a badly skewed histogram signals a paper that was too hard or too easy. Stats SA’s population pyramids are back-to-back histograms showing South Africa’s young population. Analysts at retailers, banks and Eskom build these graphs in Excel or Power BI daily — the skill you practise in pencil is what they automate."},
    activity:"Use the same 40 learners.<br>(a) Estimate how many scored below 50%.<br>(b) Estimate the IQR.<br>(c) The pass mark is 40%. What percentage passed?",
    activityAnswer:"(a) Halfway through 40 ≤ x < 60: 10 + ½ × 14 = 17.<br>(b) Q₁ ≈ 40, Q₃ ≈ 72, so IQR ≈ 32.<br>(c) 40 − 10 = 30 passed: 30/40 = <strong>75%</strong>.",
    examTip:"A typical P2 question: complete the cumulative frequency column, draw the ogive, then “use your graph to estimate…”. Draw dotted lines on the ogive for every reading — that line is the method mark, and graph answers are accepted within a tolerance, so write “≈”. “More than” questions need n minus the reading. Name skewness as “positively” or “negatively skewed”.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Cumulative frequency",d:"Running total of frequencies"},{w:"Ogive",d:"Graph of cumulative frequency vs upper class limit"},{w:"Frequency polygon",d:"Frequencies plotted at class midpoints and joined with straight lines"},{w:"Skewness",d:"Lack of symmetry in data"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"An ogive is plotted using:",opts:["Midpoints","Lower class limits","Upper class limits","Frequencies only"],a:2,et:"Cumulative frequency counts everything up to the end of a class, so it is plotted at the upper limit. Midpoints are for frequency polygons."},
      {q:"With 60 values, the median is read at cumulative frequency:",opts:["15","45","60","30"],a:3,et:"n/2 = 30. 15 and 45 are where you read Q₁ and Q₃."},
      {q:"Mean > median usually means:",opts:["Symmetric","Positively skewed","Negatively skewed","No outliers"],a:1,et:"A long tail on the right pulls the mean up above the median. Symmetric data has mean ≈ median."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Histograms and polygons show shape; ogives, plotted at upper limits from a zero anchor, give the median, quartiles and percentiles. In the exam, draw dotted reading lines and subtract from n for “at least”."},

  3: {emoji:"🔢", subtitle:"Grade 12 · Counting without listing",
    illusText:"How many 4-digit PINs are there? How many ways can 5 people line up for a photo? Counting principles answer instantly.",
    hook:{title:"How safe is your banking PIN?", body:"A 4-digit PIN with any digits: 10 × 10 × 10 × 10 = <strong>10 000</strong> options. A thief guessing 3 times has a 3-in-10 000 chance. If digits can't repeat, there are only 10 × 9 × 8 × 7 = <strong>5 040</strong>. That's why banks block your card after 3 wrong attempts."},
    intro:"The <strong>fundamental counting principle</strong>: m ways then n ways gives m × n ways. <strong>n!</strong> = n × (n − 1) × … × 1 arranges n different objects (0! = 1). Divide by the factorial of each repeat. Then probability = favourable ÷ total.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🧮 Why multiply?", body:"Think of a tree: 4 shirts, each branching into 3 pants, each into 2 pairs of takkies — 4 × 3 × 2 = 24 outfits, one per branch end.<br><br>Draw a box per position: one letter then two digits is [26][10][10] = 2 600 codes; 3 letters and 3 digits is 26³ × 10³ = 17 576 000 plates.<br><br>Trap: add only for “either/or”, never for “and then”."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🔁 Arrangements and gluing", body:"5 friends in a photo at the Union Buildings: 5! = 120. Restrictions first: Thabo on the left end leaves 4! = 24.<br><br>Lerato and Sipho together: glue them into one unit, giving 4!, and they can swap, × 2!: 4! × 2! = 48. “Not together” = 120 − 48 = 72. Forgetting the × 2! is the classic slip."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🔤 Repeats and “at least”", body:"SOWETO: with labelled O₁, O₂ there are 6! = 720 orders, but each word appears twice (swap O₁ and O₂ and nothing changes), so 720 ÷ 2! = 360. BAFANA has three A’s, which can be shuffled 3! = 6 ways, so 6!/3! = 120.<br><br>For “at least” problems, use the complement: P(at least two match) = 1 − P(all different).<br><br>Next lesson: choosing a group (order irrelevant) is arrangements ÷ repeats."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"PIN codes", body:"How many 4-digit PINs have no repeated digits?", answer:"First box: any of 10 digits. Second: 9, since one digit is used. Third: 8. Fourth: 7.<br>10 × 9 × 8 × 7 = <strong>5 040</strong>. One random guess succeeds with probability 1/5 040."},
      {title:"BAFANA", body:"BAFANA’s letters are arranged randomly. (a) How many arrangements? (b) P(it starts and ends with A)?", answer:"(a) Three A’s: 6!/3! = 720 ÷ 6 = <strong>120</strong>.<br>(b) Put an A in the first and last boxes: A _ _ _ _ A. Only one A is left, so the middle letters B, F, A, N are all different → 4! = 24 arrangements.<br>P = 24/120 = <strong>1/5</strong>."},
      {title:"Shared birth months", body:"In a group of 6, find P(at least two share a birth month), months equally likely.", answer:"“At least two” has many cases (two share, three share, two pairs…), so use the complement: all six months different.<br>Friend 1 has 12 months, friend 2 has 11 left, and so on: 12 × 11 × 10 × 9 × 8 × 7 = 665 280.<br>Total: 12⁶ = 2 985 984.<br>P(all different) ≈ 0.223.<br>P(at least two) = 1 − 0.223 ≈ <strong>0.777</strong> — more likely than not, which surprises most people."},
    ],
    formula:"m × n × … · n! arrangements · n!/(a!·b!) with repeats · P(at least…) = 1 − P(none…)",
    mistakes:["Adding instead of multiplying. Fix: “and then” means multiply.","Forgetting glued items can swap. Fix: × 2!.","Not dividing by repeats. Fix: BAFANA → ÷ 3!.","Listing every “at least” case. Fix: use 1 − P(all different).","Leaving the restriction until last. Fix: fill the restricted boxes (Thabo on the end, A at both ends) first, then the free ones."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"An 8-character password from upper- and lower-case letters and digits has 62⁸ ≈ 2.2 × 10¹⁴ options versus 10 000 for a PIN — that’s why banking apps want long passwords. Counting also drives exam timetables, courier delivery routes and gene combinations in genetics — and cybersecurity analysts use it to judge how long a brute-force attack would take."},
    activity:"(a) 3-letter codes from A–Z, no repeats?<br>(b) Locker codes: one letter then two digits (digits may repeat)?<br>(c) P(a random locker code has two equal digits)?",
    activityAnswer:"(a) 26 × 25 × 24 = <strong>15 600</strong>.<br>(b) 26 × 10 × 10 = <strong>2 600</strong>.<br>(c) Equal digits: 00, 11, …, 99 is 10 options, so 26 × 10 = 260 codes. P = 260/2 600 = <strong>1/10</strong>.",
    examTip:"P1 counting questions build up: (a) total arrangements, (b) with a restriction, (c) a probability. Draw the boxes and write the choices in each. Write the expression (6!/3!) before the answer — it earns the method mark even if you slip on the calculator. For “at least” questions, show 1 − P(…) clearly, and give probabilities to the accuracy asked.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Counting principle",d:"Multiply the number of options at each stage"},{w:"Factorial (n!)",d:"n × (n − 1) × … × 1; 0! = 1"},{w:"Arrangement",d:"An ordering of objects in a row"},{w:"Complement",d:"P(not A) = 1 − P(A)"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"4! =",opts:["4","16","24","10"],a:2,et:"4 × 3 × 2 × 1 = 24. 10 is 4 + 3 + 2 + 1 — factorial multiplies, it doesn’t add."},
      {q:"3 starters, 5 mains, 2 desserts. Number of 3-course meals:",opts:["10","30","15","60"],a:1,et:"3 × 5 × 2 = 30. Adding gives 10, the most common wrong answer."},
      {q:"Arrangements of the letters in LOOP:",opts:["24","6","4","12"],a:3,et:"4!/2! = 24 ÷ 2 = 12, because the two O’s are identical. 24 forgets to divide by the repeat."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Multiply choices, arrange with n!, glue for “together”, divide for repeats, and use the complement for “at least”. In the exam, draw boxes and write the expression first."},

  4: {emoji:"🎟️", subtitle:"Grade 12 · Enrichment (beyond CAPS) · Choosing groups when order doesn't matter",
    illusText:"Lotto doesn't care what order the balls come out. Choosing a group is different from arranging a line.",
    hook:{title:"What are your real Lotto odds?", body:"SA Lotto draws 6 balls from 58. Order doesn't matter, so the number of possible tickets is <strong>40 475 358</strong>. Buying one ticket a week, you'd expect to win the jackpot about once every <strong>778 000 years</strong>. Buying every possible ticket at R5 each would cost about R202 million. That R5 might be better in a savings account — maths helps you decide."},
    intro:"When <strong>order matters</strong> you count arrangements. When it doesn’t (a team, a committee), count ordered selections and divide by r!. This topic is <strong>enrichment</strong>: CAPS examines only the counting principle, but this method is just that principle plus dividing out repeats.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🤔 Does order matter?", body:"President, secretary, treasurer from 10: order matters, 10 × 9 × 8 = 720. A committee of 3: the group {Ayanda, Bongi, Cebo} appears in those 720 lists 3! = 6 times (ABC, ACB, BAC, BCA, CAB, CBA), so 720 ÷ 6 = 120. It is the SOWETO idea from the last lesson: divide out the orderings you don’t care about.<br><br>Test: swap two chosen people. New outcome? Order matters. Same outcome? It doesn’t."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🧮 The shortcut (enrichment)", body:"nCr = n!/(r!(n − r)!). For 10C3: (10 × 9 × 8)/3! = 120, since 7! cancels. On a Casio fx-82 nCr is SHIFT ÷; on other calculators look for the nCr key.<br><br>Choosing 3 to go equals choosing 7 to stay, so 10C3 = 10C7. The shortcut is beyond CAPS. The divide-by-r! method always works and shows your reasoning, so show it first, then use nCr to check."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🎲 Probability with groups", body:"P = favourable groups ÷ total groups. Is Lerato on a random committee of 3 from 10? Include her and choose 2 of the other 9: (9 × 8)/2! = 36, so P = 36/120 = 0.3 — matching 3 seats for 10 people.<br><br>Lotto balls have no memory: a number is never “due”."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Class reps", body:"Choose 3 class reps from 10 learners.", answer:"Does order matter? No — three reps have equal roles.<br>Ordered: 10 × 9 × 8 = 720.<br>Each group appears 3! = 6 times in that list.<br>720 ÷ 6 = <strong>120</strong> groups."},
      {title:"Soccer team", body:"5 players from 11 for five-a-side. (a) How many teams? (b) If captain Kagiso must play? (c) P(Kagiso is picked)?", answer:"(a) (11 × 10 × 9 × 8 × 7)/5! = 55 440 ÷ 120 = <strong>462</strong>.<br>(b) Kagiso is in, so choose the other 4 from 10: (10 × 9 × 8 × 7)/4! = 5 040 ÷ 24 = <strong>210</strong>.<br>(c) 210/462 = <strong>5/11</strong>. Check: 5 places shared among 11 players gives 5/11 too."},
      {title:"Lotto", body:"6 balls from 58: how many tickets?", answer:"Order of the balls doesn’t matter.<br>Ordered: 58 × 57 × 56 × 55 × 54 × 53 = 29 142 257 760.<br>Each set of 6 appears 6! = 720 times: 29 142 257 760 ÷ 720 = <strong>40 475 358</strong>.<br>P(jackpot) = 1/40 475 358; weekly, that’s about 778 000 years on average."},
    ],
    mistakes:["Arranging when order doesn't matter. Fix: do the swap test.","Forgetting ÷ r!. Fix: write it as its own step.","Dividing by r! for a podium. Fix: gold ≠ bronze, so order matters.","Thinking numbers drawn last week are less likely to come up again — every draw is independent.","Handling “must include” by subtracting at the end. Fix: put that person in first, then choose the rest from those left."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Published Lotto odds protect consumers, but only if you can read them. Factories choose quality-test samples from batches, and medical trials randomly choose patient groups using the same counting. Actuaries at insurers and statisticians at Stats SA, who choose survey samples, use it daily."},
    activity:"A pizza place offers 8 toppings; you choose 3.<br>(a) How many pizzas?<br>(b) How many include cheese?<br>(c) P(a random pizza includes cheese)?",
    activityAnswer:"(a) (8 × 7 × 6)/3! = 336 ÷ 6 = <strong>56</strong>.<br>(b) Choose 2 of the other 7: 42 ÷ 2 = <strong>21</strong>.<br>(c) 21/56 = <strong>3/8</strong>. Check: 3 of the 8 toppings get picked, so cheese is in 3/8 of pizzas.",
    examTip:"nCr is beyond CAPS, so show the counting-principle method: the ordered count ÷ r!, plus one sentence on whether order matters. Even if you use nCr, write (10 × 9 × 8)/3! so the marker sees your reasoning. The real skill being tested is deciding whether order matters — so say it in words.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Combination",d:"A selection where order doesn't matter"},{w:"Permutation",d:"An arrangement where order matters"},{w:"Independent draws",d:"Past results don't affect future ones"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"Choosing 2 friends from 5 to go to a concert (order doesn't matter):",opts:["10","20","25","5"],a:0,et:"(5 × 4)/2! = 10. 20 forgets to divide by 2!, counting “Ayanda then Bongi” and “Bongi then Ayanda” as different."},
      {q:"Gold, silver and bronze from 8 runners:",opts:["56","336","512","24"],a:1,et:"Order matters (gold ≠ bronze): 8 × 7 × 6 = 336. 56 divides by 3! as if the medals were the same."},
      {q:"After 5 draws without your number, the chance it comes up next draw:",opts:["Increases","Decreases","Stays the same","Becomes certain"],a:2,et:"Each draw is independent — the balls have no memory. Believing a number is “due” is called the gambler’s fallacy."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Ask “does order matter?” first. If not, count ordered selections and divide by r!. In the exam, show this method even if you check with nCr."},

  5: {emoji:"🩺", subtitle:"Grade 11–12 · Dependent and independent events, trees and tables",
    illusText:"A medical test is 95% accurate and you test positive. Are you 95% likely to be sick? Often not even close.",
    hook:{title:"The test that's “95% accurate”", body:"A disease affects 2% of people. A test catches 95% of sick people, but also wrongly flags 5% of healthy people. If you test positive, the chance you're actually sick is only about <strong>28%</strong>! Conditional probability (enrichment) explains why doctors order a second test — using the tree diagrams you learn in CAPS."},
    intro:"<strong>Mutually exclusive</strong>: P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B). In general P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B). <strong>Independent</strong>: P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B). <strong>Dependent</strong>: the first outcome changes the second. Tools: <strong>tree diagrams</strong> and <strong>contingency tables</strong>.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🌳 Trees and “without replacement”", body:"Multiply along branches for “and”; add end results for “or”; branches from one point add to 1.<br><br>Without replacement the second branches change. With 5 red and 3 blue sweets, after a red is eaten 4 red of 7 remain: 4/7, not 5/8. One sweet fewer, so every denominator drops by 1; the numerator drops only for the colour taken. That makes the events <strong>dependent</strong>. With replacement, or tossing a coin twice, the branches repeat and the events are independent."},
      {t:"teal", title:"⚖️ Mutually exclusive vs independent", body:"<strong>Mutually exclusive</strong>: can’t both happen, P(A and B) = 0 (one dice showing 2 and 5). <strong>Independent</strong>: no influence, P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B) (Cape Town rain and a Bafana win).<br><br>They differ: mutually exclusive events with non-zero probabilities are never independent. Example: P(A) = 0.4, P(B) = 0.5, P(A and B) = 0.2 = 0.4 × 0.5, so independent, and P(A or B) = 0.7."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🧾 Tables and “given that”", body:"In a two-way table, fill in totals first; each probability is a cell ÷ grand total. Independent if P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B).<br><br>Enrichment (beyond CAPS): “given B” shrinks the sample space to B: P(A|B) = P(A and B)/P(B). If 160 learners passed and 90 of them take Maths, P(Maths | passed) = 90/160 = 0.5625."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Sweets without replacement", body:"5 red and 3 blue sweets; two taken without replacement. (a) P(both red)? (b) P(one of each)?", answer:"First draw: red 5/8, blue 3/8. After a red: red 4/7, blue 3/7. After a blue: red 5/7, blue 2/7.<br>(a) 5/8 × 4/7 = 20/56 = <strong>5/14</strong>.<br>(b) 5/8 × 3/7 + 3/8 × 5/7 = 30/56 = <strong>15/28</strong>.<br>Check: P(both blue) = 6/56, and 20 + 30 + 6 = 56."},
      {title:"Independence from a table", body:"200 Soshanguve learners. Boys: taxi 72, no taxi 48. Girls: taxi 48, no taxi 32. Are “boy” and “taxi” independent?", answer:"Totals first: boys 120, girls 80, taxi 120, no taxi 80.<br>P(boy) = 120/200 = 0.6; P(taxi) = 120/200 = 0.6.<br>Product: 0.36.<br>P(boy and taxi) = 72/200 = 0.36.<br>Equal, so <strong>independent</strong>: boys and girls are equally likely to take a taxi."},
      {title:"The envelope problem", body:"20 envelopes: 8 hold 8 sheets with 3 red; 12 hold 8 sheets with 2 red. Choose an envelope, then a sheet. P(red)?", answer:"Draw a two-stage tree. First branch: envelope type, 8/20 = 0.4 or 12/20 = 0.6. Second branch: red 3/8 or 2/8.<br>8/20 × 3/8 = 0.4 × 0.375 = 0.15.<br>12/20 × 2/8 = 0.6 × 0.25 = 0.15.<br>P(red) = 0.15 + 0.15 = <strong>0.3</strong>."},
    ],
    formula:"P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B) · Independent: P(A and B) = P(A)·P(B) · Enrichment: P(A|B) = P(A and B)/P(B)",
    mistakes:["Confusing mutually exclusive with independent. Fix: 0 versus P(A) × P(B).","Adding along branches. Fix: along = multiply, between = add.","Same denominators without replacement. Fix: drop by 1.","Testing independence with counts instead of probabilities. Fix: divide each count by the grand total first.","No conclusion in an independence question. Fix: end with “equal, so independent”."],
    callout:{title:"Enrichment: why 28%?", body:"Picture 10 000 people: 200 sick, 190 of them test positive; 9 800 healthy, 490 test positive. Of 680 positives, only 190 are sick: ≈ 0.28. That’s why a positive HIV rapid test is confirmed with a second test, and TB screening is followed by a lab test. Actuaries, epidemiologists at the NICD and even email spam filters rely on the same reasoning."},
    activity:"100 Gqeberha Grade 12s. Job: pass 12, fail 18. No job: pass 42, fail 28.<br>(a) P(pass) and P(job and pass)?<br>(b) Independent?<br>(c) P(job or pass)?",
    activityAnswer:"(a) 54/100 = 0.54; 12/100 = 0.12.<br>(b) P(job) × P(pass) = 0.3 × 0.54 = 0.162 ≠ 0.12 → <strong>dependent</strong>.<br>(c) 0.3 + 0.54 − 0.12 = <strong>0.72</strong>.",
    examTip:"Expect a tree (label every branch, and change denominators without replacement), a contingency table, or “determine whether A and B are independent”. A labelled tree earns marks even if the final answer is wrong. For independence, marks go to P(A) × P(B), P(A and B), the comparison and a written conclusion.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Independent",d:"One event doesn't change the probability of the other"},{w:"Mutually exclusive",d:"Cannot happen at the same time"},{w:"Dependent events",d:"The first outcome changes the probability of the second"},{w:"Conditional probability",d:"Probability of A given that B has happened (enrichment)"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"P(A) = 0.3, P(B) = 0.5, independent. P(A and B) =",opts:["0.8","0.2","0.35","0.15"],a:3,et:"Independent → multiply: 0.3 × 0.5 = 0.15. 0.8 adds the probabilities, which is the “or” rule for mutually exclusive events."},
      {q:"Mutually exclusive events have P(A and B) =",opts:["1","P(A)·P(B)","0","0.5"],a:2,et:"They can't happen together, so P(A and B) = 0. P(A)·P(B) is the test for independence — a different idea."},
      {q:"A box has 4 green and 2 yellow tokens. Two are taken without replacement. P(both yellow) =",opts:["1/9","1/15","1/3","2/15"],a:1,et:"2/6 × 1/5 = 2/30 = 1/15. 1/9 is the answer with replacement (2/6 × 2/6) — forgetting to change the second branch."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Multiply along branches, add the ends, and change denominators without replacement. Test independence with P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B). In the exam, label every branch and write a conclusion."},
};

// ─── Module 6: Calculus ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
LESSON_DB.math.m6 = {
  0: {emoji:"🏎️", subtitle:"Grade 12 · From average speed to speed right now",
    illusText:"Your speedometer shows your speed at this exact instant — not your average for the trip. Calculus is how that's possible.",
    hook:{title:"Average vs instantaneous speed", body:"A car drives from Durban to Pietermaritzburg (80 km) in 1 hour — an average of 80 km/h. But the driver crawled past the Pavilion and flew up Field's Hill. A fixed traffic camera on the N3 measures your speed at <strong>one instant</strong>. How can an instant, with no time in it, have a speed? Calculus shrinks the time interval until the average settles on one value. That idea is a <strong>limit</strong>."},
    intro:"The <strong>average rate of change</strong> of f between x = a and x = b is [f(b) − f(a)]/(b − a) — Grade 11's average gradient. As b gets closer and closer to a, this approaches the <strong>instantaneous rate of change</strong>: the gradient of the <strong>tangent</strong> at x = a. A <strong>limit</strong> describes the value an expression approaches.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"📐 Average rate (with units)", body:"A stone falls s(t) = 5t² metres after t seconds. Between t = 1 s and t = 3 s it falls from s(1) = 5 m to s(3) = 45 m, so the average rate is (45 − 5)/(3 − 1) = 40/2 = 20 m/s.<br><br>A rate always has units: <em>output units per input unit</em> — metres per second here, rand per kWh on an Eskom bill, litres per minute for a JoJo tank. Geometrically it is the gradient of the <strong>secant</strong> through the two points."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🔍 Limits: 0/0 means “simplify”", body:"In lim(x→3) (x² − 9)/(x − 3), substituting gives 0/0. That is <strong>not</strong> an answer — it signals a hidden common factor. Factorise: (x + 3)(x − 3)/(x − 3) = x + 3 (allowed, because x → 3 means x never equals 3, so x − 3 ≠ 0). Substitute: 3 + 3 = <strong>6</strong>.<br><br>Check: at x = 2.9 the expression is (8.41 − 9)/(−0.1) = 5.9; at x = 3.1 it is 0.61/0.1 = 6.1. It closes in on 6 from both sides — that is what a limit means."},
      {t:"gold", title:"📏 Secant → tangent", body:"For f(x) = x², secant gradients from x = 1:<br>to x = 2: (4 − 1)/1 = 3<br>to x = 1.1: (1.21 − 1)/0.1 = 2.1<br>to x = 1.01: (1.0201 − 1)/0.01 = 2.01<br>As the points merge, the secant becomes the <strong>tangent</strong> and the gradients approach <strong>2</strong>.<br><br>Writing the second point as a + h gives next lesson's tool: gradient at a = lim(h→0) [f(a + h) − f(a)]/h = f′(a), the <strong>derivative</strong>."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Average gradient (easy)", body:"Find the average gradient of f(x) = x² + 1 between x = 1 and x = 4.<br>Step 1: f(4) = 17 and f(1) = 2.<br>Step 2: (17 − 2)/(4 − 1).", answer:"15/3 = <strong>5</strong>."},
      {title:"Evaluate a limit (exam standard)", body:"lim(x→2) (x² − 4)/(x − 2).<br>Step 1: substitution gives 0/0, so factorise: lim(x→2) (x + 2)(x − 2)/(x − 2).<br>Step 2: cancel (x − 2), which is non-zero while x → 2: lim(x→2) (x + 2).", answer:"2 + 2 = <strong>4</strong>."},
      {title:"Falling stone — the shrinking table (real life)", body:"s(t) = 5t² metres. Average speed from t = 2 to t = 2 + h, with s(2) = 20 m:<br>h = 0.1: s(2.1) = 22.05 → 2.05/0.1 = 20.5 m/s<br>h = 0.01: s(2.01) = 20.2005 → 0.2005/0.01 = 20.05 m/s<br>h = 0.001: s(2.001) = 20.020005 → 20.005 m/s<br>Algebra: [5(2 + h)² − 20]/h = (20h + 5h²)/h = 20 + 5h.", answer:"As h → 0, 20 + 5h → 20, so the speed at exactly t = 2 s is <strong>20 m/s</strong> (72 km/h)."},
    ],
    formula:"Average gradient = [f(b) − f(a)]/(b − a)",
    mistakes:["Writing “undefined” for 0/0. It means a factor must cancel — factorise, then substitute.","Writing “lim” on the final line. Once you substitute, drop it — and not before.","Subtracting in opposite orders, e.g. (f(4) − f(1))/(1 − 4), which flips the sign. Keep the same order top and bottom.","Mixing up average rate (two points) and instantaneous rate (one point, a limit)."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"During COVID-19, NICD epidemiologists watched the <strong>rate</strong> of new cases per day — a rising rate warned of a new wave before hospitals filled. Eskom's control room tracks how fast demand climbs when stoves switch on at 5 p.m. Actuaries and engineers are paid to read rates of change."},
    activity:"For f(x) = 2x²:<br>(a) Find the average gradient between x = 1 and x = 3.<br>(b) Find the average gradient between x = 1 and x = 1.01.<br>(c) Show that the average gradient from x = 1 to x = 1 + h is 4 + 2h, and state the gradient at x = 1.",
    activityAnswer:"(a) f(3) = 18, f(1) = 2 → (18 − 2)/(3 − 1) = 16/2 = 8.<br>(b) f(1.01) = 2.0402 → 0.0402/0.01 = 4.02.<br>(c) f(1 + h) = 2(1 + 2h + h²) = 2 + 4h + 2h². Subtract 2, divide by h: 4 + 2h. As h → 0 it approaches <strong>4</strong>.",
    examTip:"Average-gradient questions (2–3 marks) want f(b) and f(a) shown, then the quotient. For limits, write “lim(x→a)” on every line until you substitute — dropping it early costs marks. “Average gradient between” means two points; “gradient at” means the derivative.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Limit",d:"The value an expression approaches"},{w:"Average rate of change",d:"Gradient of the secant between two points"},{w:"Instantaneous rate",d:"Gradient of the tangent at one point"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"Average gradient of f(x) = x² from x = 0 to x = 2:",opts:["1","2","4","0"],a:1,et:"(4 − 0)/(2 − 0) = 2."},
      {q:"lim(x→5) (x² − 25)/(x − 5) =",opts:["0","5","10","Undefined"],a:2,et:"Simplifies to x + 5 → 10."},
      {q:"The gradient of the tangent at a point gives:",opts:["Average rate","Instantaneous rate","Area","The y-intercept"],a:1,et:"Tangent gradient = instantaneous rate of change."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Average gradient uses two points; shrink the gap and the limit gives the gradient at one point. 0/0 means factorise first. In the exam, keep “lim” on every line until you substitute."},

  1: {emoji:"🔬", subtitle:"Grade 12 · Deriving the derivative from scratch",
    illusText:"Before shortcuts, there was first principles — the definition that proves why the shortcuts work.",
    hook:{title:"Why should you trust the shortcut?", body:"Engineers at Sasol in Secunda don't just trust a formula from a manual — when a reactor misbehaves, they need to know where the formula came from. First principles is the definition of the derivative. Prove that the derivative of x² is 2x, and every shortcut makes sense. It's also a regular Paper 1 question."},
    intro:"The <strong>derivative</strong> of f at x is f′(x) = lim(h→0) [f(x + h) − f(x)]/h. It gives the gradient of the tangent (the instantaneous rate of change) at any x. Calculating it with this limit is called <strong>differentiating from first principles</strong>. CAPS examines it for four types: ax² + bx + c, ax³, a/x and a constant c.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"1️⃣ The three steps — and why h must cancel", body:"Step 1: find f(x + h) by replacing every x with (x + h). Step 2: subtract f(x) and simplify — every term left must contain h. Step 3: divide by h, then let h → 0.<br><br>Why not put h = 0 at the start? You'd get 0/0. While h → 0, h is tiny but <strong>not zero</strong>, so dividing by h is legal. Once h has cancelled, you can substitute.<br><br>For f(x) = 3x²: f(x + h) − f(x) = 3x² + 6xh + 3h² − 3x² = 6xh + 3h². Divide: 6x + 3h. Let h → 0: f′(x) = 6x."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🧊 The ax³ type: expand (x + h)³", body:"Expand in two stages: (x + h)² = x² + 2xh + h², then multiply by (x + h):<br>x³ + x²h + 2x²h + 2xh² + xh² + h³ = <strong>x³ + 3x²h + 3xh² + h³</strong>.<br>For f(x) = 2x³: f(x + h) − f(x) = 2(3x²h + 3xh² + h³) = 6x²h + 6xh² + 2h³. Divide by h: 6x² + 6xh + 2h². Let h → 0: f′(x) = <strong>6x²</strong>.<br><br>Trap: (x + h)³ ≠ x³ + h³. Test x = h = 1: 2³ = 8, not 2."},
      {t:"gold", title:"➗ The a/x type: common denominator", body:"For f(x) = a/x, subtract using a common denominator: a/(x + h) − a/x = [ax − a(x + h)]/[x(x + h)] = −ah/[x(x + h)].<br><br>Dividing by h cancels the h on top: −a/[x(x + h)]. Let h → 0: f′(x) = <strong>−a/x²</strong>.<br><br>Slip: dropping the bracket in a(x + h), so h never appears. The constant type is easiest: f(x + h) − f(x) = c − c = 0, so f′(x) = 0."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"f(x) = x² (easy)", body:"Full notation:<br>f′(x) = lim(h→0) [(x + h)² − x²]/h<br>= lim(h→0) [x² + 2xh + h² − x²]/h<br>= lim(h→0) (2xh + h²)/h<br>= lim(h→0) h(2x + h)/h<br>= lim(h→0) (2x + h)", answer:"f′(x) = <strong>2x</strong>."},
      {title:"f(x) = 2x² − 3x (exam standard)", body:"Step 1: f(x + h) = 2(x² + 2xh + h²) − 3(x + h) = 2x² + 4xh + 2h² − 3x − 3h.<br>Step 2: f(x + h) − f(x) = 2x² + 4xh + 2h² − 3x − 3h − (2x² − 3x) = 4xh + 2h² − 3h.<br>Step 3: f′(x) = lim(h→0) (4xh + 2h² − 3h)/h = lim(h→0) (4x + 2h − 3).", answer:"f′(x) = <strong>4x − 3</strong>."},
      {title:"f(x) = 2/x (challenging)", body:"Step 1: f(x + h) = 2/(x + h).<br>Step 2: f(x + h) − f(x) = 2/(x + h) − 2/x = [2x − 2(x + h)]/[x(x + h)] = −2h/[x(x + h)].<br>Step 3: f′(x) = lim(h→0) −2h/[x(x + h)h] = lim(h→0) −2/[x(x + h)].<br>Step 4: let h → 0: −2/(x · x).", answer:"f′(x) = <strong>−2/x²</strong>."},
    ],
    formula:"f′(x) = lim(h→0) [f(x + h) − f(x)]/h",
    mistakes:["Writing f(x) + h instead of f(x + h). Replace every x with (x + h) — don't just add h.","Dropping the minus when subtracting f(x). Put f(x) in brackets: −(2x² − 3x) = −2x² + 3x.","Letting h = 0 before dividing, which gives 0/0. Cancel h first, then let h → 0.","Expanding (x + h)³ as x³ + h³. Expand (x + h)², then multiply by (x + h).","Leaving out “lim(h→0)” on middle lines, or keeping it on the final line. Notation earns marks."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Every derivative in physics, engineering and economics rests on this definition. When a Wits mining engineer models heat flowing through rock 3 km underground, the software computes [f(x + h) − f(x)]/h with a very small h — first principles on a computer."},
    activity:"(a) Differentiate f(x) = 4 − x² from first principles.<br>(b) Differentiate g(x) = x³ from first principles.<br>(c) Use (b) to find the gradient of the tangent to g at x = −2.",
    activityAnswer:"(a) f(x + h) − f(x) = 4 − (x² + 2xh + h²) − (4 − x²) = −2xh − h². f′(x) = lim(h→0) (−2x − h) = <strong>−2x</strong>.<br>(b) g(x + h) − g(x) = x³ + 3x²h + 3xh² + h³ − x³ = 3x²h + 3xh² + h³. g′(x) = lim(h→0) (3x² + 3xh + h²) = <strong>3x²</strong>.<br>(c) g′(−2) = 3(−2)² = 3(4) = <strong>12</strong>.",
    examTip:"Marks go to the method: f(x + h), the simplified numerator, factorising out h, and limit notation. If the question says “from first principles”, a power-rule answer earns little or nothing. Write lim(h→0) on every line until h goes to 0.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Derivative",d:"Instantaneous rate of change; gradient of the tangent"},{w:"First principles",d:"Finding the derivative from the limit definition"},{w:"h",d:"A tiny change in x that approaches 0"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"In first principles, f(x + h) for f(x) = x² is:",opts:["x² + h","x² + h²","x² + 2xh + h²","2x + h"],a:2,et:"(x + h)² = x² + 2xh + h²."},
      {q:"After dividing by h you get 6x + 3h. The derivative is:",opts:["6x + 3h","6x","3h","6"],a:1,et:"Let h → 0: 6x."},
      {q:"The derivative of a constant f(x) = 7 is:",opts:["7","1","0","x"],a:2,et:"f(x + h) − f(x) = 7 − 7 = 0."},
    ],
    conclusion:"First principles: find f(x + h), subtract f(x), cancel h, then let h → 0. Practise all four types — ax² + bx + c, ax³, a/x and c — and keep the limit notation on every line."},

  2: {emoji:"⚡", subtitle:"Grade 12 · The shortcut for derivatives",
    illusText:"Bring the power down, subtract one. That's the rule that makes calculus fast.",
    hook:{title:"The speed of a falling phone", body:"A phone slips out of someone's hand on the top floor of the Carlton Centre in Johannesburg and falls s = 5t² metres after t seconds (ignoring air resistance). Its speed at any instant is ds/dt = <strong>10t m/s</strong>. After 3 seconds it's falling at 30 m/s — 108 km/h, faster than a taxi on the M1. First principles takes five lines to find 10t; the power rule takes one."},
    intro:"<strong>Power rule</strong>: if f(x) = axⁿ, then f′(x) = n·a·xⁿ⁻¹, for any real n. The derivative of a constant is 0, a constant multiple stays in front, and you differentiate a sum or difference term by term. Before differentiating, write roots and fractions as powers: √x = x^½, 1/x² = x⁻². Notation: f′(x), dy/dx and Dₓ[…] all mean the same thing.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"⬇️ Bring down, subtract one", body:"First principles gave d/dx(x²) = 2x and d/dx(x³) = 3x². The pattern is <strong>nxⁿ⁻¹</strong>, and it holds for every real power. A constant multiple just comes along: d/dx(2x⁵) = 2 × 5x⁴ = 10x⁴. A sum is done term by term.<br><br>So y = 2x⁵ − x² + 9 → dy/dx = 10x⁴ − 2x. The 9 disappears: a constant doesn't change, so its rate is 0. The sign stays with its term.<br><br>Notation matters: if the question uses y, answer with dy/dx; if it uses f(x), answer with f′(x)."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🔄 Rewrite first — no product rule in CAPS", body:"The power rule only works on terms of the form axⁿ. So rewrite: y = 3/x = 3x⁻¹ → dy/dx = −3x⁻² = −3/x². y = √x = x^½ → dy/dx = ½x^(−½) = 1/(2√x).<br><br>CAPS has no product or quotient rule, so <strong>expand brackets and split fractions</strong> first. The trap: differentiating each factor separately. y = x² · x³ is really x⁵, so dy/dx = 5x⁴. “Differentiating each bit” gives 2x · 3x² = 6x³ — wrong.<br><br>Split a fraction only when the denominator is a single term: (x³ + x)/x = x² + 1 (x ≠ 0)."},
      {t:"gold", title:"📏 Tangents and given gradients", body:"Tangent at a given x: 1) find the point (x₁; f(x₁)); 2) m = f′(x₁); 3) y − y₁ = m(x − x₁). For y = x² at x = 3: point (3; 9), m = 2(3) = 6 → y − 9 = 6(x − 3) → y = 6x − 9.<br><br>The reverse question: “Find the point where the tangent has gradient 2.” Set f′(x) = 2 and solve for x, then use f(x) for y. For f(x) = x² − 4x: f′(x) = 2x − 4 = 2 → x = 3, and f(3) = 9 − 12 = −3, so the point is (3; −3).<br><br>Key idea: f′ gives gradients; f gives y-values. Never mix them."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Differentiate y = 4x³ − 2x + 7 (easy)", body:"Term by term:<br>d/dx(4x³) = 3 × 4x² = 12x²<br>d/dx(−2x) = −2 (x¹ → 1·x⁰ = 1)<br>d/dx(7) = 0", answer:"dy/dx = <strong>12x² − 2</strong>."},
      {title:"f(x) = (x³ − 4x)/(2x) (exam standard)", body:"Step 1: the denominator is a single term, so split: f(x) = x³/(2x) − 4x/(2x).<br>Step 2: simplify each part: f(x) = ½x² − 2 (x ≠ 0).<br>Step 3: differentiate: ½ × 2x − 0.", answer:"f′(x) = <strong>x</strong> (x ≠ 0)."},
      {title:"Tangent to f(x) = x³ − 3x² at x = 1 (challenging)", body:"Step 1: point: f(1) = 1 − 3 = −2, so (1; −2).<br>Step 2: f′(x) = 3x² − 6x, so m = f′(1) = 3 − 6 = −3.<br>Step 3: y − (−2) = −3(x − 1) → y + 2 = −3x + 3.", answer:"<strong>y = −3x + 1</strong>."},
    ],
    formula:"d/dx (axⁿ) = n·a·xⁿ⁻¹ · d/dx (c) = 0",
    mistakes:["Differentiating a product like (2x − 1)² as 2(2x − 1). Expand first: 4x² − 4x + 1 → 8x − 4.","Forgetting that d/dx(5x) = 5, not 5x — the power drops from 1 to 0, and x⁰ = 1.","Differentiating the top and bottom of 3/x separately, giving 0/1 = 0. Rewrite as 3x⁻¹, then −3x⁻² = −3/x².","Using f′(x) as the y-value of the point of contact. The y-value comes from f(x).","Subtracting 1 from a negative power wrongly: x⁻² becomes −2x⁻³, not −2x⁻¹."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"In economics, the derivative of a cost function is the <strong>marginal cost</strong> — what one more loaf costs a bakery like Albany to produce. SANRAL engineers use gradients of road profiles to design passes like Van Reenen's, and pharmacologists use rates to set medicine doses."},
    activity:"(a) Find f′(x) if f(x) = 2x⁴ − 3x² + 5/x.<br>(b) Find g′(x) if g(x) = (x + 2)(x − 3).<br>(c) Find the point on g where the tangent has gradient 5.",
    activityAnswer:"(a) f(x) = 2x⁴ − 3x² + 5x⁻¹ → f′(x) = 8x³ − 6x − 5x⁻² = <strong>8x³ − 6x − 5/x²</strong>.<br>(b) Expand: g(x) = x² − 3x + 2x − 6 = x² − x − 6 → g′(x) = <strong>2x − 1</strong>.<br>(c) 2x − 1 = 5 → x = 3. g(3) = 9 − 3 − 6 = 0. Point: <strong>(3; 0)</strong>.",
    examTip:"“Use the rules of differentiation” means no first principles — but a mark is usually for the rewritten form with exponents, so show it first. Give answers with positive exponents, and match the question's notation: dy/dx, f′(x) or Dₓ[…].",
    keyterms:[{w:"Power rule",d:"d/dx(xⁿ) = nxⁿ⁻¹"},{w:"dy/dx",d:"Derivative of y with respect to x"},{w:"Tangent",d:"Straight line touching a curve with the same gradient at that point"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"d/dx (5x⁴) =",opts:["20x³","5x³","20x⁴","4x³"],a:0,et:"4 × 5 x³ = 20x³."},
      {q:"d/dx (3/x) =",opts:["0","3/x²","−3/x²","−3x"],a:2,et:"Rewrite as 3x⁻¹, then bring down −1: −3x⁻² = −3/x². Differentiating top and bottom separately gives the wrong answer 0."},
      {q:"Gradient of y = x² + 3x at x = 2:",opts:["10","4","13","7"],a:3,et:"dy/dx = 2x + 3 → 2(2) + 3 = 7. The tempting 10 is the y-value y(2) = 4 + 6 — the point, not the gradient."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Rewrite as powers, expand or split, then bring down and subtract one. f′ gives gradients, f gives points — that distinction wins the tangent-equation marks."},

  3: {emoji:"⛰️", subtitle:"Grade 12 · Sketching cubic graphs with calculus",
    illusText:"At the top of a hill and the bottom of a valley, the ground is flat for a moment. That's where the derivative is zero.",
    hook:{title:"When does profit peak?", body:"A small clothing business in Soweto models its monthly profit as a curve. Where profit stops rising and starts falling, the graph is momentarily flat — like a taxi cresting Chapman's Peak, going neither up nor down. The derivative is <strong>zero</strong>. Setting f′(x) = 0 finds those turning points, and it's how examiners make you sketch cubics."},
    intro:"A <strong>turning point</strong> (stationary point) occurs where f′(x) = 0. A cubic f(x) = ax³ + bx² + cx + d can have two turning points: a local <strong>maximum</strong> and a local <strong>minimum</strong>. A sketch needs the intercepts (x-intercepts by the factor theorem), turning points, point of inflection and the shape from the sign of a.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🎯 Turning points and their nature", body:"Solve f′(x) = 0 for x, then substitute those x-values into <strong>f(x)</strong> (not f′(x)) for the y-values.<br><br>Nature: f″(x) < 0 (concave down) means a <strong>maximum</strong>; f″(x) > 0 (concave up) means a <strong>minimum</strong>. Or check the sign of f′ either side: + then − is a maximum.<br><br>Shape check: if a > 0 the cubic starts low on the left and ends high on the right, so the left turning point is the max."},
      {t:"teal", title:"⬆️⬇️ Increasing, decreasing and the graph of f′", body:"f′(x) > 0 → f is increasing; f′(x) < 0 → f is decreasing.<br><br>Exams often give the graph of <strong>f′</strong> (a parabola) and ask about f. The x-intercepts of f′ are the x-values of f's turning points; where f′ is above the axis, f increases. The turning point of f′ gives the x-value of f's inflection.<br><br>Trap: the y-values on an f′ graph are gradients, not heights of f."},
      {t:"gold", title:"〰️ Inflection, concavity and the full sketch", body:"Where f″(x) = 0 the curve changes concavity — the <strong>point of inflection</strong>. For a cubic it sits exactly halfway between the turning points: x = (x₁ + x₂)/2.<br><br>For x-intercepts use the <strong>factor theorem</strong>: find p with f(p) = 0, so (x − p) is a factor, then divide and factorise the quadratic.<br><br>Sketches also answer “for which k does f(x) = k have three real roots?” — see Example 3."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Turning points of f(x) = x³ − 3x² − 9x + 5 (easy)", body:"Step 1: f′(x) = 3x² − 6x − 9 = 3(x² − 2x − 3) = 3(x − 3)(x + 1).<br>Step 2: f′(x) = 0 → x = 3 or x = −1.<br>Step 3: f(−1) = −1 − 3 + 9 + 5 = 10; f(3) = 27 − 27 − 27 + 5 = −22.<br>Step 4: f″(x) = 6x − 6: f″(−1) = −12 < 0 (max); f″(3) = 12 > 0 (min).", answer:"<strong>Max (−1; 10), min (3; −22)</strong>."},
      {title:"Point of inflection and concavity (exam standard)", body:"Same function: f″(x) = 6x − 6.<br>Step 1: 6x − 6 = 0 → x = 1. Check: (−1 + 3)/2 = 1.<br>Step 2: f(1) = 1 − 3 − 9 + 5 = −6.<br>Step 3: for x < 1, f″(x) < 0 (concave down); for x > 1, f″(x) > 0 (concave up).", answer:"Inflection at <strong>(1; −6)</strong>; concave down for x < 1, concave up for x > 1."},
      {title:"Reading the sketch (challenging)", body:"Same function. (a) For which k does f(x) = k have three real roots? (b) Where is f decreasing?<br>(a) The line y = k must cut the graph three times, so it lies strictly between the min value −22 and the max value 10. At k = 10 or −22 it touches a turning point: only two roots.<br>(b) f′(x) = 3(x − 3)(x + 1) < 0 between its roots.", answer:"(a) <strong>−22 < k < 10</strong>; (b) decreasing for <strong>−1 < x < 3</strong>."},
    ],
    formula:"Turning points: f′(x) = 0 · Inflection: f″(x) = 0",
    mistakes:["Substituting the x-value into f′(x) to get the y-coordinate — you'll always get 0. Use f(x).","Forgetting the y-intercept (0; d) or leaving points unlabelled.","Guessing x-intercepts. Test f(p) = 0, divide, factorise the quadratic.","Using ≤ in “three real roots”: at a turning value the line only touches. Use strict inequalities.","Drawing sharp corners at turning points — a cubic is smooth."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Economists at the South African Reserve Bank watch for turning points in inflation data to judge when a cycle peaks. Health planners model the peak of flu season to schedule nurses, and roller-coaster designers use concavity to keep riders' forces safe."},
    activity:"f(x) = x³ − 3x² − 9x + 27.<br>(a) Show that (x − 3) is a factor, factorise fully and give the intercepts.<br>(b) Find the turning points and their nature.<br>(c) Find the point of inflection, and the values of k for which f(x) = k has three real roots.",
    activityAnswer:"(a) f(3) = 27 − 27 − 27 + 27 = 0, so (x − 3) is a factor. f(x) = (x − 3)(x² − 9) = (x − 3)²(x + 3). x-intercepts −3 and 3 (touches at 3); y-intercept 27.<br>(b) f′(x) = 3x² − 6x − 9 = 3(x − 3)(x + 1) = 0 → x = −1 or 3. f(−1) = −1 − 3 + 9 + 27 = 32; f(3) = 0. <strong>Max (−1; 32), min (3; 0)</strong>.<br>(c) x = (−1 + 3)/2 = 1, f(1) = 1 − 3 − 9 + 27 = 16 → <strong>(1; 16)</strong>. Three real roots for <strong>0 < k < 32</strong>.",
    examTip:"For a cubic sketch, the marks are for: shape, y-intercept, x-intercepts and both turning points, each <strong>labelled with coordinates</strong> on the graph. Show f(p) = 0 when you use the factor theorem. For “three real roots” or “increasing” questions, use strict inequalities.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Stationary point",d:"Point where f′(x) = 0"},{w:"Local maximum/minimum",d:"Highest/lowest point in its neighbourhood"},{w:"Point of inflection",d:"Where concavity changes; f″(x) = 0"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"At a turning point:",opts:["f(x) = 0","f″(x) = 0","x = 0","f′(x) = 0"],a:3,et:"The tangent is horizontal, so its gradient f′(x) is 0. f(x) = 0 gives x-intercepts, and f″(x) = 0 gives the point of inflection."},
      {q:"If f′(x) < 0 on an interval, f is:",opts:["Increasing","Decreasing","Constant","Concave up"],a:1,et:"Negative gradient → decreasing."},
      {q:"f(x) = x³ − 12x has a local minimum at:",opts:["(−2; 16)","(0; 0)","(2; −16)","(2; 16)"],a:2,et:"f′(x) = 3x² − 12 = 0 → x = ±2. f″(2) = 12 > 0, so x = 2 is the minimum, and f(2) = 8 − 24 = −16. (−2; 16) is the maximum."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Set f′(x) = 0 for turning points, use f″ for their nature and the inflection, and the factor theorem for intercepts. Label every point with coordinates — that's where the sketch marks are."},

  4: {emoji:"📦", subtitle:"Grade 12 · Optimisation and rates of change",
    illusText:"The biggest area, the biggest box, the highest throw — the derivative finds the best answer.",
    hook:{title:"The chicken farmer's fence", body:"A farmer near Polokwane has <strong>100 m</strong> of fencing to build a rectangular chicken run against an existing wall (so only three sides need fencing). A 10 m × 80 m run gives 800 m²; so does 40 m × 20 m. Calculus finds the best: <strong>25 m × 50 m = 1 250 m²</strong>."},
    intro:"<strong>Optimisation</strong> means finding the maximum or minimum value of a quantity. Steps: 1) diagram and variable; 2) the quantity in <strong>one</strong> variable, with its domain; 3) set the derivative to 0; 4) check max or min; 5) answer in context, with units. CAPS also examines <strong>rates of change</strong>, including the calculus of motion.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🔗 Model with one variable", body:"With two unknowns you can't differentiate usefully, so use the <strong>constraint</strong> to remove one. Let x be each side perpendicular to the wall and y the side parallel to it. Then 2x + y = 100, so y = 100 − 2x, and area A = xy = x(100 − 2x) = 100x − 2x².<br><br>State the <strong>domain</strong>: lengths are positive, so 0 < x < 50. Solutions outside it are rejected."},
      {t:"teal", title:"📐 Differentiate, solve, confirm", body:"A′(x) = 100 − 4x = 0 → x = 25 → y = 50. Confirm it's a maximum: A″(x) = −4 < 0. Or do a sign check: A′(20) = 20 > 0 and A′(30) = −20 < 0, so A rises then falls.<br><br>Then answer the question: the maximum <strong>area</strong> is A(25) = 1 250 m². The trap is stopping at x = 25."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🏃 Rates of change and motion", body:"Any derivative is a rate: if V(t) is litres in a dam, V′(t) is litres per day. For motion, with displacement s(t):<br>• velocity v(t) = s′(t)<br>• acceleration a(t) = v′(t) = s″(t)<br><br>An object <strong>stops</strong> (momentarily) when v = 0 — which is also when a thrown ball reaches its <strong>maximum height</strong>. A negative velocity means it is moving back down.<br><br>Trap: substituting into s(t) when asked for velocity."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"The chicken run (easy)", body:"100 m fence, wall on one side.<br>Step 1: y = 100 − 2x, so A = 100x − 2x², 0 < x < 50.<br>Step 2: A′(x) = 100 − 4x = 0 → x = 25 m, y = 100 − 50 = 50 m.<br>Step 3: A″(x) = −4 < 0 → maximum.", answer:"<strong>Max area 25 × 50 = 1 250 m²</strong>."},
      {title:"The biggest box (exam standard)", body:"Cut squares of side x cm from the corners of a 30 cm × 30 cm card; fold up an open box.<br>Step 1: base (30 − 2x) by (30 − 2x), height x; 0 < x < 15.<br>Step 2: V = x(30 − 2x)² = x(900 − 120x + 4x²) = 4x³ − 120x² + 900x.<br>Step 3: V′ = 12x² − 240x + 900 = 12(x² − 20x + 75) = 12(x − 5)(x − 15) = 0 → x = 5 or 15. Reject 15 (no base).<br>Step 4: V″ = 24x − 240; V″(5) = −120 < 0 → max.", answer:"<strong>V = 5 × 20² = 2 000 cm³</strong> (2 litres)."},
      {title:"A ball thrown upwards (real life)", body:"At a Bloemfontein sports day, a ball thrown straight up has height s(t) = 20t − 5t² metres.<br>Step 1: v(t) = s′(t) = 20 − 10t.<br>Step 2: max height when v = 0: 20 − 10t = 0 → t = 2 s.<br>Step 3: s(2) = 40 − 20 = 20 m.<br>Step 4: a(t) = v′(t) = −10 m/s² (gravity pulls it back).", answer:"<strong>Max height 20 m at t = 2 s</strong>; acceleration −10 m/s²."},
    ],
    mistakes:["Differentiating with two variables still in the formula. Use the constraint first.","Giving x when asked for the maximum value. Substitute back; include units.","Ignoring the domain: x = 15 gives the box a 0 cm base.","Not justifying max or min with f″ or a sign check.","Substituting into s(t) to find velocity. Velocity is s′(t); acceleration is v′(t)."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Packaging engineers at companies like Nampak design cans to hold a fixed volume with the least metal — tiny savings times millions of cans. Sports scientists at the University of Pretoria's High Performance Centre use velocity and acceleration data to sharpen sprint starts."},
    activity:"A cylindrical can must hold 1 litre (1 000 cm³). Radius r cm, height h cm.<br>(a) Show that the total surface area is S = 2πr² + 2 000/r.<br>(b) Find r that makes S a minimum (2 decimal places).<br>(c) Find h and comment on the shape.",
    activityAnswer:"(a) πr²h = 1 000 → h = 1 000/(πr²). S = 2πr² + 2πrh = 2πr² + 2πr × 1 000/(πr²) = 2πr² + 2 000/r.<br>(b) S′ = 4πr − 2 000/r² = 0 → 4πr³ = 2 000 → r³ = 500/π ≈ 159.15 → <strong>r ≈ 5.42 cm</strong>. S″ = 4π + 4 000/r³ > 0, so it's a minimum.<br>(c) h = 1 000/(π × 5.42²) ≈ <strong>10.84 cm</strong>, about 2r: the least-material can is as tall as it is wide. S ≈ 553.6 cm².",
    examTip:"Part (a) often says “Show that A = …”. Even if you can't, use the given formula in later parts — those marks are still available. Justify max or min, and end in context with units. In motion, start with v(t) = s′(t).",
    keyterms:[{w:"Optimise",d:"Find the maximum or minimum value"},{w:"Constraint",d:"A fixed condition, e.g. total fencing = 100 m"},{w:"Second derivative test",d:"f″ < 0 → maximum; f″ > 0 → minimum"},{w:"Velocity",d:"Rate of change of displacement: v = s′(t)"},{w:"Acceleration",d:"Rate of change of velocity: a = v′(t)"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"A stone's height is h(t) = 30t − 5t² metres. Its velocity at t = 2 s is:",opts:["40 m/s","20 m/s","10 m/s","−10 m/s"],a:2,et:"v(t) = h′(t) = 30 − 10t, so v(2) = 30 − 20 = 10 m/s. The tempting 40 is h(2) — the height, not the velocity."},
      {q:"If A′(x) = 40 − 8x, the optimum x is:",opts:["8","40","32","5"],a:3,et:"40 − 8x = 0 → x = 5."},
      {q:"f″(x) < 0 at a stationary point means:",opts:["Maximum","Minimum","Inflection","No turning point"],a:0,et:"Concave down → maximum."},
    ],
    conclusion:"One variable, a domain, derivative = 0, justify max or min, answer in context. For motion, v = s′ and a = v′; maximum height is where v = 0."},

  5: {emoji:"🗺️", subtitle:"Enrichment · Adding up tiny pieces (beyond CAPS)",
    illusText:"Differentiation breaks things into rates. Integration adds rates back up into totals. Integration is not examined in CAPS Maths — this is a head start for university.",
    hook:{title:"How far did the taxi travel?", body:"A taxi's tracking unit saves its speed every second. The <strong>area under the speed–time graph</strong> is the distance travelled — at constant speed, just a rectangle, speed × time. But taxis speed up and slow down, so the graph curves. If v = 3t² m/s for 2 seconds, the area under the curve is <strong>8 m</strong>. Fleet-tracking companies like Cartrack turn speed data into distance this way."},
    intro:"<strong>Integration</strong> is the reverse of differentiation. The indefinite integral of xⁿ is xⁿ⁺¹/(n + 1) + C (n ≠ −1). A <strong>definite integral</strong> ∫ₐᵇ f(x) dx gives the (signed) area between the curve and the x-axis from x = a to x = b. This is <strong>enrichment</strong>: it is not part of the CAPS Mathematics exam, but it is first-year university maths for engineering, science and commerce.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"↩️ Reverse power rule", body:"Differentiation brings the power down and subtracts one, so integration does the opposite: <strong>add one to the power, then divide by the new power</strong>. ∫x² dx = x³/3 + C; ∫6x dx = 6x²/2 + C = 3x² + C.<br><br>Why + C? Because x³/3, x³/3 + 5 and x³/3 − 100 all have derivative x². The constant vanished when differentiating, so you can't know it — you write C.<br><br>Always check by differentiating your answer: d/dx(3x² + C) = 6x ✓."},
      {t:"teal", title:"📐 Area from rectangles", body:"Area comes from adding thin rectangles. Taxi example, v = 3t² from t = 0 to 2, with 4 strips of width 0.5:<br>using right-hand heights v(0.5), v(1), v(1.5), v(2) = 0.75, 3, 6.75, 12: sum 22.5 × 0.5 = 11.25 (too big);<br>using left-hand heights 0, 0.75, 3, 6.75: sum 10.5 × 0.5 = 5.25 (too small).<br><br>The true area, 8, is trapped between. With more, thinner strips both estimates squeeze onto 8 — a limit again, just like the derivative. The definite integral is that limit: ∫₀² 3t² dt = [t³]₀² = 8 − 0 = 8."},
      {t:"gold", title:"➖ Areas below the axis", body:"A definite integral counts area <strong>below</strong> the x-axis as negative. ∫₀² (x² − 4) dx = [x³/3 − 4x]₀² = 8/3 − 8 = −16/3. The negative sign says the region lies under the axis; the actual area is 16/3 ≈ 5.33 units².<br><br>Surprise: ∫₋₁¹ x³ dx = [x⁴/4]₋₁¹ = 1/4 − 1/4 = 0, yet the shaded area is 1/4 + 1/4 = ½. The two pieces cancel. To find a total area, split the integral where the graph crosses the axis and add the sizes."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"∫(4x³ − 2x) dx (easy)", body:"Term by term:<br>∫4x³ dx = 4x⁴/4 = x⁴<br>∫−2x dx = −2x²/2 = −x²<br>Check: d/dx(x⁴ − x²) = 4x³ − 2x ✓", answer:"x⁴ − x² + <strong>C</strong>."},
      {title:"Taxi distance (standard)", body:"v = 3t² m/s from t = 0 to t = 2.<br>Step 1: integrate: ∫3t² dt = 3t³/3 = t³.<br>Step 2: substitute the limits: [t³]₀² = 2³ − 0³.", answer:"Distance = <strong>8 m</strong>."},
      {title:"Area under y = x² from 0 to 3 (challenging)", body:"Step 1: estimate with 3 rectangles of width 1.<br>Right-hand heights f(1), f(2), f(3) = 1, 4, 9: area ≈ 14 (overestimate).<br>Left-hand heights f(0), f(1), f(2) = 0, 1, 4: area ≈ 5 (underestimate).<br>Midpoint heights f(0.5), f(1.5), f(2.5) = 0.25, 2.25, 6.25: area ≈ 8.75 (close).<br>Step 2: exactly: [x³/3]₀³ = 27/3 − 0.", answer:"<strong>9 units²</strong> — between 5 and 14, and the midpoint estimate 8.75 is nearly there."},
    ],
    formula:"∫xⁿ dx = xⁿ⁺¹/(n + 1) + C · ∫ₐᵇ f(x) dx = F(b) − F(a)",
    mistakes:["Forgetting + C on indefinite integrals. Different constants give the same derivative, so C must be there.","Multiplying by the new power instead of dividing: ∫x³ dx is x⁴/4, not 4x⁴.","Subtracting the limits the wrong way round: it's F(top) − F(bottom), e.g. F(2) − F(0).","Ignoring areas below the x-axis. They come out negative; for total area, split at the x-intercepts and add sizes.","Writing + C on a definite integral. The constants cancel in F(b) − F(a), so the answer is just a number."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Engineering, actuarial science, physics and economics degrees at Wits, UCT, Stellenbosch and UKZN start with integration in first year. Actuaries integrate to value pensions; Rand Water engineers integrate flow rates to know how much water reaches Gauteng from the Vaal Dam."},
    activity:"(a) Find ∫₁² 6x² dx.<br>(b) Estimate the same area with 2 strips of width 0.5, using right-hand heights. Is it too big or too small?<br>(c) Evaluate ∫₀² (x² − 4) dx and explain the sign.",
    activityAnswer:"(a) ∫6x² dx = 2x³, so [2x³]₁² = 2(8) − 2(1) = 16 − 2 = <strong>14</strong>.<br>(b) Heights f(1.5) = 6(2.25) = 13.5 and f(2) = 24: (13.5 + 24) × 0.5 = 37.5 × 0.5 = <strong>18.75</strong>. Too big, because the curve is increasing, so right-hand rectangles stick out above it.<br>(c) [x³/3 − 4x]₀² = 8/3 − 8 = <strong>−16/3</strong>. Negative because y = x² − 4 lies below the x-axis for 0 < x < 2; the area is 16/3 units².",
    examTip:"Not examined in NSC Maths. Practise these if you take IEB Advanced Programme Maths or plan to study STEM. Markers there reward the antiderivative in square brackets with the limits written in, then F(b) − F(a) shown in full.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Integral",d:"The reverse of a derivative; a total or area"},{w:"Constant of integration (C)",d:"Unknown constant added to indefinite integrals"},{w:"Definite integral",d:"Integral between limits a and b — gives a number"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"∫x³ dx =",opts:["3x² + C","x⁴ + C","4x⁴ + C","x⁴/4 + C"],a:3,et:"Add 1 to the power, then divide by the new power 4. 3x² + C is the derivative, not the integral."},
      {q:"∫₀² 2x dx =",opts:["2","8","4","0"],a:2,et:"[x²]₀² = 4 − 0 = 4."},
      {q:"The area under a speed–time graph gives:",opts:["Acceleration","Distance","Time","Speed"],a:1,et:"Speed × time = distance."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Integration reverses the power rule and adds up thin rectangles to find areas and totals; areas below the axis count as negative. It's enrichment beyond CAPS, so you're ahead for first-year university and IEB AP Maths."},
};

// ─── Module 7: Grade 12 Exam Topics ─────────────────────────────────────────
LESSON_DB.math.m7 = {
  0: {emoji:"🔄", subtitle:"Grade 12 · Undoing a function",
    illusText:"A function and its inverse are mirror images in the line y = x: (−1; 4) on f becomes (4; −1) on f⁻¹.",
    hook:{title:"Celsius to Fahrenheit and back", body:"A weather app converts 25 °C to 77 °F with F = 1.8C + 32. Your cousin in Texas wants the reverse, to check if 95 °F is a Durban-hot day. The reverse rule, C = (F − 32) ÷ 1.8, is the <strong>inverse function</strong>: 95 °F gives 35 °C. Every “convert back” button is an inverse."},
    intro:"The <strong>inverse</strong> f⁻¹ reverses f: if f(a) = b, then f⁻¹(b) = a. Its graph is the <strong>reflection of f in y = x</strong>, and domain and range swap. The inverse is a function only if f is <strong>one-to-one</strong>, so a parabola needs a <strong>restricted domain</strong>. CAPS covers the inverses of y = ax + q, y = ax² and y = bˣ.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🔀 Swap and solve", body:"The inverse turns outputs into inputs, so swap the letters: write y = f(x), swap x and y, make y the subject.<br><br>f(x) = 3x − 9 → x = 3y − 9 → y = ⅓x + 3. Check: f(5) = 6 and f⁻¹(6) = 5 ✓.<br><br>Trap: the −1 means “undo”, not “reciprocal”, so f⁻¹(x) ≠ 1 ÷ f(x)."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🪞 Reflect in y = x", body:"Swapping coordinates reflects in y = x: (−1; 4) on f(x) = 2x + 6 becomes (4; −1) on f⁻¹. Intercepts swap too.<br><br>y = 3ˣ has domain ℝ and range y > 0, so y = log₃ x has domain x > 0 and range ℝ; asymptote y = 0 becomes x = 0.<br><br>An increasing function meets its inverse on y = x, so solve f(x) = x."},
      {t:"gold", title:"✂️ One-to-one and restricting", body:"<strong>One-to-one</strong>: each y comes from one x. <strong>Horizontal-line test</strong>: if a horizontal line cuts the graph twice, it is many-to-one. y = x² fails: 2² = (−2)² = 4.<br><br>Use f⁻¹ notation only for one-to-one functions. Keep one arm: <strong>x ≥ 0</strong> (take +√) or <strong>x ≤ 0</strong> (take −√), and state it."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Linear inverse (easy)", body:"f(x) = 2x + 6. Find f⁻¹ and where f and f⁻¹ meet.", answer:"Swap: x = 2y + 6<br>Solve: y = (x − 6) ÷ 2 → <strong>f⁻¹(x) = ½x − 3</strong><br>Meet on y = x: 2x + 6 = x → x = −6 → <strong>(−6; −6)</strong>."},
      {title:"Parabola inverse (exam standard)", body:"g(x) = 2x², x ≥ 0. Find g⁻¹(x).", answer:"Swap: x = 2y²<br>y² = x ÷ 2 → y = ±√(x/2)<br>Domain x ≥ 0 becomes range y ≥ 0, so take +:<br><strong>g⁻¹(x) = √(x/2), x ≥ 0</strong>."},
      {title:"Exponential inverse (link to Logarithms)", body:"Find the inverse of y = 3ˣ with its domain and range.", answer:"Swap: x = 3ʸ<br>“y is the exponent of 3 giving x” → y = log₃ x<br>Domain and range swap:<br><strong>y = log₃ x, x > 0, y ∈ ℝ</strong>."},
    ],
    mistakes:["Writing f⁻¹(x) = 1 ÷ f(x). Fix: swap x and y and solve.","No domain restriction on a parabola, so the inverse gives two outputs. Fix: state x ≥ 0 or x ≤ 0.","Wrong root sign: for h(x) = −x², x ≤ 0, the inverse needs y ≤ 0. Fix: match the sign to the domain.","Stopping at x = 2y + 6. Fix: finish with y as the subject."],
    callout:{title:"Rands to dollars at OR Tambo", body:"The forex counter charges C = 18.50d + 75 (R18.50 per dollar plus R75 commission). Thandi has R2 000, so she needs the inverse d = (C − 75) ÷ 18.50 = 1 925 ÷ 18.50 ≈ 104.05: <strong>$104</strong>. Payroll clerks run SARS tax tables backwards the same way to find the salary behind a take-home pay."},
    activity:"(a) Find f⁻¹ for f(x) = 3x − 9 and where f meets f⁻¹.<br>(b) Find h⁻¹ for h(x) = −x², x ≤ 0, with domain and range.<br>(c) Why would h without the restriction have no inverse function?",
    activityAnswer:"(a) x = 3y − 9 → f⁻¹(x) = ⅓x + 3. On y = x: 3x − 9 = x → x = 4.5, so (4.5; 4.5). Check: f⁻¹(4.5) = 1.5 + 3 = 4.5 ✓.<br>(b) x = −y² → y = ±√(−x). Domain x ≤ 0 becomes range y ≤ 0, so h⁻¹(x) = −√(−x), domain x ≤ 0, range y ≤ 0.<br>(c) h(2) = h(−2) = −4: y = −4 cuts it twice, so it is many-to-one.",
    examTip:"Marks go to the swap step, making y the subject, the restriction (“x ≥ 0”) and a sketch with intercepts and y = x. Write “f⁻¹(x) =”, not just “y =”. For “where does f meet f⁻¹?”, solve f(x) = x.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Inverse function",d:"A function that reverses another function"},{w:"One-to-one",d:"Each output comes from exactly one input"},{w:"Line of reflection",d:"The line y = x for a function and its inverse"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"If f(x) = 4x − 8, then f⁻¹(x) =",opts:["¼x + 2","4x + 8","1/(4x − 8)","−4x + 8"],a:0,et:"x = 4y − 8 → y = (x + 8)/4 = ¼x + 2. 1/(4x − 8) is the reciprocal, a different thing: check that f⁻¹(f(3)) = f⁻¹(4) = 3 ✓."},
      {q:"(−2; 5) lies on f. Which point lies on f⁻¹?",opts:["(2; −5)","(5; −2)","(−5; 2)","(−2; −5)"],a:1,et:"Swap the coordinates. (2; −5) changes both signs, which is a rotation and not a reflection in y = x."},
      {q:"The graph of f⁻¹ is the reflection of f in:",opts:["the x-axis","the y-axis","y = x","y = −x"],a:2,et:"Inputs and outputs swap, and swapping (a; b) to (b; a) is exactly a reflection in y = x."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Swap x and y, solve for y, restrict a parabola’s domain and reflect in y = x. In the exam, state the domain and show the swap step."},

  1: {emoji:"📈", subtitle:"Grade 12 · Logarithms: the inverse of exponents",
    illusText:"log₂ 8 = 3 because 2³ = 8. A logarithm finds the exponent.",
    hook:{title:"How long until your savings double?", body:"Lerato saves R5 000 from her Shoprite till job at 8% p.a. compounded annually. When will it reach R10 000? She needs (1.08)ⁿ = 2, but n is stuck in the exponent. <strong>Logarithms</strong> bring it down. They also sit behind the Richter scale used for the 2014 Orkney earthquake."},
    intro:"A <strong>logarithm</strong> answers “what exponent?”: <strong>log_b x = y means bʸ = x</strong> (b > 0, b ≠ 1, x > 0). y = log_b x is the <strong>inverse of y = bˣ</strong>. CAPS asks for the definition, the graph for <strong>b > 1 and 0 < b < 1</strong>, and logs to find n in finance. Manipulating the log laws is <strong>not examined</strong>.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🔁 Swap the form", body:"Read log₃ 81 as “3 to the what gives 81?”: 4. Also log_b 1 = 0 and log_b b = 1.<br><br><strong>Why x > 0?</strong> A positive base to any power is positive, so no exponent gives 0 or −8: log₂ 0 is undefined.<br><br>Laws like log xⁿ = n log x explain the method but are not examined. Never write log(a + b) = log a + log b."},
      {t:"teal", title:"📉 Graphs for b > 1 and 0 < b < 1", body:"Every log graph passes through <strong>(1; 0)</strong> and <strong>(b; 1)</strong>, has domain x > 0 and asymptote x = 0.<br><br>b > 1 (log₂ x) increases: (½; −1), (2; 1), (8; 3); log₂ x ≤ 0 for 0 < x ≤ 1.<br><br>0 < b < 1 (log_½ x) decreases: (½; 1), (2; −1), (4; −2). It is log₂ x reflected in the x-axis."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🧮 Solve for the exponent", body:"bˣ = c → x = log c ÷ log b. Lerato: n = log 2 ÷ log 1.08 = 0.30103 ÷ 0.03342 ≈ 9.01.<br><br>After 9 years she has 5 000(1.08)⁹ = R9 995.02, just short, so the money passes R10 000 at the end of <strong>year 10</strong>. Interest is added only once a year, so round up in context.<br><br>The same step finds n for Annuities & Loans."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Evaluate (easy)", body:"log₂ 32.", answer:"2 to the what gives 32?<br>2⁵ = 32 → <strong>log₂ 32 = 5</strong>."},
      {title:"Sketch y = log_½ x (exam standard)", body:"Sketch y = log_½ x. For which x is y ≥ 0?", answer:"(½)⁰ = 1 → (1; 0); (½)¹ = ½ → (½; 1); (½)⁻¹ = 2 → (2; −1)<br>Asymptote x = 0; decreasing<br>On or above the x-axis between the asymptote and (1; 0):<br><strong>0 < x ≤ 1</strong>."},
      {title:"Monthly compounding (real life)", body:"R5 000 at 8% p.a. compounded monthly. When does it double?", answer:"5 000(1 + 0.08/12)ⁿ = 10 000 → (1 + 0.08/12)ⁿ = 2<br>n = log 2 ÷ log(1 + 0.08/12) = 0.30103 ÷ 0.0028857 ≈ 104.3<br>After 104 months: R9 978.87; after 105: R10 045.40<br><strong>105 months</strong> (8 years 9 months)."},
    ],
    formula:"log_b x = y ⇔ bʸ = x · bˣ = c ⇒ x = log c ÷ log b",
    mistakes:["Taking the log of 0 or a negative. Fix: check x > 0.","Writing log(a + b) = log a + log b: log 2 + log 8 = log 16, not log 10. Fix: leave sums alone.","Drawing the curve across the y-axis. Fix: draw the asymptote x = 0 first.","Rounding n down: at 104 months it has not doubled. Fix: round up in context."],
    callout:{title:"Earthquakes and vuvuzelas", body:"One Richter step is about 10 times more ground motion: Orkney’s magnitude 5.5 shook about 100 times more than a 3.5. A vuvuzela at 120 dB has 10 000 times the sound intensity of traffic at 80 dB. Council for Geoscience seismologists, sound engineers and bank actuaries use logs daily."},
    activity:"(a) Write 2⁶ = 64 in log form; evaluate log₅ 125 and log 0.01.<br>(b) Sketch y = log_⅓ x. For which x is y < 0?<br>(c) A stokvel invests R20 000 at 9% p.a. compounded annually. After how many whole years is it at least R50 000?",
    activityAnswer:"(a) log₂ 64 = 6; log₅ 125 = 3 (5³ = 125); log 0.01 = −2 (10⁻² = 0.01).<br>(b) Through (⅓; 1), (1; 0), (3; −1), decreasing, asymptote x = 0. y < 0 for <strong>x > 1</strong>.<br>(c) (1.09)ⁿ = 2.5 → n = log 2.5 ÷ log 1.09 ≈ 10.63. After 10 years R47 347.27; after 11, R51 608.53. <strong>11 years</strong>.",
    examTip:"For “find n”, isolate the bracket, then write the log step: n = log 2.5 ÷ log 1.09. That line earns the method mark. In graph questions, label (1; 0), draw the asymptote and write inequalities with correct ends (0 < x ≤ 1).",
    keyterms:[{w:"Logarithm",d:"The exponent a base must be raised to, to give a number"},{w:"Asymptote",d:"A line a graph approaches but never touches"},{w:"Inverse",d:"y = log_b x is the inverse of y = bˣ"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"log₃ 81 =",opts:["3","27","9","4"],a:3,et:"3⁴ = 81. 27 is 81 ÷ 3, a common slip: the log is the exponent, not a factor."},
      {q:"The graph of y = log₂ x passes through:",opts:["(0; 1)","(2; 0)","(1; 0)","(0; 0)"],a:2,et:"log_b 1 = 0 for every base, because b⁰ = 1. (0; 1) is on y = 2ˣ, the exponential graph, not the log graph."},
      {q:"Solve 5ˣ = 125:",opts:["x = 25","x = 3","x = 5","x = 2"],a:1,et:"5³ = 125, so x = log 125 ÷ log 5 = 3. x = 25 comes from dividing 125 by 5, which treats the exponent like a multiplier."},
    ],
    conclusion:"A logarithm finds the exponent. y = log_b x mirrors y = bˣ. In finance, isolate the bracket, take logs and round n up."},

  2: {emoji:"📐", subtitle:"Grade 12 · Compound and double angle identities",
    illusText:"cos 15° = cos(45° − 30°) = (√6 + √2) ÷ 4 ≈ 0.966: exact, without a calculator.",
    hook:{title:"The exact angle", body:"A solar-farm engineer near Upington needs cos 15° in her panel design. A calculator gives 0.9659…, but exams want an exact surd. Since 15° = 45° − 30°, <strong>compound angle identities</strong> give it."},
    intro:"<strong>Compound angles</strong>: cos(α ± β) = cos α cos β ∓ sin α sin β; sin(α ± β) = sin α cos β ± cos α sin β. <strong>Double angles</strong>: sin 2α = 2 sin α cos α; cos 2α = cos²α − sin²α = 1 − 2 sin²α = 2 cos²α − 1. CAPS: <strong>prove</strong> cos(α − β), derive the rest, then find exact values, prove identities and solve equations.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🔵 Proof of cos(α − β)", body:"P(cos α; sin α) and Q(cos β; sin β) lie on the unit circle, with angle POQ = α − β.<br><br><strong>Distance formula:</strong> PQ² = (cos α − cos β)² + (sin α − sin β)² = 2 − 2(cos α cos β + sin α sin β), using sin² + cos² = 1.<br><br><strong>Cosine rule:</strong> PQ² = 1 + 1 − 2cos(α − β).<br><br>Equate: <strong>cos(α − β) = cos α cos β + sin α sin β</strong>. This proof is examinable bookwork."},
      {t:"teal", title:"➕ Deriving the rest", body:"cos(α + β) = cos(α − (−β)); since sin(−β) = −sin β, the sign flips.<br><br>sin(α − β) = cos((90° − α) + β) = sin α cos β − cos α sin β (co-functions). Replace β with −β for sin(α + β).<br><br>Put β = α for the double angles, then use sin²α + cos²α = 1 for the other cos 2α forms."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🎯 Diagrams and equations", body:"Split angles: 15° = 45° − 30°, 75° = 45° + 30°.<br><br>Given sin θ = −5/13, <strong>draw it</strong>: x² + y² = r² gives x = ±12, and the quadrant picks the sign.<br><br>In equations, factorise rather than divide, then give the <strong>general solution</strong> with k ∈ ℤ (see Trig Equations)."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Exact value (easy)", body:"Find cos 15° without a calculator.", answer:"cos(45° − 30°) = cos 45° cos 30° + sin 45° sin 30°<br>= (√2/2)(√3/2) + (√2/2)(½)<br>= <strong>(√6 + √2) ÷ 4</strong> ≈ 0.966 ✓"},
      {title:"Diagram question (exam standard)", body:"13 sin θ + 5 = 0, θ ∈ [180°; 270°]. Find sin 2θ and cos 2θ.", answer:"sin θ = −5/13; x² = 169 − 25 = 144 → x = −12 (third quadrant)<br>cos θ = −12/13<br>sin 2θ = 2(−5/13)(−12/13) = <strong>120/169</strong><br>cos 2θ = 1 − 2(25/169) = <strong>119/169</strong>"},
      {title:"General solution (challenging)", body:"Solve sin 2x + cos x = 0.", answer:"2 sin x cos x + cos x = 0<br>cos x(2 sin x + 1) = 0 (don’t divide by cos x)<br>cos x = 0 → x = 90° + k·180°<br>sin x = −½ → reference angle 30°, quadrants III and IV → x = 210° + k·360° or 330° + k·360°<br><strong>k ∈ ℤ</strong>"},
    ],
    formula:"cos(α ± β) = cos α cos β ∓ sin α sin β · sin(α ± β) = sin α cos β ± cos α sin β · sin 2α = 2 sin α cos α · cos 2α = 1 − 2 sin²α",
    mistakes:["cos(α + β) = cos α + cos β: cos 90° = 0, but cos 60° + cos 30° ≈ 1.37. Fix: use the identity.","Wrong signs in cos(α ± β). Fix: cosine flips, sine keeps.","Dividing by cos x in sin 2x + cos x = 0 loses the solutions where cos x = 0. Fix: factorise.","cos θ = +12/13 in quadrant III. Fix: draw the quadrant."],
    callout:{title:"Waves and the grid", body:"Engineers combine waves with sin(α + β): it predicts where two cellphone signals strengthen or cancel. Eskom keeps the grid’s 50 Hz phases in step with the same maths, and solar trackers at De Aar aim panels using sums of angles."},
    activity:"(a) Find sin 75° and cos 105° exactly.<br>(b) Prove sin 2x ÷ (1 + cos 2x) = tan x.<br>(c) cos 2θ = 7/25, θ acute. Find sin θ.",
    activityAnswer:"(a) sin(45° + 30°) = (√2/2)(√3/2) + (√2/2)(½) = (√6 + √2)/4. cos(60° + 45°) = (½)(√2/2) − (√3/2)(√2/2) = (√2 − √6)/4.<br>(b) LHS = 2 sin x cos x ÷ (2cos²x) = sin x ÷ cos x = tan x = RHS.<br>(c) 1 − 2 sin²θ = 7/25 → sin²θ = 9/25 → sin θ = 3/5.",
    examTip:"Expect the cos(α − β) proof as bookwork: draw and label the unit-circle diagram. In proofs, work on one side and name each identity; marks come per step. Leave exact values as surds.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Compound angle",d:"An angle written as a sum or difference, e.g. α + β"},{w:"Double angle",d:"An angle of the form 2α"},{w:"Identity",d:"An equation true for all valid values of the variable"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"sin 2α equals:",opts:["2 sin α","2 sin α cos α","sin²α − cos²α","sin α + cos α"],a:1,et:"Put β = α in sin(α + β). 2 sin α is wrong: try α = 90°, where sin 180° = 0 but 2 sin 90° = 2."},
      {q:"cos(α + β) =",opts:["cos α cos β + sin α sin β","cos α cos β − sin α sin β","sin α cos β + cos α sin β","cos α + cos β"],a:1,et:"Replace β with −β in cos(α − β): sin(−β) = −sin β flips the sign. The plus version is cos(α − β)."},
      {q:"cos 2α can also be written as:",opts:["1 − 2 sin²α","2 sin α","1 + sin²α","cos²α + sin²α"],a:0,et:"cos²α − sin²α = (1 − sin²α) − sin²α. cos²α + sin²α is just 1."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Prove cos(α − β), derive the rest, split into special angles and factorise instead of dividing. Proofs earn marks step by step."},

  3: {emoji:"⭕", subtitle:"Grade 12 · Analytical geometry: circles and tangents",
    illusText:"Circle centre (a; b), radius r: (x − a)² + (y − b)² = r². The tangent is perpendicular to the radius.",
    hook:{title:"How far does the cellphone tower reach?", body:"A tower outside Mthatha covers everything within 5 km. With the tower at (2; −1), the edge of coverage is a <strong>circle</strong>. Is the school at (5; 3) covered? Where does the road cross the signal edge? <strong>Analytical geometry</strong> answers with coordinates."},
    intro:"You already have <strong>distance</strong>, <strong>midpoint</strong>, <strong>gradient</strong> and m₁ × m₂ = −1 (see Distance, Midpoint & Gradient). Grade 12 adds the circle <strong>(x − a)² + (y − b)² = r²</strong>, <strong>completing the square</strong>, <strong>tangents</strong> (perpendicular to the radius), line–circle intersections, and points inside or outside.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"⭕ The circle equation", body:"Every point on the circle is r from the centre, so square the distance formula: (x − a)² + (y − b)² = r². Signs flip: centre (2; −1) gives (y + 1)².<br><br>Substitute a point: less than r² is <strong>inside</strong>, equal is <strong>on</strong>, more is <strong>outside</strong>. School (5; 3): 9 + 16 = 25 = r², exactly on the edge."},
      {t:"teal", title:"📐 Tangent ⟂ radius", body:"The radius to the contact point is the shortest distance to the tangent, so it is perpendicular (as in Circle Theorems).<br><br>Find the radius gradient, flip and change sign, use y − y₁ = m(x − x₁). For x² + y² = 25 at (3; 4): radius m = 4/3, tangent m = −3/4.<br><br>Tangent length from an outside point P: √(d² − r²), by Pythagoras."},
      {t:"gold", title:"✂️ Line meets circle", body:"Substitute the line into the circle to get a quadratic. Two roots: a <strong>chord</strong>. One repeated root (Δ = 0): a tangent. Δ < 0: it misses.<br><br>This is the nature of roots again (see Quadratic Formula & Roots)."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Circle and tangent (easy)", body:"Centre (2; −1), through (5; 3). Find the circle and the tangent at (5; 3).", answer:"r² = 3² + 4² = 25 → <strong>(x − 2)² + (y + 1)² = 25</strong><br>Radius m = 4/3 → tangent m = −3/4<br>y − 3 = −¾(x − 5) → <strong>y = −¾x + 27/4</strong>"},
      {title:"Complete the square (exam standard)", body:"x² + y² − 6x + 4y − 12 = 0.", answer:"(x² − 6x) + (y² + 4y) = 12<br>(x − 3)² − 9 + (y + 2)² − 4 = 12<br>(x − 3)² + (y + 2)² = 25<br><strong>Centre (3; −2), r = 5</strong>"},
      {title:"Chord and tangents (challenging)", body:"y = x + 2 meets x² + y² = 20 at A and B. Find A, B, the tangents there, where they meet, and AB.", answer:"x² + (x + 2)² = 20 → x² + 2x − 8 = 0 → (x + 4)(x − 2) = 0<br><strong>A(2; 4), B(−4; −2)</strong><br>At A: radius m = 2 → <strong>y = −½x + 5</strong><br>At B: radius m = ½ → <strong>y = −2x − 10</strong><br>−½x + 5 = −2x − 10 → x = −10 → <strong>(−10; 10)</strong><br>AB = √(36 + 36) = <strong>6√2</strong>"},
    ],
    formula:"d = √[(x₂ − x₁)² + (y₂ − y₁)²] · m = (y₂ − y₁)/(x₂ − x₁) · (x − a)² + (y − b)² = r² · m₁ × m₂ = −1",
    mistakes:["Centre (2; −1) written as (y − 1)². Fix: signs flip in the brackets.","Not adding the completed-square constants to the right. Fix: add to both sides.","Using the radius gradient for the tangent. Fix: −1 ÷ m_radius.","Giving r = 25 from r² = 25. Fix: take the root."],
    callout:{title:"Finding a phone", body:"Each tower’s signal gives a circle of possible positions for a phone; where three circles meet, the phone is found. GPS does the same with spheres. MTN and Vodacom engineers plan coverage this way, and surveyors set out curved roads near King Shaka Airport with tangents ⟂ radii."},
    activity:"A(−1; 2) and B(5; 6) are ends of a diameter.<br>(a) Find the centre, radius and equation.<br>(b) Find the tangent at B.<br>(c) Is P(6; 1) inside, on or outside? If outside, find the tangent length from P.",
    activityAnswer:"(a) Centre = midpoint (2; 4). r² = 3² + 2² = 13. (x − 2)² + (y − 4)² = 13.<br>(b) Radius m = 2/3 → tangent m = −3/2. y − 6 = −3/2(x − 5) → y = −3/2 x + 27/2.<br>(c) (6 − 2)² + (1 − 4)² = 16 + 9 = 25 > 13: <strong>outside</strong>. Tangent length = √(25 − 13) = <strong>2√3</strong>.",
    examTip:"Sketch the centre, radius and tangent first. Write the radius gradient before the tangent gradient; it earns its own mark. Leave lengths as surds. “Show the line is a tangent”: substitute and show Δ = 0.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Tangent",d:"A line that touches a circle at exactly one point"},{w:"Completing the square",d:"Rewriting x² + bx as (x + b/2)² − (b/2)²"},{w:"Chord",d:"A line segment joining two points on a circle"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"The centre of (x + 3)² + (y − 4)² = 16 is:",opts:["(3; −4)","(−3; 4)","(3; 4)","(−3; −4)"],a:1,et:"(x + 3) = (x − (−3)), so a = −3. The radius is 4, not 16, another favourite trap."},
      {q:"A radius has gradient 2. The tangent at its endpoint has gradient:",opts:["2","−2","½","−½"],a:3,et:"Perpendicular: 2 × (−½) = −1. −2 only changes the sign; you must also flip the fraction."},
      {q:"Distance between (1; 2) and (4; 6):",opts:["5","7","25","√7"],a:0,et:"√(3² + 4²) = √25 = 5, the famous 3–4–5 triangle. 7 adds the differences instead of using Pythagoras."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Complete the square for the centre and radius, use tangent ⟂ radius, and substitute to find intersections. Sketch first and show the gradient step."},

  4: {emoji:"📉", subtitle:"Grade 12 · Regression and correlation",
    illusText:"ŷ = a + bx: the line of best fit through a scatter plot. r tells you how strong the link is.",
    hook:{title:"Do extra study hours really pay off?", body:"A Grade 12 class in Soshanguve plots extra study hours against test marks; the points rise left to right. How strong is the link, and what might 3½ hours predict? <strong>Regression</strong> fits the line; <strong>correlation</strong> measures the fit."},
    intro:"Draw a <strong>scatter plot</strong> (independent variable on x). Decide by eye: <strong>linear, quadratic or exponential</strong>? For linear data, find the <strong>least squares line</strong> ŷ = a + bx and <strong>r</strong> (−1 ≤ r ≤ 1) by calculator. <strong>Interpolation</strong> (inside the data) is reasonable; <strong>extrapolation</strong> is risky. Correlation is not causation.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"📍 Choosing a model", body:"Look for direction, strength, outliers and shape. A straight band suggests linear. Rise-then-fall (too little study, then burnout) suggests quadratic. Faster and faster growth (a viral TikTok’s views) suggests exponential.<br><br>CAPS asks you to choose intuitively; you calculate only the linear model."},
      {t:"teal", title:"📏 Least squares", body:"The least squares line makes the <strong>sum of squared vertical distances</strong> from the points as small as possible. Squaring stops errors cancelling.<br><br>b = change in ŷ per unit of x; a = prediction at x = 0. In the study data, b = 7.5 means about 7.5 extra marks for each extra hour per week.<br><br>An outlier drags the line towards itself, changing a and b, and weakens r."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🔗 Reading r", body:"Near 1: strong positive. Near −1: strong negative. Near 0: no <em>linear</em> link. Rough guide: |r| ≥ 0.8 strong, 0.5–0.8 moderate.<br><br>Ice-cream sales and drownings both rise in a Durban December because of summer, not each other. Say “is associated with”, never “causes”."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Regression line (easy)", body:"Hours x: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5. Marks y: 40, 50, 55, 65, 70. Predict for 3.5 hours.", answer:"x̄ = 3; ȳ = 56<br>b = 75 ÷ 10 = 7.5; a = 56 − 7.5(3) = 33.5<br><strong>ŷ = 33.5 + 7.5x</strong>, r ≈ 0.99 (very strong positive)<br>ŷ(3.5) = <strong>59.75 ≈ 60%</strong> (interpolation)"},
      {title:"Diesel engine (exam standard, CAPS)", body:"Speed x (rpm): 400, 500, 600, 700, 750. Power y (hp): 580, 1 030, 1 420, 1 880, 2 100. Find the line and r.", answer:"STAT mode, A + BX: a = −1 145.79, b = 4.32<br><strong>ŷ = −1 145.79 + 4.32x</strong><br>r = 0.9997 ≈ <strong>1.00</strong>: an almost perfect positive linear relationship, so predictions from this line are trustworthy inside the data range."},
      {title:"Predictions (challenging)", body:"Estimate the power at 800 rpm and the speed for 1 200 hp.", answer:"ŷ = −1 145.79 + 3 456 ≈ <strong>2 310 hp</strong> (≈ 2 309 with unrounded values): outside 400–750, so <strong>extrapolation</strong><br>1 200 = −1 145.79 + 4.32x → x = 2 345.79 ÷ 4.32 ≈ <strong>543 rpm</strong>: <strong>interpolation</strong>"},
    ],
    formula:"ŷ = a + bx · −1 ≤ r ≤ 1",
    mistakes:["Swapping x and y on the calculator. Fix: x is the independent variable.","Claiming cause. Fix: write “is associated with”.","Extrapolating far: 12 study hours predicts 123%. Fix: flag extrapolation.","Reading r ≈ 0 as no relationship; a parabola can give r ≈ 0. Fix: say “no linear relationship”."],
    callout:{title:"Insurers and supermarkets", body:"Insurers can regress claims against age to help set premiums. A supermarket might regress sales against temperature and payday to decide how much bread and cold drink to stock. Weather forecasters use correlation to check how well forecasts match what happened. Data scientists who explain r plainly are in demand in Johannesburg."},
    activity:"Taxi fares from Bree rank: distance x (km) 5, 10, 15, 20, 25, 30; fare y (R) 12, 15, 20, 22, 27, 30.<br>(a) Find the regression line and r.<br>(b) Estimate the fare for 18 km. Interpolation or extrapolation?<br>(c) A route of 12 km for R40 is added. What happens to b and r?",
    activityAnswer:"(a) x̄ = 17.5, ȳ = 21. Calculator: a = 8.2, b ≈ 0.73: ŷ = 8.2 + 0.73x; r ≈ 0.996, very strong positive.<br>(b) ŷ = 8.2 + 0.73(18) = R21.34 ≈ <strong>R21</strong>; 18 is inside 5–30, so <strong>interpolation</strong>.<br>(c) Now a ≈ 15.40, b ≈ 0.50, r ≈ 0.46. The outlier pulls the line up, flattens it and drops r from very strong to weak.",
    examTip:"Give a, b and r to two decimals and write ŷ = a + bx with numbers substituted. Interpret r in words (“very strong positive”). For predictions, show the substitution and name it interpolation or extrapolation: that comment is often a mark.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Regression line",d:"The line of best fit ŷ = a + bx"},{w:"Correlation coefficient",d:"r, the strength of a linear relationship"},{w:"Extrapolation",d:"Predicting outside the range of the data"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"r = −0.92 shows:",opts:["Weak positive","Strong positive","No correlation","Strong negative"],a:3,et:"Close to −1: as x rises, y falls, and tightly. The size 0.92 shows strength; the minus sign shows direction, so it is not positive."},
      {q:"In ŷ = 12 + 3x, the slope is:",opts:["12","15","3","x"],a:2,et:"b is the coefficient of x. 12 is a, the intercept: the prediction when x = 0."},
      {q:"Predicting outside the data range is:",opts:["Interpolation","Extrapolation","Correlation","Regression"],a:1,et:"“Extra” means outside. Interpolation stays between the smallest and largest x. Extrapolating a child’s growth from age 2 to 10 predicts an adult of about 4 m."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Plot, choose a model, fit ŷ = a + bx and interpret r in words. Round to two decimals and label every prediction as interpolation or extrapolation."},

  5: {emoji:"🧊", subtitle:"Grade 12 · Cubic polynomials and the factor theorem",
    illusText:"f(1) = 0, so (x − 1) is a factor: x³ − 2x² − 5x + 6 = (x − 1)(x − 3)(x + 2).",
    hook:{title:"Finding where the graph crosses", body:"A Transnet engineer modelling the sag of a crane beam in Durban harbour gets a cubic. She needs its roots. Guessing is slow, but the <strong>factor theorem</strong> turns one lucky guess into the whole factorisation."},
    intro:"A <strong>cubic</strong> is f(x) = ax³ + bx² + cx + d. <strong>Remainder theorem</strong>: dividing by (x − p) leaves f(p). <strong>Factor theorem</strong>: if f(p) = 0, (x − p) is a factor. Method: test divisors of d, divide, factorise the quadratic. CAPS also asks you to solve cubics and find unknown coefficients.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🧪 Remainder and factor theorems", body:"f(x) = (x − p)·q(x) + R. Put x = p: the bracket is 0, so f(p) = R. If R = 0, (x − p) is a factor.<br><br>For f(x) = x³ − 2x² − 5x + 6: f(2) = 8 − 8 − 10 + 6 = −4 (remainder); f(1) = 0, so (x − 1) is a factor.<br><br>Trap: for (x + 2), test f(−2)."},
      {t:"teal", title:"➗ Inspection or synthetic division", body:"<strong>Inspection:</strong> (x − 1)(x² + bx − 6). The x²-term gives b − 1 = −2, so b = −1.<br><br><strong>Synthetic</strong> by 1 on 1 −2 −5 6: bring down 1; 1 − 2 = −1; −1 − 5 = −6; −6 + 6 = 0. Quotient x² − x − 6, remainder 0 ✓.<br><br>Always check by expanding: (x − 1)(x² − x − 6) = x³ − 2x² − 5x + 6 ✓."},
      {t:"gold", title:"📊 Solve, sketch, find unknowns", body:"Solve: set each factor to 0. Sketch: roots, y-intercept d, turning points from f′(x) = 0 (see Turning Points); a > 0 rises to the right.<br><br>Given a factor or remainder with an unknown, substitute p and set f(p) equal to 0 or the remainder."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Factor theorem (easy)", body:"Show (x − 1) is a factor of f(x) = x³ − 2x² − 5x + 6.", answer:"f(1) = 1 − 2 − 5 + 6 = <strong>0</strong><br>So (x − 1) is a factor."},
      {title:"Factorise and solve (exam standard)", body:"Solve x³ − 2x² − 5x + 6 = 0.", answer:"(x − 1) is a factor (Example 1), so divide:<br>(x − 1)(x² − x − 6) = 0<br>Factorise the quadratic: (x − 1)(x − 3)(x + 2) = 0<br>Set each factor to zero:<br><strong>x = 1, 3 or −2</strong>"},
      {title:"Unknown coefficient (challenging)", body:"(x − 1) is a factor of 2x³ + px² − 7x + 6. Find p and factorise.", answer:"f(1) = 2 + p − 7 + 6 = 0 → <strong>p = −1</strong><br>2x³ − x² − 7x + 6 = (x − 1)(2x² + x − 6)<br>Check: 2x³ + x² − 6x − 2x² − x + 6 ✓<br><strong>= (x − 1)(2x − 3)(x + 2)</strong>"},
    ],
    formula:"f(p) = remainder on dividing by (x − p) · f(p) = 0 ⇔ (x − p) is a factor",
    mistakes:["Testing f(−p) for (x − p). Fix: f(3) = 0 means (x − 3).","Division slips: a wrong middle term ruins the quadratic. Fix: expand to check.","Giving roots when asked to factorise, or brackets when asked to solve. Fix: factors are brackets; roots are numbers.","Setting f(p) = 0 when a remainder was given. Fix: set f(p) = remainder."],
    callout:{title:"Boxes and roller coasters", body:"A Nampak designer cuts squares of side x from a 30 cm × 20 cm sheet to fold a box: V = x(30 − 2x)(20 − 2x), a cubic. Solving V = 1 000 finds a one-litre box. Engineers join cubic curves for smooth roller-coaster track at Gold Reef City."},
    activity:"(a) Factorise g(x) = x³ + 2x² − 5x − 6.<br>(b) Solve g(x) = 0 and give the y-intercept.<br>(c) x³ + kx² + x − 3 leaves remainder 15 when divided by (x − 2). Find k.",
    activityAnswer:"(a) g(−1) = −1 + 2 + 5 − 6 = 0, so (x + 1) is a factor: g(x) = (x + 1)(x² + x − 6) = (x + 1)(x + 3)(x − 2).<br>(b) x = −1, −3 or 2; y-intercept g(0) = −6; a > 0, so the graph rises to the right.<br>(c) f(2) = 8 + 4k + 2 − 3 = 15 → 4k = 8 → <strong>k = 2</strong>.",
    examTip:"Write the substitution in full: “f(1) = 1 − 2 − 5 + 6 = 0, so (x − 1) is a factor”. That sentence earns the mark. Show the quadratic quotient. For “solve”, end with x-values.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Factor theorem",d:"f(p) = 0 means (x − p) is a factor"},{w:"Remainder theorem",d:"The remainder on dividing by (x − p) is f(p)"},{w:"Cubic",d:"A polynomial of degree 3"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"If f(3) = 0, a factor of f(x) is:",opts:["(x − 3)","(x + 3)","(3x)","(x − 1/3)"],a:0,et:"x − 3 = 0 when x = 3. (x + 3) would need f(−3) = 0: the root and the bracket’s number have opposite signs."},
      {q:"The remainder when f(x) = x³ + 1 is divided by (x + 1):",opts:["2","0","−2","1"],a:1,et:"f(−1) = −1 + 1 = 0, so (x + 1) is a factor: x³ + 1 = (x + 1)(x² − x + 1). The answer 2 comes from using f(1)."},
      {q:"The y-intercept of f(x) = 2x³ − x + 7 is:",opts:["2","−1","7","0"],a:2,et:"f(0) = 7. Every term with x vanishes at x = 0. 2 is the leading coefficient, which controls the shape, not the intercept."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Find a root with the factor theorem, divide, factorise the quadratic, then solve or sketch. Show f(p) = 0 in full."},
};

// ─── Module 8: More CAPS Topics ─────────────────────────────────────────────
LESSON_DB.math.m8 = {
  0: {emoji:"🛢️", subtitle:"Grade 10 · Surface area and volume of prisms and cylinders",
    illusText:"A 2 m wide, 1.6 m tall water tank holds about 5 000 litres.",
    hook:{title:"How much water does the tank hold?", body:"Drive through Gqeberha after the drought scares and you’ll see plastic water tanks beside many houses. A tank 2 m wide and 1.6 m tall — how many litres does it hold, and how much plastic does it take to make? <strong>Volume</strong> gives the capacity; <strong>surface area</strong> gives the material."},
    intro:"A <strong>right prism</strong> has the same cross-section all the way through. <strong>Volume = area of base × height</strong>; <strong>surface area = sum of all the faces</strong> (draw the net). Cylinder: V = πr²h, SA = 2πr² + 2πrh (closed). Units: <strong>1 m³ = 1 000 litres</strong>, 1 cm³ = 1 ml. CAPS also asks what happens when you multiply one dimension, or all of them, by k.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"📦 Volume = base × height", body:"Stack R5 coins: each is a thin circle, and the pile’s volume is one coin’s area times the height. So <strong>every</strong> right prism uses V = (area of base) × height: a box stacks rectangles, a tent stacks triangles, a tank stacks circles.<br><br>Mini-step: a triangular prism with triangle base 4 cm, height 3 cm and length 10 cm: V = ½ × 4 × 3 × 10 = 60 cm³.<br><br>Trap: the triangle’s height and the prism’s length are different lengths — label them h and H."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🗺️ Surface area from the net", body:"Unfold the solid and add the pieces. A cylinder unrolls into two circles and a rectangle 2πr wide — the label on a tin of pilchards — so the curved area is 2πrh.<br><br>An open-top tank has only <strong>one</strong> circle: SA = πr² + 2πrh.<br><br>Units: 1 m² = 10 000 cm² and 1 m³ = 1 000 000 cm³. Convert lengths <strong>first</strong>."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🔍 Scaling by k", body:"One dimension × k: volume × k. A box twice as long holds twice as much.<br><br><strong>All</strong> dimensions × k: area × k², volume × k³. Double every side and the volume is 2³ = 8 times bigger.<br><br>Watch the cylinder: r is squared in πr²h, so doubling <strong>only the radius</strong> multiplies the volume by 4, while doubling only the height multiplies it by 2. In Spheres, Cones & Pyramids, r³ scales even faster."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Camping tent (easy)", body:"A tent is a triangular prism 2 m long. Each triangular end has base 1.6 m, height 0.6 m and sloping sides of 1 m. Find V and the closed surface area.", answer:"Triangle area = ½ × 1.6 × 0.6 = 0.48 m²<br>V = 0.48 × 2 = <strong>0.96 m³</strong><br>Faces: two triangles 2 × 0.48 = 0.96 m²; floor 1.6 × 2 = 3.2 m²; two sloping sides 2 × (1 × 2) = 4 m²<br>SA = 0.96 + 3.2 + 4 = <strong>8.16 m²</strong>"},
      {title:"Cylinder (exam standard)", body:"r = 7 cm, h = 10 cm. Find V and the closed surface area.", answer:"V = πr²h = π × 49 × 10 = 490π ≈ <strong>1 539 cm³</strong> (about 1.5 litres)<br>SA = 2πr² + 2πrh = 98π + 140π = 238π ≈ <strong>748 cm²</strong><br>Open top instead: 49π + 140π = 189π ≈ 594 cm²"},
      {title:"Water tank (real life)", body:"A tank has diameter 2 m and height 1.6 m. (a) Find its capacity in litres. (b) A bigger model doubles only the radius. What is its capacity?", answer:"(a) r = 2 ÷ 2 = 1 m<br>V = π × 1² × 1.6 = 1.6π ≈ 5.027 m³<br>× 1 000 → <strong>≈ 5 027 litres</strong><br>(b) r = 2 m: V = π × 4 × 1.6 = 6.4π ≈ 20.106 m³ ≈ <strong>20 106 litres</strong><br>Radius × 2 → volume × 4, not × 2."},
    ],
    formula:"Prism: V = base area × h · Cylinder: V = πr²h, SA = 2πr² + 2πrh · 1 m³ = 1 000 L",
    mistakes:["Using the diameter as the radius. Fix: halve it first.","Mixing cm and m. Fix: convert every length to one unit before calculating.","Saying doubling every side doubles the volume. It multiplies it by 2³ = 8. Fix: all × k → V × k³.","Counting two circles for an open tank. Fix: tick off each face of the net.","Rounding early. Fix: keep π (e.g. 238π) and round once."],
    callout:{title:"Where you’ll use this", body:"A plumber in Mthatha uses V = πr²h to check that a “5 000 L” tank really holds that much. Packaging engineers at Western Cape canneries choose the radius and height that hold a tin’s contents with the least metal, because surface area is the material bill. Engineers size reservoirs and farmers size silos the same way."},
    activity:"Measure a 330 ml cooldrink can: r ≈ 3.3 cm and h ≈ 11.5 cm.<br>(a) Calculate its volume and compare it with 330 ml.<br>(b) Calculate its closed surface area.<br>(c) A new can doubles the radius but keeps the height. Find its volume.",
    activityAnswer:"(a) V = π × 10.89 × 11.5 ≈ <strong>393 cm³</strong> = 393 ml — more than 330 ml, because the can isn’t full to the top and isn’t a perfect cylinder.<br>(b) SA = 2π(10.89) + 2π(3.3)(11.5) = 97.68π ≈ <strong>307 cm²</strong>.<br>(c) Radius × 2 → volume × 4: 4 × 393.4 ≈ <strong>1 574 cm³</strong>.",
    examTip:"Write the formula, substitute with units, and round only at the end — each step earns a method mark. In scaling questions, show the factor (k, k² or k³) and why.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Prism",d:"A solid with the same cross-section throughout"},{w:"Net",d:"A 2D shape that folds into a 3D solid"},{w:"Capacity",d:"How much a container holds"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"1 m³ equals:",opts:["100 litres","1 000 litres","10 000 litres","1 litre"],a:1,et:"A cube 1 m on each side holds 1 000 one-litre cartons (10 × 10 × 10). 100 litres is the common slip."},
      {q:"Volume of a cylinder:",opts:["2πrh","πr²h","4πr²","⅓πr²h"],a:1,et:"Base area (πr²) × height. 2πrh is only the curved side, ⅓πr²h is a cone and 4πr² is a sphere’s surface."},
      {q:"All dimensions × 3. Surface area is multiplied by:",opts:["3","6","9","27"],a:2,et:"Area scales by k² = 9. 27 is the volume factor (k³) — the common wrong answer."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Volume = base area × height; surface area = the faces of the net. One dimension × k gives volume × k; all × k gives area × k², volume × k³. Exams: formula, substitution, units, round last."},

  1: {emoji:"⚽", subtitle:"Grade 10–11 · Spheres, cones and pyramids",
    illusText:"Cone and pyramid: ⅓ × base area × height. Sphere: V = ⁴⁄₃πr³.",
    hook:{title:"The ice-cream cone question", body:"A café soft-serve cone looks generous, but it holds exactly one third of a cylinder with the same base and height. A rondavel’s thatched roof is a cone, a soccer ball is a sphere, and many church roofs are pyramids. All use the Grade 10–11 formulas for <strong>spheres, cones and pyramids</strong>."},
    intro:"<strong>Sphere</strong>: V = ⁴⁄₃πr³, SA = 4πr². <strong>Cone</strong>: V = ⅓πr²h, SA = πr² + πrs, with <strong>slant height</strong> s = √(r² + h²). <strong>Pyramid</strong> (square or equilateral-triangle base): V = ⅓ × base area × h; SA = base + triangular faces. <strong>Hemisphere</strong>: V = ⅔πr³, curved SA = 2πr². Composite solids: add volumes, count only outside surfaces.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🔺 Why one third?", body:"Fill a cone with water and pour it into a cylinder with the same base and height: it takes exactly three cones. The same is true for a pyramid and its prism. So V = ⅓ × (base area) × h.<br><br>Mini-step: a cone with r = 3 cm and h = 4 cm: V = ⅓π(9)(4) = 12π ≈ 37.7 cm³.<br><br>For an equilateral-triangle base with side a, the base area is (√3/4)a². Side 4: (√3/4)(16) ≈ 6.93."},
      {t:"teal", title:"📐 Two heights", body:"The <strong>vertical height h</strong> goes from the apex straight down to the centre of the base; it goes in the <strong>volume</strong>. The <strong>slant height s</strong> runs down the outside surface; it goes in the <strong>surface area</strong>.<br><br>Both come from Pythagoras. Cone: s = √(r² + h²). Square pyramid with base edge b: s = √((b/2)² + h²), using half the base edge.<br><br>Trap: using h in πrs. A cone with r = 3, h = 4 has s = 5, so the curved area is 15π, not 12π."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🧩 Composite solids", body:"Real objects are built from pieces: a silo is a cylinder with a cone roof, a capsule is a cylinder with two hemispheres.<br><br><strong>Volume:</strong> add the pieces. <strong>Surface area:</strong> add only what you could paint. Where a hemisphere sits on a cylinder, the joining circle is hidden, so leave out both circles.<br><br>Spheres scale fast: double r and the volume is 2³ = 8 times bigger, the k³ rule from Surface Area & Volume."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Sphere (easy)", body:"A ball has radius 6 cm. Find its volume and surface area.", answer:"V = ⁴⁄₃π(6³) = ⁴⁄₃π(216) = 288π ≈ <strong>905 cm³</strong><br>SA = 4π(6²) = 144π ≈ <strong>452 cm²</strong>"},
      {title:"Square pyramid (exam standard)", body:"A roof is a right pyramid with a square base of side 6 m and vertical height 4 m. Find its volume and total surface area (base included).", answer:"V = ⅓ × 6² × 4 = ⅓ × 36 × 4 = <strong>48 m³</strong><br>Slant height of a face: s = √(3² + 4²) = √25 = 5 m (half the base edge, 3 m)<br>One face = ½ × 6 × 5 = 15 m²; four faces = 60 m²<br>SA = 36 + 60 = <strong>96 m²</strong>"},
      {title:"CAPS composite solid (challenging)", body:"A cylinder (r = 2 cm, h = 10 cm) has a hemisphere on one end and a cone of height 2 cm on the other. Find the total volume and the outside surface area.", answer:"Cylinder: π(2²)(10) = 40π<br>Hemisphere: ⅔π(2³) = 16π/3<br>Cone: ⅓π(2²)(2) = 8π/3<br>V = 40π + 24π/3 = 48π ≈ <strong>151 cm³</strong><br>Surfaces: cylinder side 2π(2)(10) = 40π; hemisphere 2π(2²) = 8π; cone s = √(4 + 4) = 2√2, so π(2)(2√2) = 4√2π<br>SA = (48 + 4√2)π ≈ <strong>169 cm²</strong> (no flat circles — they are hidden)"},
    ],
    formula:"Sphere: V = ⁴⁄₃πr³, SA = 4πr² · Cone: V = ⅓πr²h, SA = πr² + πrs · Pyramid: V = ⅓ × base × h",
    mistakes:["Using h instead of s in a surface area. Fix: label both; s goes on the surface.","Forgetting the ⅓ in cone and pyramid volumes. Fix: write ⅓ first.","Cubing the diameter in ⁴⁄₃πr³ — eight times too big. Fix: halve first.","Using the full base edge for a pyramid’s slant height. Fix: use b/2.","Adding hidden circles in a composite solid. Fix: count only outside surfaces."],
    callout:{title:"Where you’ll use this", body:"A thatcher in the Eastern Cape prices a rondavel roof per square metre. A cone roof with r = 4 m and h = 3 m has s = 5 m and area πrs = 20π ≈ 62.8 m²; at R300 per m² that’s about R18 850. Free State grain silos with cone roofs are sized with πr²h + ⅓πr²h."},
    activity:"A silo is a cylinder (r = 3 m, h = 10 m) with a cone roof (h = 2 m).<br>(a) Find its total volume.<br>(b) Find the area of the cone roof.<br>(c) Find the roof plus curved wall area (no floor).",
    activityAnswer:"(a) Cylinder: π(9)(10) = 90π. Cone: ⅓π(9)(2) = 6π. V = 96π ≈ <strong>301.6 m³</strong>.<br>(b) s = √(3² + 2²) = √13 ≈ 3.61 m. Roof = πrs = 3√13π ≈ <strong>34.0 m²</strong>.<br>(c) Wall = 2π(3)(10) = 60π ≈ 188.5 m². Total ≈ 188.5 + 34.0 = <strong>222.5 m²</strong>.",
    examTip:"Formulas are usually given in the question; the marks are for choosing the right lengths. Label r, h and s on a sketch and show the Pythagoras step for s. Keep π until the last line.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Slant height",d:"Distance from the apex to the edge of the base, along the surface"},{w:"Hemisphere",d:"Half of a sphere"},{w:"Apex",d:"The top point of a cone or pyramid"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"Volume of a cone:",opts:["πr²h","⅓πr²h","⁴⁄₃πr³","2πrh"],a:1,et:"One third of the cylinder with the same base and height. πr²h is the full cylinder — the usual slip."},
      {q:"A cone has r = 6, h = 8. The slant height is:",opts:["10","14","48","√28"],a:0,et:"√(36 + 64) = 10. Adding 6 + 8 = 14 ignores Pythagoras; 6, 8, 10 is the 3-4-5 triangle doubled."},
      {q:"Surface area of a sphere:",opts:["2πr²","4πr²","πr²","⁴⁄₃πr²"],a:1,et:"4πr² — exactly four times the area of a circle with the same radius. Archimedes asked for a sphere inside a cylinder on his tomb to honour results like this."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Cones and pyramids hold one third of their cylinder or prism. Use h for volume, s for surface area, and count only outside surfaces in composite solids."},

  2: {emoji:"🔢", subtitle:"Grade 11 · Quadratic number patterns",
    illusText:"3, 8, 15, 24 … first differences 5, 7, 9 · second difference 2 → Tₙ = n² + 2n.",
    hook:{title:"How many handshakes?", body:"At a matric farewell, everyone shakes hands with everyone else once. 2 people: 1 handshake; 3 people: 3; 4: 6; 5: 10. The differences grow by 1 each time — a <strong>quadratic pattern</strong>. It also tells FIFA that a World Cup group of 4 teams needs 6 matches."},
    intro:"In a <strong>quadratic pattern</strong> the <strong>first differences</strong> change but the <strong>second differences are constant</strong>. General term: <strong>Tₙ = an² + bn + c</strong>, with <strong>2a = second difference</strong>, <strong>3a + b = T₂ − T₁</strong> and <strong>a + b + c = T₁</strong>. Use Tₙ to find terms, positions, and where the first difference has a given value.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"📊 Why the second difference is 2a", body:"Write out the first terms of Tₙ = an² + bn + c:<br>T₁ = a + b + c, T₂ = 4a + 2b + c, T₃ = 9a + 3b + c.<br>First differences: T₂ − T₁ = 3a + b and T₃ − T₂ = 5a + b. Second difference: 2a.<br><br>That is where the three equations come from. If the first differences are constant, a = 0 and the pattern is linear."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🔗 The first differences form a linear pattern", body:"The first differences 5; 7; 9 of 3; 8; 15; 24 form a linear pattern. In general Tₙ₊₁ − Tₙ = 2an + a + b.<br><br>So “between which terms is the difference 21?” is a linear equation; for Tₙ = n² + 3 it is 2n + 1 = 21.<br><br>Trap: the answer n means Tₙ and Tₙ₊₁ — name both."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🎯 Largest or smallest term", body:"Tₙ is a parabola sampled at n = 1, 2, 3, … so if a > 0 and the turning point is at n > 1, the terms fall to a minimum, then rise.<br><br>Mini-step: Tₙ = n² − 10n + 30. Turning point at n = −b/(2a) = 5, and T₅ = 25 − 50 + 30 = 5, the smallest term. If −b/(2a) is not a whole number, test the nearest whole n on each side.<br><br>It is the same turning-point idea as in Parabola y=ax²+q."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Find Tₙ (easy)", body:"3; 8; 15; 24; …", answer:"First differences: 5; 7; 9. Second difference: 2<br>2a = 2 → a = 1<br>3a + b = 5 → 3 + b = 5 → b = 2<br>a + b + c = 3 → 1 + 2 + c = 3 → c = 0<br><strong>Tₙ = n² + 2n</strong><br>Check T₄ = 16 + 8 = 24 ✓"},
      {title:"Position and first differences (exam standard)", body:"For 4; 7; 12; 19; …: (a) find Tₙ, (b) which term is 103? (c) between which two consecutive terms is the first difference 21?", answer:"(a) Differences 3; 5; 7, second difference 2 → a = 1; 3 + b = 3 → b = 0; 1 + 0 + c = 4 → c = 3. Tₙ = n² + 3<br>(b) n² + 3 = 103 → n² = 100 → n = 10 (n > 0): <strong>the 10th term</strong><br>(c) Tₙ₊₁ − Tₙ = (n + 1)² + 3 − n² − 3 = 2n + 1<br>2n + 1 = 21 → n = 10<br><strong>Between T₁₀ and T₁₁</strong> (124 − 103 = 21 ✓)"},
      {title:"Handshakes and matches (real life)", body:"0; 1; 3; 6; 10; … are the handshakes for 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 people. Find Tₙ and the handshakes for 300 people.", answer:"Second difference 1 → a = ½<br>3(½) + b = 1 → b = −½<br>½ − ½ + c = 0 → c = 0<br>Tₙ = ½n² − ½n = n(n − 1)/2<br>World Cup group: T₄ = 4 × 3 ÷ 2 = 6 matches ✓<br>T₃₀₀ = ½(90 000) − 150 = <strong>44 850 handshakes</strong>"},
    ],
    formula:"Tₙ = an² + bn + c · 2a = second difference · 3a + b = T₂ − T₁ · a + b + c = T₁",
    mistakes:["Using the second difference as a. Fix: 2a = second difference, so halve it.","Taking the first first-difference as b. Fix: it is 3a + b.","Treating n as a term, not a position. Fix: n counts places.","Keeping a negative n. Fix: positions are natural numbers — reject it.","Not checking Tₙ. Fix: test T₃ or T₄."],
    callout:{title:"Where you’ll use this", body:"School league organisers in Soweto use n(n − 1)/2 for fixtures: 10 schools playing each other once need 45 matches, 90 home and away. Network engineers count links between computers the same way."},
    activity:"A beadwork pattern uses 5; 11; 19; 29; … beads in each row.<br>(a) Find Tₙ.<br>(b) Which row uses 131 beads?<br>(c) Between which two consecutive rows does the number of beads increase by 44?",
    activityAnswer:"(a) Differences 6; 8; 10, second difference 2 → a = 1; b = 6 − 3 = 3; c = 5 − 1 − 3 = 1. <strong>Tₙ = n² + 3n + 1</strong>.<br>(b) n² + 3n − 130 = 0 → (n + 13)(n − 10) = 0 → <strong>row 10</strong> (n = −13 rejected).<br>(c) Tₙ₊₁ − Tₙ = 2n + 4 = 44 → n = 20: <strong>rows 20 and 21</strong> (461 and 505).",
    examTip:"Write the pattern, first differences and second differences in three rows — that earns method marks. Show 2a, 3a + b and a + b + c on separate lines, and reject impossible n in words (n ∈ ℕ).",
    keyterms:[{w:"Quadratic pattern",d:"A pattern with a constant second difference"},{w:"General term",d:"A formula Tₙ for the nth term"},{w:"Second difference",d:"The difference between consecutive first differences"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"2; 6; 12; 20; … has second difference:",opts:["2","4","6","8"],a:0,et:"First differences 4, 6, 8 rise by 2. The pattern is n(n + 1): twice the handshakes for n + 1 people."},
      {q:"If the second difference is 6, then a =",opts:["6","3","12","2"],a:1,et:"2a = 6, so a = 3. Choosing 6 forgets the 2."},
      {q:"Tₙ = n² + 1. T₅ =",opts:["11","26","25","6"],a:1,et:"5² + 1 = 26. 11 comes from 2 × 5 + 1 — squaring is not doubling."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Constant second differences mean quadratic: 2a, 3a + b and a + b + c give Tₙ. First differences are linear; n is a natural number. Show the difference rows."},

  3: {emoji:"📍", subtitle:"Grade 10 · Distance, midpoint and gradient",
    illusText:"A(−2; 3) to B(4; −5): distance 10, midpoint (1; −1), gradient −4/3.",
    hook:{title:"The shortest route", body:"On a town map drawn on a grid, a spaza shop is at (−2; 3) and a clinic at (4; −5). How far apart are they in a straight line? Where should a new taxi stop go if it must be exactly halfway? How steep is the road joining them? <strong>Analytical geometry</strong> answers all three with coordinates — the same way your phone’s map app measures the distance to Maponya Mall."},
    intro:"For points A(x₁; y₁) and B(x₂; y₂): <strong>distance</strong> AB = √[(x₂ − x₁)² + (y₂ − y₁)²]; <strong>midpoint</strong> M = ((x₁ + x₂)/2; (y₁ + y₂)/2); <strong>gradient</strong> m = (y₂ − y₁)/(x₂ − x₁). <strong>Parallel</strong> lines have equal gradients; <strong>perpendicular</strong> lines have m₁ × m₂ = −1; <strong>collinear</strong> points have equal gradients between pairs. Horizontal lines have m = 0; vertical lines have an undefined gradient. With these three formulas you can find unknown points and prove what kind of quadrilateral a shape is.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"📏 Distance is Pythagoras", body:"Join A and B, then draw a horizontal and a vertical line to make a right-angled triangle. The legs are the change in x and the change in y, and AB is the hypotenuse: AB² = (x₂ − x₁)² + (y₂ − y₁)².<br><br>The order doesn’t matter, because squaring removes the sign.<br><br>Working backwards: if a distance is given with an unknown coordinate, square both sides and solve. You usually get <strong>two</strong> answers, one on each side."},
      {t:"teal", title:"⚖️ Midpoint, forwards and backwards", body:"The midpoint is the average of the x-values and the average of the y-values.<br><br>Reverse it: if M is the midpoint of PR, then R = (2x_M − x_P; 2y_M − y_P).<br><br>Fourth vertex of a parallelogram: the diagonals bisect each other, so they share a midpoint. For ABCD with A(1; 2), B(5; 3), C(6; 7): midpoint of AC = (3.5; 4.5) = midpoint of BD, so D = (7 − 5; 9 − 3) = (2; 6)."},
      {t:"gold", title:"📈 Gradient and shape proofs", body:"Gradient is rise over run. Points (1; 2), (3; 6) and (5; 10) are collinear: both gradients are 4/2 = 2.<br><br>To prove a shape: parallelogram — opposite sides parallel or diagonals share a midpoint; rectangle — a parallelogram with a right angle (m₁ × m₂ = −1); rhombus — all sides equal; square — a rhombus with a right angle.<br><br>Inclination & Equation of a Line turns gradients into angles and equations."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Distance (easy)", body:"Find the distance between A(−2; 3) and B(4; −5).", answer:"AB = √[(4 − (−2))² + (−5 − 3)²]<br>= √[6² + (−8)²]<br>= √(36 + 64) = √100 = <strong>10</strong><br>Also: midpoint (1; −1) and gradient −8/6 = −4/3."},
      {title:"Missing endpoint (exam standard)", body:"M(4; 1) is the midpoint of PR, with P(2; 5). Find R.", answer:"(2 + x)/2 = 4 → 2 + x = 8 → x = 6<br>(5 + y)/2 = 1 → 5 + y = 2 → y = −3<br><strong>R(6; −3)</strong><br>Check: ((2 + 6)/2; (5 − 3)/2) = (4; 1) ✓"},
      {title:"Unknown coordinate (challenging)", body:"The distance from (2; k) to (5; 1) is 5 units. Find k.", answer:"√[(5 − 2)² + (1 − k)²] = 5<br>9 + (1 − k)² = 25 (square both sides)<br>(1 − k)² = 16<br>1 − k = 4 or 1 − k = −4<br><strong>k = −3 or k = 5</strong><br>Both points lie 5 units away: one above, one below."},
    ],
    formula:"d = √[(x₂ − x₁)² + (y₂ − y₁)²] · M = ((x₁ + x₂)/2; (y₁ + y₂)/2) · m = (y₂ − y₁)/(x₂ − x₁)",
    mistakes:["Sign errors when subtracting negatives: 4 − (−2) is 6, not 2. Fix: put negatives in brackets.","Mixing the order: (y₂ − y₁) over (x₁ − x₂) flips the sign. Fix: start both from the same point.","Forgetting the square root in the distance formula. Fix: the last step is always √.","Taking only one root when solving for an unknown coordinate. Fix: (1 − k)² = 16 gives ±4, so two answers.","Proving a square from equal sides only (that’s a rhombus). Fix: also show a right angle with m₁ × m₂ = −1."],
    callout:{title:"Where you’ll use this", body:"Every erf in South Africa has its corner beacons recorded as coordinates on a Surveyor-General diagram. Land surveyors calculate boundary lengths with the distance formula, and town planners in places like Cosmo City use midpoints and gradients to place roads, stormwater drains and taxi stops. Your phone's map app uses the same idea, adjusted for the curved Earth."},
    activity:"P(1; 1), Q(4; 5), R(8; 2) and S(5; −2).<br>(a) Show that all four sides are equal.<br>(b) Show that PQ ⊥ QR.<br>(c) Show that the diagonals bisect each other and are equal. What is PQRS?",
    activityAnswer:"(a) PQ = √(3² + 4²) = 5; QR = √(4² + (−3)²) = 5; RS = √((−3)² + (−4)²) = 5; SP = √((−4)² + 3²) = 5. All <strong>5</strong>.<br>(b) m_PQ = 4/3, m_QR = −3/4; (4/3)(−3/4) = −1, so <strong>PQ ⊥ QR</strong>.<br>(c) Midpoint PR = (4.5; 1.5) = midpoint QS. PR = √(49 + 1) = √50 = QS. Equal sides and a right angle: <strong>PQRS is a square</strong>.",
    examTip:"Write the formula, substitute with brackets around negatives, then simplify — the substitution line earns a mark on its own. In “prove” questions, end with a sentence that names the property: “PQ = QR and PQ ⊥ QR, so PQRS is a square”.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Gradient",d:"The steepness of a line: change in y over change in x"},{w:"Midpoint",d:"The point exactly halfway between two points"},{w:"Collinear",d:"Lying on the same straight line"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"Midpoint of (2; 6) and (8; 0):",opts:["(5; 3)","(10; 6)","(3; 5)","(6; 6)"],a:0,et:"Average each coordinate: (10/2; 6/2). (10; 6) forgets to divide by 2."},
      {q:"Gradient through (0; 0) and (2; 6):",opts:["2","3","⅓","6"],a:1,et:"Rise over run: 6 ÷ 2 = 3. ⅓ is run over rise, upside down."},
      {q:"Perpendicular gradients multiply to:",opts:["0","1","−1","Undefined"],a:2,et:"m₁ × m₂ = −1, e.g. 2 and −½. Product 1 is not a rule; equal gradients mean parallel."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Distance (Pythagoras), midpoint (averages) and gradient (rise over run) prove almost anything about straight-line shapes. Work backwards for missing points, and expect two answers from a given distance."},

  4: {emoji:"↗️", subtitle:"Grade 11 · Inclination and the equation of a line",
    illusText:"tan θ = m. A gradient of −2 means an inclination of about 116.6°.",
    hook:{title:"How steep is that road?", body:"Signs on mountain passes in Mpumalanga warn truck drivers of steep descents, often as a percentage: a 10% gradient drops 10 m for every 100 m across. Building rules say a wheelchair ramp may be no steeper than 1 in 12. Engineers turn these gradients into angles. In Grade 11, the <strong>angle of inclination</strong> links a gradient to an angle, and you write the <strong>equation of a line</strong> from two facts."},
    intro:"The <strong>angle of inclination θ</strong> is the angle a line makes with the positive x-axis, measured anticlockwise (0° ≤ θ < 180°): <strong>tan θ = m</strong>. A positive gradient gives an acute angle; a negative gradient gives an obtuse angle (θ = 180° + the calculator angle). The <strong>equation of a line</strong>: y = mx + c, or through (x₁; y₁) with gradient m: <strong>y − y₁ = m(x − x₁)</strong>. Angles inside triangles come from the inclinations.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"📐 tan θ = m", body:"Gradient is rise over run, and in the right-angled triangle under the line, rise ÷ run is the tangent of θ. So tan θ = m.<br><br>Negative gradient: the calculator gives a negative angle, e.g. tan⁻¹(−2) ≈ −63.4°. A line can’t lean backwards below the axis, so add 180°: θ ≈ 116.6°.<br><br>Special angles: m = 1 → 45°; m = √3 → 60°; m = 1/√3 → 30°; m = −1 → 135°. A 10% road: tan θ = 0.1 → θ ≈ 5.7°."},
      {t:"teal", title:"✏️ Point–gradient form", body:"y − y₁ = m(x − x₁) is the gradient formula with (x; y) as any point on the line. You need only one point and m.<br><br>Recipe: find m (from two points, from a parallel line, or as −1/m for a perpendicular line), substitute one point, then simplify to y = mx + c.<br><br>Trap: with x₁ = −1, the bracket becomes (x + 1). Write the substitution in full before simplifying."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🔀 Angles between lines", body:"Where two lines cross the x-axis, they form a triangle with it. The exterior angle of a triangle equals the sum of the two opposite interior angles, so the angle between the lines is the <strong>bigger inclination minus the smaller</strong>.<br><br>This is how CAPS asks for ∠BAC in a triangle on the Cartesian plane: find both inclinations, then subtract, giving the reason (ext ∠ of △).<br><br>Check whether the angle you need is that difference or 180° minus it — a sketch tells you."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Through two points (easy)", body:"Find the equation of the line through (−1; 4) and (3; −4), and its inclination.", answer:"m = (−4 − 4)/(3 − (−1)) = −8/4 = −2<br>y − 4 = −2(x + 1) → y = −2x − 2 + 4<br><strong>y = −2x + 2</strong><br>tan θ = −2 → calculator −63.4° → θ = 180° − 63.4° = <strong>116.6°</strong>"},
      {title:"Perpendicular line (exam standard)", body:"Find the line through (4; −1) perpendicular to y = ½x + 3, and its inclination.", answer:"Given gradient ½, so perpendicular m = −2 (½ × −2 = −1)<br>y − (−1) = −2(x − 4) → y + 1 = −2x + 8<br><strong>y = −2x + 7</strong><br>Same gradient as y = −2x + 2, so the lines are parallel and θ = <strong>116.6°</strong> again."},
      {title:"CAPS angle in a triangle (challenging)", body:"A(2; 5), B(−3; −4) and C(4; −2). Find ∠BAC.", answer:"m_AB = (−4 − 5)/(−3 − 2) = 9/5 → θ_AB = tan⁻¹(1.8) ≈ 60.95°<br>m_AC = (−2 − 5)/(4 − 2) = −7/2 → tan⁻¹(−3.5) ≈ −74.05°, so θ_AC ≈ 105.95°<br>AB and AC cut the x-axis at D and E; in △ADE, θ_AC is the exterior angle at E:<br>∠BAC = 105.95° − 60.95° = <strong>45°</strong> (ext ∠ of △)"},
    ],
    formula:"tan θ = m · y − y₁ = m(x − x₁)",
    mistakes:["Giving a negative inclination. Fix: add 180°.","Using point–gradient form before finding m. Fix: m first.","Sign slips when x₁ is negative. Fix: write x − (−1) = x + 1.","Using m, not −1/m, for a perpendicular line. Fix: flip and change the sign.","Adding inclinations for the angle between lines. Fix: subtract (ext ∠ of △)."],
    callout:{title:"Where you’ll use this", body:"SANS 10400 limits wheelchair ramps to 1 in 12: tan θ = 1/12, θ ≈ 4.8°, so architects designing a clinic in Polokwane check every ramp this way. SANRAL engineers design road gradients, roofers set roof pitch, and railways keep tracks almost flat."},
    activity:"P(0; 1), Q(3; 4) and R(5; 0).<br>(a) Find the equation of QR.<br>(b) Find the inclinations of PQ and QR.<br>(c) Find ∠PQR.",
    activityAnswer:"(a) m_QR = (0 − 4)/(5 − 3) = −2. y − 4 = −2(x − 3) → <strong>y = −2x + 10</strong>.<br>(b) m_PQ = (4 − 1)/(3 − 0) = 1 → θ = <strong>45°</strong>. m_QR = −2 → θ = 180° − 63.43° = <strong>116.57°</strong>.<br>(c) ∠PQR = 116.57° − 45° = <strong>71.57°</strong> (ext ∠ of △).",
    examTip:"State tan θ = m, show the calculator angle and the 180° adjustment — each line earns a mark. For angles in a triangle, name both inclinations, subtract, and give the reason (ext ∠ of △). Leave the line in the form the question asks for, usually y = mx + c.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Angle of inclination",d:"Angle between a line and the positive x-axis"},{w:"Point–gradient form",d:"y − y₁ = m(x − x₁)"},{w:"y-intercept",d:"Where a line crosses the y-axis (c)"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"A line with inclination 45° has gradient:",opts:["0","1","−1","√3"],a:1,et:"tan 45° = 1: it rises 1 for every 1 across. √3 belongs to 60°."},
      {q:"Gradient −1 gives inclination:",opts:["45°","−45°","135°","90°"],a:2,et:"Calculator gives −45°; add 180° to get 135°. Inclination is never negative."},
      {q:"Line through (0; 3) with m = 2:",opts:["y = 3x + 2","y = 2x + 3","y = 2x − 3","y = x + 3"],a:1,et:"(0; 3) is the y-intercept, so c = 3 and y = 2x + 3. Swapping m and c is the common slip."},
    ],
    conclusion:"tan θ = m turns gradients into angles (add 180° when m is negative); point–gradient form turns two facts into an equation. For angles between lines, subtract inclinations and give the reason."},

  5: {emoji:"🎲", subtitle:"Grade 10–11 · Venn diagrams and probability rules",
    illusText:"P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B).",
    hook:{title:"Soccer, netball — or both?", body:"In a Grade 10 class in Umlazi, 22 of 40 learners play soccer, 15 play netball and 6 play both. What’s the chance a random learner plays at least one? 22 + 15 = 37 counts the 6 twice. A <strong>Venn diagram</strong> fixes that."},
    intro:"<strong>Probability</strong> P(E) = favourable outcomes ÷ total outcomes, with 0 ≤ P ≤ 1. A <strong>Venn diagram</strong> shows events as circles inside a rectangle (the sample space S). <strong>Addition rule</strong>: P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B). <strong>Mutually exclusive</strong>: P(A and B) = 0. <strong>Complementary</strong>: P(not A) = 1 − P(A). Grade 11 adds <strong>independent</strong> events, P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B), and Venn diagrams with <strong>three</strong> events.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🎯 Relative frequency vs theory", body:"<strong>Theoretical</strong> probability comes from reasoning: a fair coin gives P(heads) = ½. <strong>Relative frequency</strong> comes from an experiment: toss it 50 times, get 28 heads, and the relative frequency is 28/50 = 0.56.<br><br>The more trials, the closer the relative frequency tends to the theoretical value. Insurers use relative frequency from past claims to price car insurance in Gauteng."},
      {t:"teal", title:"⭕ Filling a Venn diagram", body:"Work from the middle out: fill the overlap first, then the “only” regions, then “neither” outside the circles. All regions must add to the total.<br><br>With three events, start with the region in all three, then the three “two only” regions, then the “one only” regions. If a value is missing, call it x and use the total to solve.<br><br>Trap: “A” means the whole circle; “A only” excludes the overlap."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🔗 Exclusive, independent or neither?", body:"Find P(A and B), often from the addition rule.<br><br>If P(A and B) = 0, the events are <strong>mutually exclusive</strong>: they can’t happen together. If P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B), they are <strong>independent</strong>: one doesn’t change the chance of the other.<br><br>They are different: events with non-zero probabilities can’t be both."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Fill the Venn (easy)", body:"40 learners: 22 soccer, 15 netball, 6 both. Find each region and P(soccer or netball).", answer:"Both: 6<br>Soccer only: 22 − 6 = 16<br>Netball only: 15 − 6 = 9<br>Neither: 40 − (16 + 6 + 9) = <strong>9</strong><br>Check: 16 + 6 + 9 + 9 = 40 ✓<br>P(S or N) = (22 + 15 − 6)/40 = <strong>31/40 = 0.775</strong>"},
      {title:"Which kind of events? (exam standard)", body:"P(A) = 0.45, P(B) = 0.3 and P(A or B) = 0.615. Are A and B mutually exclusive, independent or neither?", answer:"P(A and B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A or B)<br>= 0.45 + 0.3 − 0.615 = 0.135<br>0.135 ≠ 0, so not mutually exclusive<br>P(A) × P(B) = 0.45 × 0.3 = 0.135 = P(A and B)<br><strong>Independent, not mutually exclusive</strong>"},
      {title:"Three events (challenging)", body:"80 patients tried drugs A, B and C: A 40, B 35, C 40, A and C 21, B and C 18, all three 7, at least one 68. Find how many took none, A and B, and A and B but not C.", answer:"None: 80 − 68 = <strong>12</strong><br>A∩C only: 21 − 7 = 14; B∩C only: 18 − 7 = 11<br>Let A∩B only = x. A only = 40 − 7 − 14 − x = 19 − x; B only = 35 − 7 − 11 − x = 17 − x; C only = 40 − 7 − 14 − 11 = 8<br>(19 − x) + (17 − x) + 8 + x + 14 + 11 + 7 = 68 → 76 − x = 68 → x = 8<br>A and B but not C: <strong>8</strong>; A and B: 8 + 7 = <strong>15</strong>"},
    ],
    formula:"P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B) · P(not A) = 1 − P(A) · independent: P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B)",
    mistakes:["Adding P(A) and P(B) without subtracting the overlap. Fix: subtract P(A and B).","Confusing mutually exclusive with independent. Fix: test P(A and B) = 0, or = P(A) × P(B).","Writing 22 in the soccer-only region. Fix: subtract the overlap — 16.","Leaving out “neither”. Fix: regions must add to the total.","Calling a relative frequency “wrong”. Fix: small samples vary; more trials bring it closer."],
    callout:{title:"Where you’ll use this", body:"Researchers at the South African Medical Research Council test whether risk factors are independent, and actuaries use the addition rule and independence to price funeral policies and car insurance. If you enjoy probability, actuarial science is one of South Africa’s best-paid careers."},
    activity:"Survey 30 classmates: WhatsApp, TikTok, both or neither? Sample results: 24 use WhatsApp, 15 use TikTok, 12 use both.<br>(a) Fill in all four Venn regions.<br>(b) Find P(WhatsApp or TikTok).<br>(c) Are the events independent, mutually exclusive or neither?",
    activityAnswer:"(a) Both 12; WhatsApp only 24 − 12 = 12; TikTok only 15 − 12 = 3; neither 30 − 27 = <strong>3</strong>.<br>(b) P(W or T) = (24 + 15 − 12)/30 = 27/30 = <strong>0.9</strong>.<br>(c) P(W) × P(T) = 0.8 × 0.5 = 0.4 and P(both) = 12/30 = 0.4, so <strong>independent</strong>; not mutually exclusive because P(both) ≠ 0.",
    examTip:"Fill the intersection first, work outwards, and show the totals check. For “independent?” write the test with numbers: P(A) × P(B) = … = P(A and B). A conclusion without numbers earns nothing.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Mutually exclusive",d:"Events that cannot happen at the same time"},{w:"Complement",d:"All outcomes not in the event"},{w:"Independent events",d:"Events where one doesn't affect the other's probability"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"P(A) = 0.3, P(B) = 0.5, mutually exclusive. P(A or B) =",opts:["0.15","0.2","0.65","0.8"],a:3,et:"No overlap to subtract: 0.3 + 0.5 = 0.8. 0.15 is P(A) × P(B), the independence product."},
      {q:"P(rain) = 0.35. P(no rain) =",opts:["0.65","0.35","1.35","0"],a:0,et:"Complement: 1 − 0.35 = 0.65. Weather services estimate these chances from relative frequencies in past data."},
      {q:"P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B) shows events are:",opts:["Mutually exclusive","Complementary","Independent","Impossible"],a:2,et:"That product test defines independence. Mutually exclusive events have P(A and B) = 0 instead."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Fill Venn diagrams from the middle out, subtract the overlap, and test P(A and B) = 0 (exclusive) or P(A) × P(B) (independent). In exams, show the numbers behind every conclusion."},

  6: {emoji:"📉", subtitle:"Grade 11 · Shifting graphs and average gradient",
    illusText:"y = a(x + p)² + q: turning point (−p; q). The graph moves; its shape stays.",
    hook:{title:"Moving the arch", body:"A designer’s parabolic arch for a Durban mall entrance must move 1 m right and 8 m down. Instead of starting again, she <strong>shifts the graph</strong>: change p and q and the arch moves. Grade 11 functions are about these shifts — and the <strong>average gradient</strong> between two points."},
    intro:"Grade 11 forms: <strong>parabola</strong> y = a(x + p)² + q, turning point (−p; q); <strong>hyperbola</strong> y = a/(x + p) + q, asymptotes x = −p and y = q; <strong>exponential</strong> y = a·b^(x + p) + q, asymptote y = q. <strong>x + p moves the graph p units LEFT</strong>; <strong>+ q moves it up</strong>; a sets the shape. <strong>Average gradient</strong> from x = a to x = b: [f(b) − f(a)] ÷ (b − a).",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"↔️ Why x + p moves left", body:"In y = (x + 3)², the value inside the bracket is 0 when x = −3, so the turning point moves to x = −3: three units left.<br><br>q is simpler: + q adds to every y-value, so the graph moves up.<br><br>From standard form, complete the square (see Quadratic Formula & Roots): 2x² − 4x − 6 = 2(x² − 2x) − 6 = 2(x − 1)² − 8. Turning point (1; −8)."},
      {t:"teal", title:"📍 Hyperbola and exponential features", body:"Hyperbola y = a/(x + p) + q: the asymptotes cross at (−p; q). Its two <strong>axes of symmetry</strong> are lines of gradient 1 and −1 through that point: y = (x + p) + q and y = −(x + p) + q.<br><br>Exponential y = a·b^(x + p) + q: p slides it sideways, q moves the asymptote to y = q.<br><br>Equation from a sketch: substitute the turning point or asymptotes, then one more point to find a."},
      {t:"gold", title:"📏 Average gradient", body:"The average gradient is the gradient of the straight line (secant) joining two points on a curve: change in y ÷ change in x.<br><br>For f(x) = x² from x = 1 to x = 3: (9 − 1)/(3 − 1) = 4.<br><br>On a distance–time graph it is average speed. In Limits & Rate of Change the two points move together and it becomes the gradient at a point."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Parabola (easy)", body:"y = 2(x − 1)² − 8. Find the turning point and intercepts.", answer:"Turning point: <strong>(1; −8)</strong>, axis x = 1<br>y-intercept: 2(0 − 1)² − 8 = 2 − 8 = <strong>−6</strong><br>x-intercepts: 2(x − 1)² = 8 → (x − 1)² = 4 → x − 1 = ±2<br><strong>x = 3 or x = −1</strong>"},
      {title:"Hyperbola (exam standard)", body:"y = 3/(x − 2) + 1. Find the asymptotes, the axes of symmetry and the intercepts.", answer:"Asymptotes: <strong>x = 2 and y = 1</strong><br>Axes of symmetry through (2; 1): y = (x − 2) + 1 → <strong>y = x − 1</strong>; y = −(x − 2) + 1 → <strong>y = −x + 3</strong><br>y-intercept: 3/(−2) + 1 = <strong>−½</strong><br>x-intercept: 3/(x − 2) = −1 → x − 2 = −3 → <strong>x = −1</strong>"},
      {title:"Exponential (challenging)", body:"y = 2^(x + 1) − 4. Find the asymptote, intercepts and the average gradient from x = 0 to x = 1.", answer:"Asymptote: <strong>y = −4</strong><br>y-intercept: 2¹ − 4 = −2 → <strong>(0; −2)</strong><br>x-intercept: 2^(x + 1) = 4 = 2² → x + 1 = 2 → <strong>(1; 0)</strong><br>Average gradient = (0 − (−2))/(1 − 0) = <strong>2</strong>"},
    ],
    formula:"y = a(x + p)² + q → TP (−p; q) · y = a/(x + p) + q → asymptotes x = −p, y = q · average gradient = [f(b) − f(a)] ÷ (b − a)",
    mistakes:["Reading the turning point of (x − 1)² as x = −1. Fix: set the bracket to 0.","Forgetting asymptotes. Fix: draw them dotted first.","Axes of symmetry y = x and y = −x through the origin. Fix: they pass through (−p; q).","Confusing average gradient with gradient at a point. Fix: average uses two x-values.","Run over rise. Fix: change in y goes on top."],
    callout:{title:"Where you’ll use this", body:"Hire a taxi for R600 and share it among x friends: each pays 600/x, a hyperbola. Add a R20 toll each: y = 600/x + 20, and the asymptote y = 20 shows nobody pays less than R20. Reserve Bank economists read average gradients off inflation graphs."},
    activity:"(a) Sketch y = −(x + 2)² + 4: turning point and intercepts.<br>(b) Sketch y = 2/(x − 1) − 3: asymptotes and intercepts.<br>(c) Give the hyperbola’s axes of symmetry and the parabola’s average gradient from x = −2 to x = 0.",
    activityAnswer:"(a) TP <strong>(−2; 4)</strong>, opens down. y-intercept −4 + 4 = 0. x-intercepts: (x + 2)² = 4 → <strong>x = 0 or x = −4</strong>.<br>(b) Asymptotes <strong>x = 1, y = −3</strong>. y-intercept 2/(−1) − 3 = <strong>−5</strong>. x-intercept: 2/(x − 1) = 3 → x = <strong>5/3</strong>.<br>(c) <strong>y = x − 4</strong> and <strong>y = −x − 2</strong>. Average gradient = (0 − 4)/(0 + 2) = <strong>−2</strong>.",
    examTip:"List the features (shape, turning point or asymptotes, intercepts) before drawing — each carries a mark, as does labelling them. For average gradient, write [f(b) − f(a)] ÷ (b − a) with values.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Turning point",d:"The maximum or minimum point of a parabola"},{w:"Asymptote",d:"A line the graph approaches but never touches"},{w:"Average gradient",d:"Gradient of the line joining two points on a curve"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"The turning point of y = (x + 3)² − 5 is:",opts:["(3; −5)","(3; 5)","(−3; 5)","(−3; −5)"],a:3,et:"x + 3 = 0 gives x = −3, and q = −5. (3; −5) is the classic sign slip."},
      {q:"y = 4/(x + 1) − 2 has a vertical asymptote at:",opts:["x = −1","x = 1","y = −2","x = 4"],a:0,et:"Division by zero when x + 1 = 0, so x = −1. y = −2 is the horizontal asymptote."},
      {q:"Average gradient of f(x) = 2x² from x = 0 to x = 2:",opts:["2","8","4","16"],a:2,et:"(f(2) − f(0)) ÷ (2 − 0) = (8 − 0) ÷ 2 = 4. 8 forgets to divide by the run."},
    ],
    conclusion:"p moves the graph sideways (x + p → left), q up or down, a sets the shape. Average gradient is rise over run between two points. List every feature before sketching."},

  7: {emoji:"➕", subtitle:"Grade 12 · Series and sigma notation",
    illusText:"Σ (2k + 1) for k = 1 to 5 = 3 + 5 + 7 + 9 + 11 = 35.",
    hook:{title:"The bouncing ball and the stokvel", body:"A ball dropped from 8 m bounces back half as high each time. How far does it travel? A Tembisa stokvel member adds R50 more each month — what has she paid after a year? Both are <strong>series</strong>. The ball bounces forever yet travels exactly 24 m (Example 3)."},
    intro:"A <strong>series</strong> is the sum of the terms of a sequence; <strong>sigma notation</strong> Σ is shorthand for that sum. <strong>Arithmetic series</strong>: Sₙ = n/2[2a + (n − 1)d]. <strong>Geometric series</strong>: Sₙ = a(rⁿ − 1)/(r − 1), r ≠ 1. A geometric series <strong>converges</strong> when −1 < r < 1, with <strong>sum to infinity</strong> S∞ = a/(1 − r). CAPS expects you to derive both Sₙ formulas and to find n from a sum.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"Σ Reading sigma", body:"Below the Σ is the starting value of k, above it the last value. Substitute k = 1, 2, 3, … into the formula and add.<br><br>Number of terms from k = p to k = q is <strong>q − p + 1</strong>: k = 3 to 10 has 8 terms, not 7.<br><br>Mini-step: 2 + 6 + 18 + … to 6 terms, a = 2, r = 3: S₆ = 2(3⁶ − 1)/(3 − 1) = 728."},
      {t:"teal", title:"➕ Deriving the formulas (examinable)", body:"<strong>Arithmetic:</strong> write Sₙ = a + (a + d) + … + [a + (n − 1)d], then the same sum backwards. Add: each of the n pairs is 2a + (n − 1)d, so 2Sₙ = n[2a + (n − 1)d].<br><br><strong>Geometric:</strong> Sₙ = a + ar + … + arⁿ⁻¹. Multiply by r: rSₙ = ar + … + arⁿ. Subtract: Sₙ − rSₙ = a − arⁿ, so Sₙ = a(1 − rⁿ)/(1 − r).<br><br>Learn these steps; they are often a 4-mark question."},
      {t:"gold", title:"♾️ Sum to infinity", body:"If −1 < r < 1, rⁿ → 0 as n grows, so Sₙ = a(1 − rⁿ)/(1 − r) settles at S∞ = a/(1 − r). For 8 + 4 + 2 + …: 8 ÷ (1 − ½) = 16.<br><br>If |r| ≥ 1 the terms don’t shrink and the series diverges.<br><br>With x: 1 + (x − 2) + (x − 2)² + … converges when −1 < x − 2 < 1, so 1 < x < 3. And 0.999… = 0.9 + 0.09 + … = 0.9 ÷ (1 − 0.1) = 1 exactly."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Sigma (easy)", body:"Find Σ (2k + 1), k = 1 to 5.", answer:"k = 1 to 5: 3 + 5 + 7 + 9 + 11<br>Arithmetic: a = 3, d = 2, n = 5<br>S₅ = 5/2[2(3) + 4(2)] = 5/2 × 14 = <strong>35</strong>"},
      {title:"Largest n (exam standard)", body:"Find the largest n for which Σ 2ᵏ (k = 1 to n) < 1 000.", answer:"Geometric: a = 2, r = 2<br>Sₙ = 2(2ⁿ − 1)/(2 − 1) = 2ⁿ⁺¹ − 2<br>2ⁿ⁺¹ − 2 < 1 000 → 2ⁿ⁺¹ < 1 002<br>2⁹ = 512 and 2¹⁰ = 1 024, so n + 1 ≤ 9<br><strong>n = 8</strong> (S₈ = 510; S₉ = 1 022 is too big)"},
      {title:"The bouncing ball (real life)", body:"A ball is dropped from 8 m and each bounce reaches half the previous height. Find the total distance travelled.", answer:"Down: 8 + 4 + 2 + … = 8 ÷ (1 − ½) = 16 m<br>Up: 4 + 2 + 1 + … = 4 ÷ (1 − ½) = 8 m<br>Total = 16 + 8 = <strong>24 m</strong><br>(Or 8 + 2 × 4/(1 − ½) = 24: every height after the drop is travelled twice.)"},
    ],
    formula:"Arithmetic: Sₙ = n/2[2a + (n − 1)d] · Geometric: Sₙ = a(rⁿ − 1)/(r − 1) · S∞ = a/(1 − r), −1 < r < 1",
    mistakes:["Using S∞ when |r| ≥ 1. Fix: state −1 < r < 1 first.","Miscounting terms: k = 3 to 10 has 8. Fix: q − p + 1.","Mixing up Tₙ (one term) and Sₙ (the total). Fix: read “term” or “sum”.","Rounding n up in “largest n”. Fix: round down and test the next n.","Counting a ball’s first drop twice. Fix: only later heights are travelled up and down."],
    callout:{title:"Where you’ll use this", body:"Clinic medication works like a geometric series. Take 100 mg every morning; if half leaves the body by the next day, the level after each dose is 100, 150, 175, … and settles at S∞ = 200 mg. Pharmacists use this to set doses, and Annuities & Loans is built on geometric series too."},
    activity:"A stokvel member pays R200 in month 1 and R50 more each month.<br>(a) How much has she paid after 12 months?<br>(b) In which month does she first pay at least R700?<br>(c) After how many months do her total payments first exceed R10 000?",
    activityAnswer:"(a) a = 200, d = 50, n = 12: S₁₂ = 6[400 + 550] = 6 × 950 = <strong>R5 700</strong>.<br>(b) 200 + (n − 1)50 ≥ 700 → n − 1 ≥ 10 → <strong>month 11</strong> (R700).<br>(c) Sₙ = n/2[400 + 50(n − 1)] = 25n² + 175n > 10 000 → n² + 7n − 400 > 0 → n > 16.8, so <strong>17 months</strong>. Check: S₁₆ = R9 200, S₁₇ = R10 200.",
    examTip:"Identify the series type and write a, d or r before choosing a formula. In “largest n” questions, show the inequality, solve it, then test the boundary values. Derivation questions are marked line by line.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Series",d:"The sum of the terms of a sequence"},{w:"Sigma notation",d:"Σ, a short way to write a sum"},{w:"Convergent",d:"A series whose sum approaches a finite value"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"Σ 3(½)ᵏ⁻¹ from k = 1 to ∞ equals:",opts:["3","4.5","6","Diverges"],a:2,et:"a = 3, r = ½, so S∞ = 3 ÷ (1 − ½) = 6. 3 is only the first term; 4.5 is the first two. It converges because |r| < 1."},
      {q:"How many terms in Σ from k = 4 to 15?",opts:["12","11","15","19"],a:0,et:"15 − 4 + 1 = 12. Subtracting gives 11 — the fencepost error that loses marks every year."},
      {q:"A geometric series converges when:",opts:["r > 1","r = 1","r < −1","−1 < r < 1"],a:3,et:"Only then do the terms shrink towards 0. That is why 0.999… (r = 0.1) adds up to exactly 1."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Series add sequences: identify arithmetic or geometric, use the matching Sₙ (and know its derivation), and use S∞ only when −1 < r < 1. In exams, test boundary values for “largest n”."},
};

